\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Americans Ramp Up Their Opposition To Vaccine Mandates\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson, Carter Evans, Gayle King \par \uc2 716 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And, Tony, this comes as the vocal minority of Americans ramp up their opposition to vaccine mandates. \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  employees staged a protest outside the company`s headquarters in Dallas. While in California, some parents are protesting vaccine mandates for kids. Carter Evans is in Los Angeles. Good morning, Carter.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS (CBS News Correspondent): Good morning. Well, California already requires students to get at least ten vaccinations including measles, mumps and rubella. But when it comes to the COVID vaccine, well, some parents say the state just went too far.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LAURA CUNNINGHAM (Parent): For the government to push something so hard on us is ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Thousands of parents, teachers and students across California came out to protest Monday, saying kids shouldn`t be kicked out of the classroom just because they are unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA JOLLY (High School Student): I am vaccinated, but I do think that people should have a choice on whether they get vaccinated or not because the vaccine, like, hasn`t been out for that long.\par \par \uc2 ALYANA NORD (Mother): I don`t want my kids to be test tube-- experimental test tubes.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Can you explain how this is not an experiment on children?\par \par \uc2 DR. PETER HOTEZ (Texas Children`s Hospital Center for Vaccine Development Director): These vaccines have gone through extraordinary levels of-- of testing. Research and development for over a decade went into this current generation of COVID vaccines.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Doctor \uc2 Peter Hotez\uc2  is with the Texas Children`s Hospital Center for Vaccine Development.\par \par \uc2 DR. PETER HOTEZ: These are as well-vetted and tested as just about any vaccines we-- we`ve ever used.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Students at a high school in Bend, Oregon, walked out of school in support of staff refusing the vaccine. The protests come as vaccination deadlines are closing in around the country. Nineteen states and the District of Columbia have vaccine mandates for at least some state workers, but only Washington, Oregon and Colorado will not allow workers to opt out of the vaccine with a weekly test.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s a big day, my friend. Big day.\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Monday was the cutoff in Washington State for public employees, health care workers and school employees to be fully vaccinated. Washington State University head football coach Nick Rolovich was fired along with four of the team`s assistant coaches because they refused to get the shot.\par \par \uc2 PAT CHUN (Washington State University Athletics Director): Nobody wanted to be at this moment. We didn`t hire Nick to be here for a mid-season change. Unfortunately, we stand here today having to make a transition.\par \par \uc2 JENNY A. DURKAN\uc2  (Seattle Mayor): If people make the choice that they don`t want to keep their jobs because they don`t want to be vaccinated, they will have that choice.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CARTER EVANS: Well, more than a third of Chicago`s police officers still have not reported their vaccination status despite last week`s deadline. And in Seattle, the police department says there are contingency plans now in place in case of staffing shortages. Here in California, students who do not comply or have an exemption could do an independent study at home. Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope it remains a controversial topic. Thank you, Carter.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump is suing to block the release of documents to a special committee investigating the January 6th assault on the \uc2 Capitol\uc2 . Mister Trump`s lawsuit calls the House committee`s request an illegal fishing expedition. President Biden has rejected his claim that the documents are protected by executive privilege. The former President returned to New York City headquarters yesterday to answer questions in another lawsuit. Immigration activists claim that Mister Trump`s security staff assaulted them back in 2015.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211025ehaj0007b\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for October 17, 2021, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Manuel Bojorquez, Lilia Luciano, David Agus, Debra  Alfarone, Imtiaz Tyab,  Chris Tye, Weijia Jiang, Ian Lee \par \uc2 3,655 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Tonight, kidnapped in Haiti. A group of American missionaries and children seized by a gang. The latest on efforts to find and free them.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, Strike-tober. From hospitals to the factory floor, workers walk off jobs nationwide over staff shortages and more pay, while Hollywood workers strike a deal.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: I`m Lilia Luciano in Los Angeles, where Hollywood crews won`t be going on strike tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Plus, COVID boosters. Who needs them? And what about mixing and matching vaccines? We`ll get a checkup from Doctor David Agus.\par \par \uc2 And we visit the National Zoo, where orangutans are the latest creatures getting their shots.\par \par \uc2 Presidential push. A deadline approaches to save Joe Biden`s agenda.\par \par \uc2 Mistaken strike. The U.S. will pay these Afghans for killing their relatives in a drone strike. CBS News is there and speaking to them.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: I imagine there`s no compensation that can make up for all that you`ve lost.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: And later, the rare rock mined in Greenland that could help save the planet.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Good evening. I`m Nikki Battiste. Jericka Duncan is off.\par \par \uc2 It is one of the world`s most dangerous countries besieged by poverty, political instability and violence. And tonight, the fate of more than a dozen American missionaries and family members reportedly kidnapped in the country is unknown. Police say they have been seized by a notorious criminal gang. It`s the latest in a series of kidnappings that happened just outside the capital Port-au- Prince. CBS` Manuel Bojorquez is there with the latest.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (CBS News National Correspondent): By some estimates, gangs control half of Haiti`s capital Port-au-Prince, and kidnappings are on the rise. But the sheer number of victims in this case and the fact that they are foreigners is brazen even by the gang`s standard.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The kidnappings reportedly happened as a group of missionaries was leaving an orphanage just outside of Port-au-Prince on Saturday. An armed gang abducted sixteen Americans, including five children, as well as one Canadian. The missionaries are affiliated with Ohio`s \uc2 Christian Aid\uc2  Ministries. The group`s website says its "`Haiti`-Sponsor-A-Child School Program`. pays for school tuition, uniforms, text books and meals for Haitians in fifty-two schools." In a statement about the kidnappings, \uc2 Christian Aid\uc2  Ministries said, "Join us in praying for those who are being held hostage, the kidnappers, and the families, friends, and churches of those affected." A surge in gang violence, including kidnappings of Haitians, continues to roil this country, already in the midst of a political crisis after a presidential assassination in July and an earthquake that killed more than two thousand in August.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The region where the kidnapping of the missionaries happened is controlled by one gang that is known to be among the most dangerous in Haiti, blamed earlier this year with the kidnapping of five priests and two nuns. Nikki.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: CBS` Manuel Bojorquez in Port-au-Prince, thank you.\par \par \uc2 American workers are testing their power. Thousands have hit picket lines in several states amid a nationwide labor shortage, seeking better pay, benefits, and working conditions. There`s even a name for it- -Strike-tober. CBS` Lilia Luciano is in Los Angeles with more. Lilia.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (CBS News Correspondent): Nikki, here in L.A., a strike by Hollywood crews has been averted for now. But nationwide, many workers are walking off the job and right onto the picket line.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Pandemic pressure is spurring strikes, including this one in a suburb of Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 MARY TURNER (ICU Nurse): You don`t mess with the nurses.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: A walkout by nurses prompted a hospital in Plymouth to close its emergency room.\par \par \uc2 MARY TURNER: This is like my fifth picket during this pandemic. I find that deplorable.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Staffing shortages and the battle over benefits are impacting almost every industry. Ten thousand John Deere workers are on strike. The Illinois-based company is expected to post record profits this year.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Action.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And a sixty-thousand Hollywood production workers strike was tentatively averted this weekend at the eleventh hour, though union members must still vote on the three-year deal with the studios.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA HAVERLAND (Prop Master): Union members are steaming mad.\par \par \uc2 Another three hundred wine bottles.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Alicia Haverland is a prop master in the IATSE union.\par \par \uc2 What were some of people`s complaints?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA HAVERLAND: I think it`s just the constant, endless feeling that, A, you`re going to lose your job; and, B, you`re never going to sleep. I was never hoping for a strike. I was hoping for a real, reasonable contract.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: They called it a Hollywood ending. Is it?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA HAVERLAND: I make Michael Bay movies. Things blow up. That`s kind of a Hollywood ending. I guess it depends on the movie.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Alicia says she wouldn`t call it an ending quite yet. And while crews will be back at work in sets like this one tomorrow, another massive walkout is expected soon by twenty-four thousand Kaiser employee-- workers who voted to strike. Nikki.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Lilia Luciano, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Today, Doctor \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  said Americans shouldn`t hesitate to spend holidays with their families if they are fully vaccinated. For the latest on COVID and the vaccines, CBS News medical contributor Doctor David Agus joins us from Los Angeles. Doctor Agus, good to see you. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  advisory committee recommended Friday that all J&J recipients get a second dose. Should that second dose be J&J or another vaccine?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS (CBS News Medical Contributor): Well, Nikki, fifteen million in the-- people in the country got that J&J vaccine and are basically waiting to see what to do. The recommendations are going to be two months after that shot, at least. You should get a second shot, either J& A-- J or the RNA vaccines. You know, the J&J probably was meant to be a two-shot vaccine, and so getting a second one will raise immunity. Getting an RNA vaccine, as a second one, will raise it even more.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: On the topic of mixing and matching, which we`re all curious about, which provides better protection--mix and matching vaccines or the same vaccine for all doses?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: If we take antibody level as a surrogate for how much immunity you get, probably mixing gives you slightly better protection, with slightly more side effects. So I do think getting the same one is okay, and you will get enough protection to get you through Delta. But if you want a little bit more protection, mixing may have some benefit.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: We see Minnesota hitting another critical COVID point with hospitalizations peaking again. Is our country going to be in a better or worse position as we kick off the New Year?\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID AGUS: You know, that-- that`s, you know, if I had that, I could, you know, win in Vegas. But I certainly think now we`re doing better against Delta. We`re vaccinating fifty-seven percent of the country, and as the next couple of weeks, we`re going to start to vaccinate children five through eleven should that get approved. And I think it will. And that`s another twenty-eight million people on the roster that can be vaccinated. So I think we`re going to be in a much better situation the end of the year. And I would vote good that around the holidays, we`re going to be back together without really worrying.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Doctor David Agus, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Former President Bill Clinton is out of the hospital. The seventy-five- year-old spent six days at UC Irvine Medical Center, treated for urological and blood infection. Doctors say his fever and blood work are normal. Clinton will finish a course of antibiotics at his home in New York.\par \par \uc2 Democrats in the nation`s capital face a fast approaching deadline to scale back President Biden`s agenda ambitions. CBS` Debra Alfarone is at the White House with the details.\par \par \uc2 DEBRA ALFARONE: Nikki, President Biden spent a rare weekend here at the White House, even fitting in some time to have a date night with the first lady. The House and Senate returns to Washington tomorrow. And negotiations over the President`s ambitious and expensive Build Back Better agenda will no doubt jump start again.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We`re not going to get 3.5 trillion dollars. We`ll get less than that. But we`re going to get it. And we`re going to come back and get the rest.\par \par \uc2 DEBRA ALFARONE: President Biden will start off the week with this admission behind him and negotiations ahead. On the potential chopping block--trillions of dollars, courtesy of moderate Democrats who are balking at the price tag. That could put the administration`s sweeping one-hundred-and-fifty- billion-dollar program pushing clean energy in doubt.\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG\uc2  (Transportation Secretary; CNN): The administration and the President are committed to bold climate action. Exactly what legislative form that takes is what`s being negotiated right now. But the bottom line is we have to act on climate for the good of our children, and by the way, for the good of our economy.\par \par \uc2 DEBRA ALFARONE: To complicate matters more, liberal Democrats say they`ll hold off voting on yet another piece of legislation--the one-trillion-dollar bipartisan infrastructure plan--as leverage to negotiate keeping what they want in the larger spending package.\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG\uc2  (Meet the Press): I`m not going to predict legislative mechanics. What I will say is that we`ve got to get this done.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RON JOHNSON (R-Wisconsin; Fox News): I hope for a Democrat gridlock. You know, oftentimes, in Washington, DC, gridlock is the better alternative. But when it`s Democrat gridlock, pray for it. I-- I hope that`s exactly what happens.\par \par \uc2 DEBRA ALFARONE: Democratic leadership has set a deadline of the end of the month to get both bills passed. President Biden said Friday there is no specific deadline, but he will push to get everything done. Nikki.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Debra Alfarone, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The United States has offered to pay the families of ten civilians, seven of them children, killed in a botched drone strike. It happened in the final days of America`s withdrawal from Afghanistan. CBS` Imtiaz Tyab is in Kabul, where he spoke with relatives of the victims.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: This is what the military once called a righteous strike, after firing Hellfire missiles at this house in Kabul, thinking it was an ISIS-K target. But for Romal Ahmadi, it`s been a living nightmare. The predator drone that targeted his brother Zemerai on August the 29th killed him and nine others, mostly children, including two of Ahmadi`s own sons and his baby daughter. The Pentagon now calls it a horrible mistake and has offered the Ahmadi family compensation of an undisclosed sum, as well as the opportunity to settle in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 I imagine there`s no compensation that can make up for all of that you`ve lost.\par \par \uc2 (Romal Ahmadi speaking foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: He says no compensation can take away my pain. And those who died will never come back. As Ahmadi shows us photos of his children, Arwin, Benyamin, and little Hayat, who were killed in the strike, his uncle Muhammad Nesim couldn`t hold back his tears, saying I feel very sad, especially when I see their pictures. It brings me back to that day. The Hellfire missile strike was just days after a horrific ISIS-K attack that killed thirteen U.S. service members and nearly one hundred and fifty Afghans trying to evacuate from the Kabul airport after the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  seized control of the country. According to the Pentagon, six drones tracked this white Toyota--seen here on security camera video-- for eight hours, believing it was linked to an imminent attack by ISIS-K. Instead it was being driven by Zemerai Ahmadi, who worked for a U.S. humanitarian aid agency specializing in malnutrition.\par \par \uc2 GENERAL \uc2 FRANK MCKENZIE\uc2  (U.S. Central Command Commander): We now assess that it is unlikely that the vehicle and those who died were associated with ISIS-K or were a direct threat to U.S. forces. I offer my profound condolences to the family and friends of those who were killed.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: A painful mistake the Ahmadi family will have to live with no matter how much they`re compensated.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab, CBS News, Kabul.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATISTE: The Grand Mosque in Mecca returned to full capacity today. Masks and vaccinated worshipers stood shoulder-to-shoulder for the first time since the pandemic began. The mosque in Saudi Arabia is Islam`s holiest site.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, the new trainees learning to drive eighteen wheelers amid a nationwide demand for truckers.\par \par \uc2 We head to the National Zoo where some animals are getting their COVID shots.\par \par \uc2 And later, mining the earth for a climate change solution.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: A lack of truck drivers is partly behind delivery delays across the country. The shortage is expected to reach one hundred thousand by 2023. It`s part of the transportation bottleneck that`s clogging U.S. ports especially in California. But as Chris Tye of our Chicago station WBBM reports, problems can create opportunities.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TYE (WBBM-TV Reporter): Trucker shortages have left cargo ships like these stacked up off the coast of California--\par \par \uc2 MAN: Look at the truck realistically.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TYE: --and left classrooms for would-be truckers in Chicago at capacity.\par \par \uc2 VERNON COOPER: Wow. I mean, the demands probably never been greater, obviously, just from an industry standpoint with the shortage of drivers.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TYE: Vernon Cooper runs Progressive Truck Driving School in south suburban Lancing. Classes here last a hundred and sixty hours at a cost of four thousand dollars. Starting salaries in the industry range between sixty and eighty thousand. And a new trend, twenty-five percent of students here are now female. GIA BRUNETTE (ph): By the end of October, I should be done.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TYE: Gia Brunette left a job as an occupational therapist where COVID knocked down her hours to four a day. This midlife reboot has triggered an entrepreneurial spirit.\par \par \uc2 GIA BRUNETTE: I`m going to try to get with a company that I can grow with. And then can I probably perhaps buy my own truck, and kind of do-- you know, be my own owner/operator.\par \par \uc2 NICK NAJJAR: There is the supply side, which is getting drivers in the industry, getting them trained, getting them in trucks, and then getting equipment to account for the increase in demand for freight.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TYE: Nick Najjar is the director of transportation for Land O` Lakes Diary. He says also stuck in these freighters, trucking equipment and engineering parts vital to making consumer production work, all part of a logistical headache that may leave store shelves a little thin this holiday season. But for Gia, job security has never been better.\par \par \uc2 GIA BRUNETTE: With this, I know can I go and work as many hours as I need to, you know. I don`t have to be limited to just four hours.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS TYE: And as that guessing game continues, those leaving truck driving school are making in year one what veterans used to make after twenty years.\par \par \uc2 Chris Tye, CBS News, Arlington Heights, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Still ahead on the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, first humans, now primates. The National Zoo starts shots to protect its beloved residents from COVID.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The Smithsonian`s National Zoo says its lions and tigers that tested positive for COVID last month are recovering well. Zoo staff are also taking preemptive measures to protect other animals as CBS` Weijia Jiang shows us.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: During the pandemic, this became an iconic image at the National Zoo, but good luck sticking a mask on one of these guys. So when one big cat caught COVID-19 in September--\par \par \uc2 DON NEIFFER: All nine of our great cats contracted COVID.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: How do you think they contracted COVID?\par \par \uc2 DON NEIFFER: So, they got it from somebody who looks like you or I. So, from a human.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Chief Veterinarian Don Neiffer believes an asymptomatic staff member transmitted the virus to the great cats.\par \par \uc2 DON NEIFFER: We really started having concerns about three of the cats, they weren`t drinking, they weren`t eating, so they weren`t drinking, getting any water from their food.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Neiffer worried that a sixteen-year-old lion named Shera would die, but like the others, she recovered. Still the outbreak was a reminder it`s not just people but certain animals that can get sick with COVID-19. Great apes, including orangutans, are among the most at risk. This week, they became some of the first at the zoo to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Good job.\par \par \uc2 DON NEIFFER: A lot of training, a lot of trust between the animals. And immediately after receiving that vaccine, I think two orangutans actually rubbed their injection site, which I do too when I get my shots.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Neiffer is hopeful that the vaccine will prevent other animals from struggling like Shera did.\par \par \uc2 DON NEIFFER: The severity of the disease, it seems like it`s changing. Our cats here were really hit hard by it.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The two-shot vaccine was developed specifically for animals by a veterinary pharmaceutical company, being administered at zoos around the country under emergency use.\par \par \uc2 Given how sick the cats got, you must be very relieved that there is a vaccine available.\par \par \uc2 DON NEIFFER: Yes. It`s really a game changer. But I know those of us that are getting the animals vaccinated, especially those of us who have dealt with these situations, we`re feeling a lot happier than we did six months ago.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The great cats will get their shots in about ninety days. Doses of hope that they will stay healthy.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang, CBS News, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: How beautiful are those animals.\par \par \uc2 Next on \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS, Greta Thunberg takes a surprising spin on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Spain`s erupting volcano on the island of La Palma keeps putting on a show and more disruptions. Half of the island`s flights were cancelled today. About seven thousand people have been forced to evacuate. The island`s president says there is no end in sight.\par \par \uc2 Olympic planners apparently leave nothing to chance. At the Acropolis in Greece today, this was the dress rehearsal for the flame lighting for the Beijing 2022 Winter Games. This flame will serve as a backup for the February event. One hiccup--two protesters were detained for drawing attention to human rights abuses in China.\par \par \uc2 Activist Greta Thunberg took to the stage at a climate live concert in Stockholm last night, not to make a speech but to dance.\par \par \uc2 (Greta Thunberg performing)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Thunberg surprised the crowd with those moves. Earlier she criticized the upcoming climate summit of world leaders Scotland, saying it`s unlikely to lead to big changes.\par \par \uc2 When we return, buried deep inside a mountain, a possible solution to climate change.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Finally tonight, miners and investors are getting excited about unique rocks found in Greenland. They`re similar to those found on the Moon and could be critical to our future on Earth. CBS` Ian Lee has the story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Who knew destroying the planet could help save it? In Greenland, scientists blasted open a mountain filled with what they call climate-saving rocks.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: The chemistry here is unique. It`s created in the early days of the formation of our-- our world.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: It`s called anorthosite, and what makes this rock so special is its purity, containing only three minerals--calcium, silica, and alumina. Miners are crushing it to create a more sustainable source of aluminum, along with other everyday items.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: We`d have a very good quality for fiberglass. We would also use it for fillers for paint and ceramics.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Experts say anorthosite produces less waste and greenhouse gas emissions. It`s found all around the island`s rugged terrain and turquoise fjords, but that`s not the only place.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: You find it in Canada. You find it in-- in Scandinavia, Russia, South Africa, you know, lots of places.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: So far, miners have collected more than three hundred tons. Even \uc2 NASA\uc2  is looking to see if what they learn down here could help them someday mine similar rocks on the Moon.\par \par \uc2 Ian Lee, CBS News.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Fascinating.\par \par \uc2 That`s the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS for this Sunday. Coming up tonight, 60 MINUTES. I`m Nikki Battiste in New York. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS   RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials  herein are protected by United  States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted,  displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any  trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020211208ehah00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Strike-Tober Continues As Thousands Of Workers Hit The Picket Line Nationwide\b0\par\par\uc2 Nikki Battiste, Lilia Luciano \par \uc2 458 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: American workers are testing their power. Thousands have hit picket lines in several states amid a nationwide labor shortage, seeking better pay, benefits, and working conditions. There`s even a name for it--Strike-tober. CBS` Lilia Luciano is in Los Angeles with more. Lilia.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (CBS News Correspondent): Nikki, here in L.A., a strike by Hollywood crews has been averted for now. But nationwide, many workers are walking off the job and right onto the picket line.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Pandemic pressure is spurring strikes, including this one in a suburb of Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 MARY TURNER (ICU Nurse): You don`t mess with the nurses.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: A walkout by nurses prompted a hospital in Plymouth to close its emergency room.\par \par \uc2 MARY TURNER: This is like my fifth picket during this pandemic. I find that deplorable.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Staffing shortages and the battle over benefits are impacting almost every industry. Ten thousand John Deere workers are on strike. The Illinois-based company is expected to post record profits this year.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Action.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: And a sixty-thousand Hollywood production workers strike was tentatively averted this weekend at the eleventh hour, though union members must still vote on the three-year deal with the studios.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA HAVERLAND (Prop Master): Union members are steaming mad.\par \par \uc2 Another three hundred wine bottles.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Alicia Haverland is a prop master in the IATSE union.\par \par \uc2 What were some of people`s complaints?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA HAVERLAND: I think it`s just the constant, endless feeling that, A, you`re going to lose your job; and, B, you`re never going to sleep. I was never hoping for a strike. I was hoping for a real, reasonable contract.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: They called it a Hollywood ending. Is it?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA HAVERLAND: I make Michael Bay movies. Things blow up. That`s kind of a Hollywood ending. I guess it depends on the movie.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Alicia says she wouldn`t call it an ending quite yet. And while crews will be back at work in sets like this one tomorrow, another massive walkout is expected soon by twenty-four thousand Kaiser employee-- workers who voted to strike. Nikki.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Lilia Luciano, thanks.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020211208ehah00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Missionaries And Children Kidnapped By Gang In Haiti\b0\par\par\uc2 Nikki Battiste, Manuel Bojorquez \par \uc2 607 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Tonight, kidnapped in Haiti. A group of American missionaries and children seized by a gang. The latest on efforts to find and free them.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, Strike-tober. From hospitals to the factory floor, workers walk off jobs nationwide over staff shortages and more pay, while Hollywood workers strike a deal.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: I`m Lilia Luciano in Los Angeles, where Hollywood crews won`t be going on strike tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Plus, COVID boosters. Who needs them? And what about mixing and matching vaccines? We`ll get a checkup from Doctor David Agus.\par \par \uc2 And we visit the National Zoo, where orangutans are the latest creatures getting their shots.\par \par \uc2 Presidential push. A deadline approaches to save Joe Biden`s agenda.\par \par \uc2 Mistaken strike. The U.S. will pay these Afghans for killing their relatives in a drone strike. CBS News is there and speaking to them.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: I imagine there`s no compensation that can make up for all that you`ve lost.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: And later, the rare rock mined in Greenland that could help save the planet.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Good evening. I`m Nikki Battiste. Jericka Duncan is off.\par \par \uc2 It is one of the world`s most dangerous countries besieged by poverty, political instability and violence. And tonight, the fate of more than a dozen American missionaries and family members reportedly kidnapped in the country is unknown. Police say they have been seized by a notorious criminal gang. It`s the latest in a series of kidnappings that happened just outside the capital Port-au-Prince. CBS` Manuel Bojorquez is there with the latest.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (CBS News National Correspondent): By some estimates, gangs control half of Haiti`s capital Port-au-Prince, and kidnappings are on the rise. But the sheer number of victims in this case and the fact that they are foreigners is brazen even by the gang`s standard.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The kidnappings reportedly happened as a group of missionaries was leaving an orphanage just outside of Port-au-Prince on Saturday. An armed gang abducted sixteen Americans, including five children, as well as one Canadian. The missionaries are affiliated with Ohio`s \uc2 Christian Aid\uc2  Ministries. The group`s website says its "`Haiti`- Sponsor-A-Child School Program`. pays for school tuition, uniforms, text books and meals for Haitians in fifty-two schools." In a statement about the kidnappings, \uc2 Christian Aid\uc2  Ministries said, "Join us in praying for those who are being held hostage, the kidnappers, and the families, friends, and churches of those affected." A surge in gang violence, including kidnappings of Haitians, continues to roil this country, already in the midst of a political crisis after a presidential assassination in July and an earthquake that killed more than two thousand in August.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The region where the kidnapping of the missionaries happened is controlled by one gang that is known to be among the most dangerous in Haiti, blamed earlier this year with the kidnapping of five priests and two nuns. Nikki.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: CBS` Manuel Bojorquez in Port-au-Prince, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020211208ehah00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Thousands Of Workers On Strikes\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson, Jonathan Vigliotti \par \uc2 723 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thousands of workers are on strike this morning across the U.S. That includes more than ten thousand employees at John Deere plants. Around fourteen hundred Kellogg`s employees have been on strike for nearly two weeks. And twenty-four thousand \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  health care workers have authorized a strike. On Monday, film and TV production across the country could shut down, and a work stoppage involving sixty thousand crew members. They`re demanding safer working conditions. Jonathan Vigliotti has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AMY THURLOW (ph): Currently, I work sometimes eighty hours a week.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: You say eighty hours a week so casually.\par \par \uc2 AMY THURLOW: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Amy Thurlow is a writer`s assistant in Hollywood, a job that makes just over state minimum wage. She`s also a member of the International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees or IATSE, a union with around sixty thousand of some of the most essential workers in entertainment, like camera operators and set designers who could all strike on Monday.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW LOEB (IATSE International President): We`re talking about people who love their work.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: IATSE President Matthew Loeb says his members are asking for better pay and quality of life like a guarantee of ten hours between shifts.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW LOEB: No person might go to work in the morning and not get a meal for nine or ten hours or a break during the day.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: IATSE is not the only union taking action. More than ten thousand \uc2 united auto workers\uc2  at John Deere plants are striking for the first time in thirty-five years, walking away from a contract that would have provided up to a six percent raise which workers said was not enough as company earnings are expected to hit record highs.\par \par \uc2 KARI BORMANN: Really doing what we`ve needed to do--standing up for ourselves.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: With more than ten million open jobs across the U.S. and much of the country rebounding from the worst of COVID-19, a more assertive labor force shouldn`t come as a surprise, says former labor secretary \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REICH\uc2  (Former Labor Secretary): At the end of that crisis, workers begin to say, wait a minute, maybe I ought to be doing better. You have this huge surge in pent-up demand. At the same time, you`ve got a shortage of workers, and a lot of workers know that this is the time if there`s ever a time to flex their muscles.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, I`m Jonathan Vigliotti in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now all of the companies involved in these labor disputes say they are committed to reaching fair contracts for all parties.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the workers are saying what we got so far is not fair.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. We have new developments in a story we`ve followed for weeks now, a prominent South Carolina lawyer accused of trying to have himself killed. Alex Murdaugh is now charged with stealing more than four million dollars of insurance money intended for his housekeeper`s sons. She died, you`ll recall, in 2018 after a fall in Murdaugh`s home. While he`s been arrested as of yesterday at a Florida drug rehab facility, and his lawyer says Murdaugh had been there for the past six weeks after allegedly asking a friend to shoot him in the head so his surviving son could collect ten million dollars in life insurance. Murdaugh is charged with insurance fraud and filing a false police report in that case. Hard to keep track of all this. He`s also a person of interest in the murders of his wife and another son back in June. Lot going on.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Very bizarre and sad story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020211025ehaf0006q\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Thousands Of U.S. Workers Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Charlie De Mar \par \uc2 464 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, tonight, more than ten thousand workers at John Deere are out on strike. It`s the first strike there in more than three decades, and we may see more high profile strikes as unions up their demands. CBS` Charlie De Mar reports from a John Deere factory in Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR (CBS News Correspondent): These John Deere workers say they plan to picket around the clock after negotiations with the agriculture equipment giant broke down over a new labor contract that would have increased salaries for some workers by six percent. Kari Bormann (ph) has worked for the company for fifteen years.\par \par \uc2 KARI BORMANN: Really doing what we`ve needed to do--standing up for ourselves, standing our ground.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: The walkout comes as the company is forecasting its best earnings ever. The strike is just the latest example of workers flexing their muscle. The union representing sixty thousand Hollywood crew members say they will go on strike as soon as Monday, which could halt most entertainment production.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH EINBINDER (Actress): Livable hour and benefits. Like, it`s the bare minimum that they`re asking for.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: And workers are pushing back. At Kellogg, they are in their second week of their strike. This week, the Labor Department released data showing a record 4.3 million workers quit their jobs in August, nearly three percent of the workforce. With more than ten million job openings across the country, former U.S. Labor Secretary \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2  says workers are emboldened.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REICH\uc2 : Employers are feeling the muscle that American workers are now flexing. Employers have got to pay more and provide better working conditions, and they are getting the message.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: Do you think that`s, you know, plays into this, that sort of the power has shifted to the employees?\par \par \uc2 KARI BORMANN: Absolutely. Absolutely. I think it`s time that we can tell them what we want.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DE MAR: This is the first time in thirty-five years that workers here at John Deere have walked off the job. The company says they are working day and night to try and bring an end to this strike. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Charlie De Mar, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211015ehae0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Southwest Cancels Thousands Of Flights, Leading To Chaos At Airports Across The Country\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Errol Barnett \par \uc2 872 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight--\par \par \uc2 Travel, chaos and confusion. \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  cancels thousands of flights, leaving passengers stranded. Could a pilot walkout over vaccine mandates be the cause? Passengers sound off after mask cancellations.\par \par \uc2 TERESA SMITH: Not a great end to a good vacation.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the blame game.\par \par \uc2 Triple weather threat. Dangerous storms from the Plains to the Upper Midwest, tornadoes in Oklahoma and snow in the Rockies. The latest on the severe weather.\par \par \uc2 Plane crash. Homes destroyed when a small aircraft plunges into a California neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 Breakthrough treatment. The first pill to treat COVID is a step closer to authorization. Plus, new research on the devastating effect COVID has on pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 Out of a spy movie. What we`re learning tonight about a Navy scandal, involving a nuclear engineer, a peanut butter sandwich and a plot to sell America`s secrets to a foreign government.\par \par \uc2 Schools targeted. The dangerous new challenge promoted on social media. Schools vandalized. Now, there`s a warning that teachers could be victimized next.\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from "Help"; \uc2 Parlophone Records\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Setting the record straight. Decades later, who Paul McCartney says broke up The Beatles.\par \par \uc2 And this incredible story. A runner who lost his sight at an early age, but not his ability to inspire.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the CBS EVENING NEWS with Norah O`Donnell, reporting from the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you so much for joining us on this Monday. Well, we`re going to begin tonight with the anger and frustration of \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  passengers as a weekend of delays and cancellations stretched all the way into Monday. More than three hundred and sixty flights were canceled today after about two thousand were called off over the weekend. Now, why it`s happening isn`t exactly clear. The airline blames the weather and, quote, "other constraints" but disruptions began shortly after the pilots union tried to block Southwest`s new COVID vaccine mandate. What is clear is that thousands of Southwest pas-- passengers were left stranded, lines were long and tempers were short at airports in several cities as passengers were forced to wait hours and in some cases days, yes, days, to get on other flights. Well, CBS` Errol Barnett is going to lead us off tonight from Reagan National Airport, just outside of here in DC. Good evening, Errol.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT (CBS News Transportation Correspondent): Norah, good evening. There are folks out there tonight still waiting to get to their weekend destinations and some having to pay hundreds of dollars out of pocket just to get there. Now while Southwest struggles to rebuild its schedule, we spoke to the union representing Southwest pilots who say there was no walkout.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: From L.A. to Miami--\par \par \uc2 TERESA SMITH: It`s not a great end to a good vacation.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: --Dallas to Denver--\par \par \uc2 RYAN LACY We`re-- we`re going to try to get there. When? I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: --thousands of Southwest customers had weekend travel plans ruined by more than two thousand flight cancellations. Tonight, the company offering a, quote, "tremendous apology to customers and employees." citing "weather and external constraints."\par \par \uc2 Did any of your pilots call out sick this weekend?\par \par \uc2 CASSEY MURRAY: We have data on what exactly happened, and so our pilot sick rates were-- are right in line with-- with what was occurring this summer.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Captain Cassey Murray is president of the Southwest pilots` union and insists there was no protest of the company`s pending vaccine mandate. But he acknowledged the union is suing Southwest over its policy.\par \par \uc2 CASSEY MURRAY: The pilots were-- were picking up extra flying.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Southwest stood out because other carriers had nowhere near its rate of cancellations. Some stranded passengers hit the road to get back home.\par \par \uc2 JULIANNE MATTOX: We`re home.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: After how many hours on the road?\par \par \uc2 JULIANNE MATTOX: Twenty-five total.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Twenty-five hours.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Julianne Mattox attended a wedding in Austin with two friends before Southwest canceled their Sunday flight back to DC, offering Wednesday instead.\par \par \uc2 JULIANNE MATTOX: The general consensus among everyone was it`s easier to-- to rent a car and-- and just get out of town.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Fifteen hundred miles later, the trio made it home safely today.\par \par \uc2 JULIANNE MATTOX: So, probably, we won`t be looking at Southwest anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: And this is not over yet. Southwest says it is trying to rebuild its schedule. But, Norah, it does expect for there to be continued cancellations through Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right. Errol Barnett, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020211012ehab0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King; Tony Dokoupil; Vlad Duthiers; Mola Lenghi; Laurie  Segall; Allison Seymour; Chester Lampkin; \par \uc2 6,606 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING, CBS NEWS ANCHOR: It`s Thursday, September second, 2021. We welcome you back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. That`s Tony Dokoupil, he`s Vladimir Duthiers, I`m Gayle King. Devastating and historic rain and floods in the northeast. The latest on the destruction from what was once Hurricane Ida.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For our series "The Work Shift," we look at how working from home hurts small businesses that rely on people showing up in person.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And losing loved ones to QAnon. "60 MINUTES+" shows families being torn apart by baseless online conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 KING: First, today`s eye opener at 8:00.\par \par \uc2 Extreme weather cutting a destructive path through multiple states, affecting at least 57 million people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Nearby Down Brook (ph) flooded overnight, creating this mess that you see here behind me. The road impassable.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The system made its way across New Jersey, Pennsylvania, New York City, Connecticut, producing rain rates up to four inches an hour.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The governor says there are more line workers in the state than at any other point in history. But for the hundreds of thousands still without power, things can`t move fast enough.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The fire is still burning and we`re less than 50 yards away from homes that are heavily guarded by exhausted firefighters, who have been battling this blaze for weeks with no clear end in sight.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In her dissent, Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  called the court`s decision "stunning." But for now this Texas law, which bans abortion after just six weeks of pregnancy, will remain in place.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The company that makes Ms. Dash seasoning has come out with a new seasoning made from -- get this -- Twix candy bars. The Twix shaker seasoning blend says it combines the bar`s crunchy cookie, soft caramel and milk chocolate flavors.\par \par \uc2 You know what this would be great on?\par \par \uc2 A Snickers.\par \par \uc2 KING: You guys cook. Any takers?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL, CBS NEWS ANCHOR: I don`t think you cook with that. You put it on some yogurt. Called dessert.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS, CBS NEWS ANCHOR: I`d eat that.\par \par \uc2 KING: Something new to think about.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin with the extreme weather that was here in the Northeast. Flash flooding from record-breaking rain killed at least nine people in the area, including seven right here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 There are states of emergency this morning in New York and New Jersey, which also saw tornadoes. Now this is the same system that devastated Louisiana as Hurricane Ida. Mola Lenghi`s in Middlesex, New Jersey, with more on the story.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you. Most of us had never seen anything like this and it was really, really scary.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI, CBS NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, pretty intense stuff here, Gayle. First responders have been evacuating people from these homes and these flooded neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 I`m at a four-way intersection and three of the four streets are completely flooded as far as I can see. It was a wild night across several states as the remnants of Ida tore down power lines, brought historic rain, dangerous flooding and tornadoes up and down the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 The rain here in the tri-state area was so extreme that, for the first time ever, New York City was placed under a flash flood emergency warning. These videos show just a glimpse, up to a foot of water flooding parts of New York City, stranding people and nearly swallowing cars.\par \par \uc2 The water gushing into people`s homes, leaving tens of thousands in the area without power. Officers and the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  have been responding to 9-1-1 calls of people stuck in the storm.\par \par \uc2 You can see video here of water pouring down in New York City subways. Some lines are running again this morning. But all of them, all lines on the subway system were shut down last night.\par \par \uc2 Video at the Newark International Airport showed flooding there, as well, yesterday. The weather prompting delays and cancelations of flights, causing a nightmare and a headache there.\par \par \uc2 In New Jersey, we saw not just intense flooding but homes destroyed by tornadoes. We watched this one rip through Mullica Hill, splintering this home nearby. It was the strongest torn hit the state in 30 years.\par \par \uc2 This appears to be another tornado that just touched down across the Delaware River in Pennsylvania. As you can imagine, power outages are being reported up and down the East Coast, with more than 200,000 people affected just in Pennsylvania, New York and New Jersey alone -- Guys.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: We`re glad to see the sun is at least shining this morning. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to our special series "The Work Shift," on how work has changed during the pandemic. This crisis has ushered in a change that many Americans wanted for years: flexibility, the freedom to work remotely, the freedom to work from anywhere.\par \par \uc2 For some, the end of being tied to an office is a reason to celebrate. But offices did not just create tall buildings; they also created thousands of jobs to support those people working inside them. Now many of those jobs are at risk.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL (voice-over): America spends so much time obsessing about the office --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Accounts payable, Nina speaking --\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL (voice-over): -- that sometimes it can feel like desk jobs are the only jobs around.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Clearing my desk. I can`t concentrate.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL (voice-over): But for workers like me to be here --\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Hey. Long time no see.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL (voice-over): There need to be people like Mohammad Salim (ph) who, for the past 10 years, has run a newsstand inside the \uc2 CBS\uc2  broadcast center in Manhattan. It was a dream location for him and his wife, Shaista (ph).\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMAD SALIM (PH), NEWSSTAND OPERATOR: This is a Monday to Friday business and it`s morning to evening, not too many long hours.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: This is a good job to have.\par \par \uc2 SALIM (PH): Good job to have.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL (voice-over): But as the coronavirus spread in March of 2020 --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s going to be a new reality in this city.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL (voice-over): -- the city, the nation and our building closed, meaning the Salims (ph) were closed, as well, for more than a year.\par \par \uc2 When they finally could reopen, they found thousands of dollars worth of expired food that had to be tossed and repurchased.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: If you don`t mind me asking, what portion of your savings have you had to spend to stay afloat?\par \par \uc2 SALIM (PH): Approximately I spent already $25,000.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL (voice-over): Muhammad has been driving for Uber while waiting for his business to bounce back. But so far, New York`s grand reopening hasn`t been so grand.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: How does the foot traffic and the business today compare --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SALIM (PH): Ten percent right now, 10 percent people are here in the building.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SALIM (PH): Used to I do $1,000 sale for the day.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: For a day?\par \par \uc2 SALIM (PH): For a day before. Now is like approximately $200.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL (voice-over): And what`s happening here is happening all over. Before the pandemic, New York`s Hudson Yards opened four gleaming new office towers and dozens of retail and food locations to serve the workers inside them, workers who are only just now and just barely trickling back.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: First day back since February --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: First day back, wow.\par \par \uc2 How many people are back in your office?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Not everyone and you got to be flexible. Everyone`s got their own decisions to make.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Is it like 10 percent back, 30 percent back?\par \par \uc2 20? 20 percent?\par \par \uc2 OK.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL (voice-over): That means a lot less business for people like the local dry cleaner.\par \par \uc2 DEWI INDAHWATI, DRY CLEANERS MANAGER: Hi. How are you?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL (voice-over): Dewi Indahwati is the manager here and buildings where her delivery guy once collected heaps of business casual office clothes to be cleaned and pressed, he now sometimes walks away with nothing.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: So this is pre-COVID-19, business booming, big cart.\par \par \uc2 INDAHWATI: Right.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: This is now.\par \par \uc2 INDAHWATI: Now. Sometimes it`s empty.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL (voice-over): Lately she`s been calling longtime customers to find out when or if their business might return.\par \par \uc2 INDAHWATI: I try to make with the customer like a family, like a friend. So like, hey, how you doing?\par \par \uc2 How you doing -- you always say that to them.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Are they going to come back to you?\par \par \uc2 INDAHWATI: Yes. But some go, oh, I work at home. I`m with my mom. You know, what can I say?\par \par \uc2 Oh, OK, when you coming back?\par \par \uc2 I don`t know. Maybe yes, maybe not.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL (voice-over): The reality is some may never come back. Office vacancies across the U.S. have risen above 16 percent. And one survey shows three-quarters of companies here in New York still have no plans to bring employees back to the office full time.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Hey, how are you? DOKOUPIL (voice-over): That fact is killing the Hudson Yards Cafe and the food cart`s co-owner, Alex Campos (ph).\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: How were things going before coronavirus?\par \par \uc2 ALEX CAMPOS (PH), HUDSON YARDS CAFE: Oh, excellent.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Excellent?\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS (PH): Yes. Excellent.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL (voice-over): But now business is still down 95 percent. Pastries that used to sell out by 10:00 in the morning still crowd the window long after lunch. And when we met him, Alex was close to his breaking point.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS (PH): I mean, I`ve been working really hard.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Having a moment?\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS (PH): Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL (voice-over): All these months later it`s still difficult to process.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: You OK, bud?\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS (PH): Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: How it all went so wrong so quickly and why towers that should be filled with people are not.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: You OK?\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS (PH): Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: What are you thinking, what are you feeling right now?\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS (PH): I work too hard and I think, for one day you just lost everything.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: And there are a lot of livelihoods at stake here. Businesses like food carts, dry cleaners, newsstands, it employs more than 150,000 Americans, that was as of 2019; 28 percent, almost a third of those jobs, disappeared last year alone.\par \par \uc2 And that may only be the start of a major shift. According to one expert, we`ll talk about in our next hour, this could be the beginning of the end of this model. My eyes almost fell out of my head when Mohammed in our building said he spent $25,000 of his own savings just to restock his store.\par \par \uc2 It`s not like we`re all back in business here and he`s making his money back. He`s not.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: What I love about your journalism and the reporting that you do is you often make us think about our place in the world and how we`re interconnected with the world around us in ways that we don`t sometimes consider.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: Mr. Salim, he works in our building and we hadn`t thought about that.\par \par \uc2 KING: I never about that, never thought about dry cleaning and was really touched by the man who got emotional with you. And he said something very poignant when he said, I work so hard and then you lose it all in one day, through no fault of your own.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: I know, and he`s at the pinnacle of his career. He and his partner worked for years to get a corner like that with four new buildings --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: -- he was set up to make money. And instead, it`s just going to go away. That 150,000 number for jobs, that`s the size of the oil and gas extraction in this country. That`s bigger than the coal industry. It`s a major part of our economy and it`s really hurting.\par \par \uc2 KING: It takes me back to what \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  said when he was here, that we can control this if we do the right thing and get the vaccine. Everybody can go back to work.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KING: You can go back to work.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Culturally, we may decide we don`t want to.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: If Mr. Salim is watching, I`m coming downstairs today. I`m going to be buying some food.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: All right, I`m sure he is.\par \par \uc2 All right, tomorrow, our series, "The Work Shift," looks at the exodus from big cities into small towns and rural areas during the pandemic as people move closer to nature. See how these population changes have unintended consequences, like soaring, absolutely soaring housing prices.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:11. Have a look at the weather.\par \par \uc2 CHESTER LAMPKIN, AMS METEOROLOGIST: Hi, everyone. I`m meteorologist Chester Lampkin tracking a beautiful day here. Look at this sunshine. Blue skies. Low humidity. Temps in the 70`s this afternoon. We`ll stay there for tomorrow as well.\par \par \uc2 A few more clouds on Friday, 79. Back to the 80`s this weekend. We may see an isolated shower or thunderstorm on Sunday but nothing that will ruin your outdoor plans this holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: "60 MINUTES+" is sharing the stories of six people who say QAnon damaged their relationships with loved ones. These six strangers first connected on a Reddit forum called QAnon Casualties, an online community with more than 180,000 members.\par \par \uc2 "60 MINUTES+" correspondent Laurie Segall spoke to the group about their frustration and their sense of loss at watching loved ones, who they say have fallen into a web of conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAURIE SEGALL, CBS NEWS CORRESPONDENT: How many of you have loved ones who support QAnon?\par \par \uc2 How many of you have loved ones who believe there`s a global cabal of liberal elite pedophiles who run a child sex trafficking ring?\par \par \uc2 That`s pretty extraordinary, even saying those words out loud.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What`s even stranger is hearing them say it.\par \par \uc2 SEGALL (voice-over): By now you`ve heard of the term QAnon, the pro-Trump web of conspiracy theories that began as a fringe online movement in 2017 but has since exploded. These six people -- sons, daughters, brothers and partners -- say that are bound by the loss of their loved ones to QAnon.\par \par \uc2 SEGALL: There`s almost a lingo for what it is that you guys are experiencing: Q siblings, Q spouse, Q parents, what does it mean to have Q family?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: To not have them. They are lost to us. And we are clinging for the idea of them without the Q.\par \par \uc2 SEGALL: You guys are all in a sense united by loss and grief. But we`re not talking about death. What`s extraordinary is we`re talking about conspiracies.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And I think that they`re very similar, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 "60 MINUTES+" correspondent and Dot Dot Dot media founder Laurie Segall joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Laurie, these people are grieving the loss of loved ones as if they almost died. But they`re not dead.\par \par \uc2 What`s this like for them?\par \par \uc2 SEGALL: I mean, it`s just devastating, right. And you saw all those people, you can see the pain is palpable. I mean, the stories are just so tragic.\par \par \uc2 One of the women we spoke to, when you watch the piece you`ll hear her speak about how she, before this she had struggled with thoughts of suicide and depression. But she said her father saved her life.\par \par \uc2 And now fast forward a year later, she says she can`t even speak to her father. She had to cut him off for mental health reasons.\par \par \uc2 Another man says he can`t hold his girlfriend because he`s been vaccinated and she says he`s been jabbed. She believes he`s, quote, "shedding." So it`s really the --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KING: There`s a cover story in "People" magazine right now about a father who everybody said was normal, devoted to his children, who killed his two young children because he believed something about QAnon was affecting them.\par \par \uc2 How does this happen to people, who are seemingly so normal, so thoughtful and so smart?\par \par \uc2 SEGALL: I think that`s what a lot of folks are struggling with. And I think there are a lot of different factors. One -- I would go ahead and say someone who`s covered technology for a long time, look at the misinformation that`s been going online and that`s been allowed to spread online over the last couple of years.\par \par \uc2 KING: They believe the misinformation.\par \par \uc2 SEGALL: And people believe it. You click on one thing, it serves you up another thing, now you add to this a pandemic, where people have been isolated, they`re home, looking for a community.\par \par \uc2 Really this is what a lot of this is. This is a community, so much so that our experts are calling this a public health crisis. They see people are self-radicalizing. They say we need to have education in schools about this. We need trusted voices to speak on it.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: So in the old days, a crazy person could put a flyer under your windshield wiper and you would ball it up and it`s done. Now the misinformation comes much more quickly.\par \par \uc2 So is the answer for tech companies to take care of this?\par \par \uc2 SEGALL: I think they should have taken care of this a long time ago. I mean, I remember the rise of QAnon happened in 2017. \uc2 Facebook\uc2  put an emphasis on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  groups to really help build community. What they didn`t realize at the time was they were helping people behind closed internet doors gather.\par \par \uc2 And they didn`t crack down on this for years. But I think now, as our experts are saying, looking at this as almost a public health crisis, where there`s more information on this, there`s more educational tools.\par \par \uc2 One of our experts has a site called Antidote, where people can learn how to speak to their relatives who have fallen into conspiracy theories. I think that`s something we`ve got to learn how to grapple with, because this is almost a new normal.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: It really is. Can`t wait to see your report. Laurie Segall, thank you very much, as always.\par \par \uc2 You can watch Laurie`s full report, "QAnon Casualties" right now on "60 MINUTES+" on the \uc2 ViacomCBS\uc2  streaming service Paramount Plus.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Ahead, history in the baking. We`ll meet a woman who uses cookies to tell the stories of forgotten Asian American pioneers. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 KING: Coming up, a Seattle man`s stunning medical bill surprise: two summer surgeries but two very different prices. Yikes. Local news is coming right up.\par \par \uc2 ALLISON SEYMOUR, CBS NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good Thursday morning. I`m Allison Seymour. We have a sad update to pass along. A 19-year-old man has died after homes were flooded at an apartment in Rock Creek Woods Apartments in Rockville, Maryland, yesterday morning.\par \par \uc2 A heavy downpour sent floodwaters into a ground floor apartment and it happened about 3:00 am yesterday when most families were sound asleep. The 19-year-old Milken Daniel Sedaio (ph) drowned in the flood. Another person is still unaccounted for. A \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  has been set up to help the family.\par \par \uc2 We have that information on our website, wusa9.com -- Chester.\par \par \uc2 LAMPKIN: Thanks a lot, Allison and today, improving weather. Dry skies all day long. Temperatures comfortable. Low humidity. 70`s to around 80 degrees this afternoon here in the DMV.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, similar weather, we`ll just throw in a few more clouds. We`ll see a high near 80 again. Low 80`s on Saturday. Low 80`s on Sunday. Sunday, we may dodge a couple showers or a thunderstorm. But for the most part, the holiday weekend is looking pretty solid.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Good morning. Coming up today on "GREAT DAY WASHINGTON," we explore why 41 percent of workers are contemplating quitting their job. If you`re one of them, we have some tips on how to reignite your career without leaving your job. See you for "GREAT DAY," starting at 9 am.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS, CBS NEWS ANCHOR: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. It is that time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 And Tony, what have you got for us?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL, CBS NEWS ANCHOR: I`ve got a little bit of a cultural add-on to the story that we reported earlier about America`s shift to working remotely and how it`s hurting a lot of businesses that rely on office workers, like newsstands and dry cleaners.\par \par \uc2 We also spoke to Nikil Saval, the author of an amazing book called "Cubed: A Secret History of the Workplace." It`s about how offices came to be and essentially defined our skylines and our culture in this relatively short history and how it now may be going away. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKIL SAVAL, AUTHOR: Right now we have pretty high vacancy rates across American cities. But even those vacancy rates probably understate the degree to which people won`t be returning to offices.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: What does that mean for the guy with the coffee cart or the dry cleaner, the lunch spot?\par \par \uc2 SAVAL: It`s a real problem and it`s a real question. I think that they`ve already suffered, you know, over the last 1.5 years. And I think that it`s not sustainable right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: So the big question is, like, all those buildings behind us are office buildings by and large.\par \par \uc2 So what happens to them if only half of the people need to use them?\par \par \uc2 They`re going to be converted -- we`re going to need new comic strips, new sitcoms, new movies --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KING: We`re still not back 100 percent. I actually really do miss an office full of people. I really do.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: I do, too. I miss seeing our colleagues, walking over to a producer and saying, let`s look at this, you and I, figure out how to do it --\par \par \uc2 KING: I just think it`s a better work environment.\par \par \uc2 But you`re right, Tony, maybe the time -- maybe it has really changed permanently. Hope not.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: And Nikil there, the reason he`s in a tie is because he`s actually now a state senator, not a writer, and he represents Philadelphia. So he`s trying to figure this out for his own city.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: It`s like new paradigm and we don`t know it yet.\par \par \uc2 KING: Continuing about working from home, be careful what you do during a Zoom meeting.\par \par \uc2 Are we all Zoomed out?\par \par \uc2 Yes, we are. A new survey shows almost one in four bosses at U.S. companies has fired a worker for a video or audio conference mistake. Most of these mistakes involve interfering with a virtual meeting; for example, not being able to share information or showing up late because of technical glitches.\par \par \uc2 Other problems include bad internet connections, frozen screens and audio problems. Now 42 percent of the bosses in the survey blame workers for the mishaps. I`ll bet workers have something else to say about that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KING: We`re all in a Zoom meeting and people are on mute and you go, God, don`t you have this right by now?\par \par \uc2 Until it happened to me the other day.\par \par \uc2 "Gayle, you`re on mute."\par \par \uc2 You feel stoopid with two Os, oh, sorry. But it is. We`re still navigating Zoom.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: Well, I mean, first of all --\par \par \uc2 KING: I`m sick of it myself.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: It`s not the worker`s fault if your internet service provider is giving you less than ideal service.\par \par \uc2 KING: It`s not.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: No. It`s not. I hope those people get their jobs back if that`s really why you`re getting laid off but I have a feeling it may be a little something else.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: Yes, maybe something else.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: All right. So I told you earlier about "The Wonder Years" reboot.\par \par \uc2 KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: So I have another one.\par \par \uc2 KING: Don Cheadle`s doing it.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: Yes. I told you about "The Wonder Years." My "Talk of the Table" is about another upcoming reboot. This one, "The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air." And Will Smith, who starred in the original sitcom, just revealed his replacement -- check out the surprise Zoom call.\par \par \uc2 We like this Zoom call, Gayle, where will breaks the good news to a young actor. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILL SMITH, ACTOR: From the deepest parts of my heart, I want to say congratulations to you. You have the role of Will on "Bel-Air."\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, God. Thank you. I`m ready. I`m so ready, y`all. I`ll ready to bite down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: Yes. Look at this -- he was finally there to sit on his throne as "The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air." Jabari Banks, that`s his name, he calls it a dream come true. According to "Variety," Banks actually lives in West Philadelphia, born and raised on the school ground is where I spent most of my days.\par \par \uc2 He graduated from University of the Arts in Philadelphia last year. Of course the original show ran from 1990 to 1996. The reboot will stream on NBC.\par \par \uc2 I hope Jabari is a huge star and then he gets to do actual original series.\par \par \uc2 Can we come up with a new idea, people in Hollywood, please?\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: You`re not a reboot fan?\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: I mean, like, how many of them do there need to be?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KING: They were talking the other day about rebooting "Entourage," which I also think could be fun. But I loved Jabari`s reaction, I loved that he said he was ready. He looked like he was talking in his closet.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: -- struggling actor, you know.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That`s the difference between starting off and then being Will Smith.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KING: You go, Jabari. That`s great. Good for Will. I love Will was clearly enjoying that moment, too. I like that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He totally was.\par \par \uc2 KING: Turning now to a very important story about health care costs and how two similar surgeries ended up with two very different bills for one Seattle man.\par \par \uc2 His name is Ely Bair. He had related jaw surgeries at the same hospital under the same insurance provider. Now Bair thought that would mean similar medical costs. But he was, oh, so wrong.\par \par \uc2 His bill for the second procedure was nearly -- listen to this -- 10 times higher. Yes, 10 times. We`re looking at the story as part of our continuing Bill of the Month partnership with Kaiser Health News and NPR.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ELY BAIR, JAW SURGERY PATIENT: I had migraines, I would say, almost weekly.\par \par \uc2 KING (voice-over): Ely Bair had severe jaw pain until he underwent a two- part surgery to realign his mouth palate. He had Premera health insurance through his job that covered most of the first surgery on his upper jaw in 2018. And he paid $3,000 out of pocket.\par \par \uc2 Bair switched jobs before his second surgery on his lower jaw in 2020 but still had Premera health insurance and did the procedure at the same hospital.\par \par \uc2 BAIR: After the second surgery, that really fixed everything. My migraines were gone.\par \par \uc2 KING (voice-over): And then the bills came. This time, Bair`s out-of- pocket expenses totaled more than $27,000. He learned that having the same insurance provider did not mean the same coverage when he changed jobs. And his new plan, had a $5,000 lifetime limit policy for jaw surgery.\par \par \uc2 BAIR: I was speechless because I had never heard of that. I had a little mini breakdown. The amount was pretty devastating.\par \par \uc2 KING (voice-over): After Bair filed a complaint with the state attorney general`s office, the hospital lowered his bill to about $7,000. Bair is now paying that balance but he feels blindsided.\par \par \uc2 BAIR: I feel like I have a lot of distrust in hospitals and the insurance industry. I just feel like the process is too confusing and, you know, could wind up devastating if you get a surprise bill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KING: Now Premera insurance did not respond to our request for comment. But In a statement to Kaiser Health News it said that the plan details are available online and it highly recommends members to contact them to verify the benefits.\par \par \uc2 The Swedish hospital told CBS News that Bair was billed for the care that he received. However, the surgeon advocated to get the bill lowered since the patient and the provider were not aware of the lifetime limit before his surgery.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Dr. Elisabeth Rosenthal, she`s the editor-in-chief of Kaiser Health News.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you, Dr. Rosenthal.\par \par \uc2 Can you explain this insurance policy issue that Ely was relying on?\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH ROSENTHAL, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, KAISER HEALTH NEWS: Oh, yes. One of the wonderful things the Affordable Care Act did was to impose lifetime limits in 10 essential categories. But jaw surgery is not one of them.\par \par \uc2 And it`s in this gray area between health insurance and medical insurance. And that`s where Ely got trapped.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is so many of us assume that, when we see the same name on our insurance card, the policies will work the same. But what benefits you get from any one insurance depends largely on what your employer decides to give you.\par \par \uc2 So in switching employers, he had the same-looking insurance card but the benefits were hugely different.\par \par \uc2 KING: It always seems so complicated to me.\par \par \uc2 So what can people do before and after the surgery to avoid this happening to them?\par \par \uc2 Or is there anything you can do?\par \par \uc2 Are you frozen?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, and she lost her --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ROSENTHAL: Well, I think one thing that`s really important for people --\par \par \uc2 KING: Go ahead. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 ROSENTHAL: One thing that`s really important for people to know is, if you ask your doctor, is this going to be covered, you know, they want it to be covered. So they`re going to say, sure, it should be covered by your insurance.\par \par \uc2 But as Ely discovered, this was -- in an 80-page booklet. So his doctor didn`t know. So it`s really important to contact your insurer to verify those benefits.\par \par \uc2 The second thing is, if you don`t get redress and you get this surprise bill, you know, good for Ely, go to your state attorney general or your state consumer protection board. They may be able to help. And your doctor may be able to help.\par \par \uc2 You know, patients and doctors shouldn`t have to read 80-page booklets and the fine print to know if their essential surgery is going to be covered or not.\par \par \uc2 KING: You know, Dr. Elisabeth Rosenthal, those 80-page booklets will get you every single time. I don`t know anybody who`s reading that stuff. Thank you, though, thank you very much, even though I know we should. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, our "A More Perfect Union" series looks at an artist`s sweet tribute to trailblazing Asian Americans, who are often overlooked.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:39, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 CHESTER LAMPKIN, AMS METEOROLOGIST: Meteorologist Chester Lampkin here on a Thursday. Good morning to you. Looking at the weather we have today, just really fantastic stuff with those numbers. Just shy of 80 across most locations -- close to 80 here in the district, 80 Fredericksburg, 76 in Gavisburg (ph) and ample sunshine through the day on your hourly planner here.\par \par \uc2 Heading into the next couple of days, still nice, Friday and Saturday, maybe an isolated shower or storm on Sunday and a little warmer for Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: Our series, "A More Perfect Union," aims to show that what unites us as Americans is far greater than what divides us. I love this series. Today we are sharing the story of an artist who`s working to tell the stories of Asian American icons, who are often left out of the history books. And she`s doing that by baking.\par \par \uc2 That`s right. Adriana Diaz first caught up with Jasmine Cho before the pandemic and before Adriana`s pregnancy with her soon-to-be-born baby.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JASMINE CHO, BAKER:\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ, CBS NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Jasmine Cho is an avid baker but her confections aren`t necessarily for consumption.\par \par \uc2 CHO: They`re not really intended to be eaten. They`re intended to be digested internally.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ: I like that.\par \par \uc2 CHO: Intellectually, yes. It`s their face, it`s their identity.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ (voice-over): Digested intellectually because of what and who they represent, people she`s trying to rescue from the back burners of history, Asian American icons who helped shape our country yet remain largely unknown.\par \par \uc2 CHO: Yes, so this is Afong Moy, the first Chinese woman to emigrate to America. And she was basically brought over to be showcased to the world, almost like a circus freak show.\par \par \uc2 Sammy Lee was the first Asian American to win a gold medal for the U.S. in Olympic diving. He had to practice his diving into a sand pit because he was barred from entering the public pools in his neighborhood. So I always use a little bit of this dust.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ (voice-over): Jasmine bakes modern icons, too, like actors Awkwafina, Keanu Reeves and in case you haven`t guessed.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ: Gave him a little definition.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ (voice-over): "Star Trek`s" George Takei.\par \par \uc2 Her sugar cookies are being exhibited at Pittsburgh State Admits (ph) Academy, where Marianne Lyon (ph) directs education on inclusion and global awareness.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ: When you first saw Jasmine`s cookies, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 MARIANNE LYON (PH), EDUCATOR: I thought so brilliant. So creative. So colorful. So everything that I want the world to think and see of us.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ (voice-over): Lyon (ph) said the cookies captivate students.\par \par \uc2 LYON (PH): They sit still for a moment to think, well, but it`s a cookie. It`s about something that`s so serious. And so then, you know, that struggle then starts to make just a little more indentation in the brain.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ (voice-over): Growing up in Los Angeles, as the daughter of Korean immigrants, Jasmine was bullied over race.\par \par \uc2 CHO: Everyone was always throwing so many racial slurs at me. Everything that you can think of like go back to your -- your country.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ (voice-over): That sense of exclusion deepened in school, given the lack of Asian American stories in textbooks. But then a college elective filled the blanks.\par \par \uc2 CHO: I remember even as a kid I thought, why are we only always like 2 percent to 5 percent of America`s population?\par \par \uc2 And then I learned about all these exclusion laws that barred every demographic of Asian people from coming into the country based on race. And I`m like, oh, that`s why.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ (voice-over): On campus at Jasmine`s alma mater, Carlow University, we tested students` knowledge. Most had trouble identifying the portraits.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No. I have no idea.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ (voice-over): But some recognized Jasmine.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Wait. I saw you at a TEDx event.\par \par \uc2 CHO: This is my pride and joy, Grace Lee Boggs, she was a Chinese American activist.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ (voice-over): The university even invited her to create a mural. The significance is not lost on her.\par \par \uc2 CHO: It`s just -- yes. I can`t even --\par \par \uc2 DIAZ: It`s overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 CHO: Yes. I can`t look --\par \par \uc2 DIAZ: You OK?\par \par \uc2 CHO: Yes, no. I haven`t been able to really like even look at this because it -- it means a lot, you know. These are the stories and everything that I really yearned for throughout my life. And then to have the honor of being in the position to actually create it and to create something that`s so everlasting, I`m just very honored.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ (voice-over): For \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING, Adriana Diaz, Pittsburgh.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KING: A big thank you to Jasmine. So many things that we didn`t -- or I didn`t know and that we`re all learning. That`s why these kind of stories are so important. I`m so glad that Adriana did that one for us.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: Yes, the notion that, you know, we have people who contributed to the fabric of our society and we don`t know their names the way we know other names.\par \par \uc2 KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: I like when they said but it`s a cookie. But it`s so serious, but it`s a cookie. In that struggle, is the art.\par \par \uc2 KING: Jasmine making a difference.\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: On today`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING podcast, we are joined by Brett Schulman, co-founder and CEO of the fast casual Mediterranean restaurant company CAVA. He shares how the chain navigated the pandemic. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 KING: I love this song.\par \par \uc2 Elton John spent lockdown making a new album. He enlisted the help of some of his close friends. Some of them are famous, like Miley Cyrus, Stevie Wonder and Nicki Minaj. The album will be called, what else, "The Lockdown Sessions." It will debut on October 22nd.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Elton John is in the middle of his farewell tour. He`s taking three years to say goodbye. I actually want to go to his farewell concert. He was taking three years. So there`s been a pause, of course, because of the pandemic. But now he`s found something else to do while in lockdown, giving us the music. I like it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: Speaking of new sessions, we`re rehearsing the relaunch show.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, you`re excited about your graphic package.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DOKOUPIL: That`s a tease. That`s what`s coming.\par \par \uc2 DUTHIERS: -- package for what to watch when we debut from Times Square. Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 KING: That`s Tuesday. That will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210902eh920002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 48 HOURS: THE SECRETS OF CHAD DAYBELL`S BACKYARD\b0\par\par\uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti; Morgan Loew \par \uc2 6,598 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: 48 Hours Mystery\par \uc2 FTET\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN LOEW, CBS NEWS CONSULTANT (voice-over): This case elicits such an anger.\par \par \uc2 These were two beautiful kids, Tylee and JJ --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: JJ, say hi.\par \par \uc2 LOEW (voice-over): -- who were brutally murdered.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Happy family.\par \par \uc2 LOEW (voice-over): Who would do something like this and why would they do it?\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL, CBS NEWS ANCHOR: A grand jury just indicted a mom and her husband on murder charges for the deaths of her two children, who were found buried in the ...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI, CBS NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Lori Vallow Daybell and her new husband, Chad Daybell, have been called the doomsday couple, monsters, murderers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The penalty for the most serious charges range from life in prison, to the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Prosecutors claim the couple used their belief in doomsday scenarios to justify the killings.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everything that`s happening, it eats at the soul a bit.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): This group says the gruesome case is far from over.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The time has come that we need to share our story.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): After a year of public silence--\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think about it every day.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): -- Chad Daybell`s children are speaking out for the first time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It was just so heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My father needs someone to be a voice for him, to let people know what`s real, what we know.\par \par \uc2 LOEW (voice-over): This became the most famous missing persons case in the country. Tylee Ryan and JJ Vallow disappeared in September of 2019. For months, Lori Vallow Daybell and Chad Daybell, her new husband, refused to answer any questions about where these kids were.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Can you tell me where your kids are?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There`s people around the country praying for your children, praying for you guys.\par \par \uc2 Why don`t you give us answers?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That`s great?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (voice-over): Even after Lori was arrested, she still refused to say anything about where the kids were.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Lori, where are your kids?\par \par \uc2 How come you`re not telling anybody where your kids are?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (voice-over): Then came that shattering news that they had all feared.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Detectives and investigators have recovered what`s believed to be human remains.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: Months after vanishing into thin air, the remains of JJ Vallow and Tylee Ryan were found buried here, in Chad Daybell`s back yard.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): He remains behind bars, charged with murdering them.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: Did your father, Chad Daybell, play any role in killing JJ and Tylee?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s completely unthinkable.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: How is it possible two children are found buried in your father`s back yard and he had nothing to do with it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He was framed.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Up until that terrible day, when authorities swarmed their father`s property, looking for two missing kids, the five Daybell children say they were just an ordinary Mormon family, living outside Rexburg, Idaho. But that has changed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: People are acting like we`ve done horrible crimes and that we are going to hurt people.\par \par \uc2 People sent me \uc2 Facebook\uc2  messages threatening my son.\par \par \uc2 That`s just daily life now.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Their father, Chad Daybell, is charged with one of the most savage crimes to ever befall this community: the murders of his new wife`s two children, 16-year-old Tylee Ryan and 7-year-old JJ Vallow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s just not possible. Anyone who says that my dad could kill a person doesn`t know my dad.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: The evidence doesn`t look good.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think would interpret it as many people have. But we had to remember that this is just one side of the story. And it does look really bad.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Emma, Mark, Leah, Seth and Garth are speaking out together for the first time. They say we`ve only heard one side of the story and they`re here to tell their father`s side, how they believe the remains of Tylee and JJ ended up here in their father` back yard, a story that begins with their stepmother, Lori Vallow.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: Looking back, was your father meeting Lori and marrying her the worst mistake of his life?\par \par \uc2 EMMA MURRY, CHAD`S DAUGHTER: Absolutely. None of this would`ve happened if Lori Vallow had never come into my family`s life.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): The Lori Vallow the world has come to know -- the doomsday mom, who refused to say where her children were, the woman now accused, with Chad Daybell, of murdering her own two kids -- is nothing like the doting mother April Raymond once considered a dear friend.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RAYMOND, LORI VALLOW`S FRIEND: She did everything for JJ, everything for Tylee. They were the center of her world. And so, I`m not sure what hijacked that priority.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): April met Lori in 2015, in Hawaii. At the time, Lori was married to business man Charles Vallow and they were raising the two children, Tylee, Lori`s teenage daughter from a former marriage, and their adopted son, JJ, the grandson of Charles` sister, Kay Woodcock.\par \par \uc2 JJ had autism and Lori was the ideal mother for him, says April.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND: JJ was very difficult to take care of. So I really admired how patient she was with him and how much care she took of him.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): But it was Tylee`s bond with JJ that Tylee`s close friend, Vaisia Itaaehau, will always remember.\par \par \uc2 VAISIA ITAAEHAU, TYLEE RYAN`S FRIEND: I loved being around JJ and Tylee. Tylee was really protective over JJ and she was kind of like another mama to him.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): But around 2017, relatives say Lori`s relationships with her kids began to shift, sometime after she started reading the books of Chad Daybell.\par \par \uc2 CHAD DAYBELL, WRITER: I recently released my autobiography.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Daybell was a professional gravedigger turned novelist and podcaster from Rexburg, Idaho. He wrote religious tales about the destruction of the world.\par \par \uc2 Investigative reporter and CBS News consultant, Morgan Loew:\par \par \uc2 LOEW: He tapped into this prepper element out there, people who wanted to prepare for Armageddon.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Daybell was married to this woman, Tammy, and they were raising five children. Some say, over the years, Chad`s religious beliefs became extreme. He believed he could talk to dead people and see into the future.\par \par \uc2 C. DAYBELL: You`ll know more about my two near death experiences and how that --\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): According to one witness, Chad talked about dark spirits and zombies.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: Chad had this belief that some people who were on this Earth weren`t really the people they were supposed to be. Their souls had left them and evilness took over their bodies. And the only way to release their spirits to heaven was to kill the bodies.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Sometime in 2018, Lori met Chad Daybell, developing what friends say seemed like a cult-like devotion to him.\par \par \uc2 ITAAEHAU: She was a fan of his writing and when she actually got to talk with him, yes, just escalated from there.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Lori started doing podcasts with Chad. Soon, she was talking about dark spirits and zombies and telling friends and family that she had been chosen by God to carry out his mission.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: Lori believed that she was chosen to lead the 144,000 after the apocalypse, to lead the survivors of the human race to an eternal life, while the rest of society burned.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): She also believed she had been appointed another, more ominous role, says April.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND: Part of her on mission on Earth was to eliminate the darkness, the demonic, the evil.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): April says Lori told her one of those evil beings was her then husband, Charles Vallow.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND: The way she had explained it to me about Charles was that Charles was already dead and that there was a demon living inside of him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OFFICER DORENBUSH, RESPONDING POLICE: What did she say yesterday?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES VALLOW, LORI`S HUSBAND: She said, "You`re not Charles. I don`t know who you are, what you did with Charles."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Charles was so spooked by Lori, he went to the police.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DORENBUSH: Why don`t you come over here?\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Their conversation was recorded on the officer`s bodycam.\par \par \uc2 C. VALLOW: She thinks she`s a resurrected being and a -- and a -- a god.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LOEW: He was worried about his safety and the safety of his children.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DORENBUSH: So what makes her a danger to herself and others?\par \par \uc2 C. VALLOW: She`s threatened me, murder, kill me.\par \par \uc2 DORENBUSH: She threatened to murder you?\par \par \uc2 C. VALLOW: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): In the winter of 2019, Charles changed the beneficiary of his $1 million life insurance policy from Lori to his sister, Kay Woodcock, JJ`s biological grandmother. After almost 13 years of marriage, Charles filed for divorce.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 C. VALLOW: She`s unhinged. It scared the crap out of me.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Charles Vallow had good reason to be scared.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DORENBUSH: Why don`t you come over here?\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): By the summer of 2019, Charles Vallow had gone to the police twice, claiming there was something desperately wrong with his estranged wife, Lori Vallow.\par \par \uc2 C. VALLOW: She`s -- psychologically, she`s gone. Something`s happened to her.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): But no one acted on it and Charles Vallow let his guard down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): On the morning of July 11th, Charles went to Lori`s rental home in Chandler, Arizona, to pick up JJ and take him to school.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: He goes into the house. Lori and Tylee and JJ are there and so is Alex Cox.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Alex Cox, Lori`s brother.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: Alex was Lori`s protector. He never hesitated to resort to violence if he believed he was defending his sister.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Within minutes of entering the house that morning, Charles was lying on the floor in a pool of blood, shot twice in the chest by Alex Cox, in what Alex claimed was self-defense.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: 9-1-1, where is your emergency?\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Alex calmly called 9-1-1.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is he breathing?\par \par \uc2 ALEX COX, LORI`S BROTHER: I can`t tell.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LOEW: He said, I need to report a shooting. I -- I shot my brother-in-law in self-defense.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And is he hurt?\par \par \uc2 Or is he alive or --\par \par \uc2 COX: Yes, there`s blood. He`s not moving.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You`re going to press down hard and fast into his chest.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Dispatchers instructed Alex on how to do CPR.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you still doing chest compressions with him?\par \par \uc2 COX: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): When police questioned him, Alex was sitting on the curb outside the home. Alex told them Charles and Lori had gotten into a fight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So you both -- so you get into an argument?\par \par \uc2 What is it over?\par \par \uc2 COX: Well, it was over my sister. He was -- he was getting physical with her and so when --\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Alex told them he got between Charles and Lori and shot Charles dead when Charles charged at him. At some point, Lori and Tylee got into the car and left.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LOEW: Lori takes JJ to school and she comes back later with Tylee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): The police blurred Tylee`s face because she was a minor.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hi, who are -- are you -- ?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE)..\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK. Just stand over there for just a second, guys.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LOEW: What was striking about the footage that we saw was that there`s no remorse. There`s no, "Oh, my gosh, my husband`s dead."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How long have you lived here?\par \par \uc2 LORI VALLOW, JJ AND TYLEE`S MOTHER: Like three weeks.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, geez. Yes, OK.\par \par \uc2 L. VALLOW: That`s why the neighbors don`t know us very well.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 L. VALLOW: Like, "Hi, neighbors; sorry."\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you working at all?\par \par \uc2 L. VALLOW: No.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Lori, Alex and Tylee were questioned at the police headquarters. All told similar stories of self-defense and were sent home.\par \par \uc2 About two months later, Lori and the two kids moved to Rexburg, Idaho, not far from where Chad Daybell lived with his wife, Tammy.\par \par \uc2 Lori`s brother, Alex Cox, the man she considered her protector, moved into this same complex.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: Lori sort of viewed Alex as the destroyer of bad things, in her eyes. He was there to take care of the problems.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): It appears one problem was Tylee Ryan.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: We know that in the spring of 2019, Lori started referring to Tylee as a "zombie" to at least one of her close friends.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): One of those close friends was April Raymond.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND: And she had described Tylee as having a dark spirit.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): These are the last known pictures of Tylee Ryan. On September 8th, she was with Lori, JJ and Alex at Yellowstone National Park. The 16-year old was never seen again. And just two weeks later, JJ Vallow would go missing.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: Tylee was JJ`s protector. Once she was gone, JJ had nobody. He had nobody to protect him or watch out for him.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): The night before JJ vanished, Lori`s good friend, Melanie Gibb, and her boyfriend, David Warwick, were staying at Lori`s townhome. They later testified that they were recording a religious podcast in Lori`s kitchen when Alex Cox appeared.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELANIE GIBB, LORI`S FRIEND: He brought JJ into the house during the middle of the podcast.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How did Alex Cox bring JJ into the house?\par \par \uc2 GIBB: He carried him in and he was asleep.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): This photo of JJ wearing red pajamas was taken that same day. It is the last known photo of him.\par \par \uc2 David testified that Alex put JJ to bed in Lori`s room but the little boy was nowhere to be seen the next morning. Lori told David why.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID WARWICK, MELANIE GIBB`S BOYFRIEND: She said that he was being "a zombie" and climbed up on the cabinets, climbed up on top of the fridge, smashed her picture of Christ down and then climbed up onto the upper cabinets and got between the top of the cabinet and the ceiling.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK. And then, what did she tell you?\par \par \uc2 WARWICK: I asked to see him and she just said that he was out of control, so she had Alex come and get him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): JJ was never seen again. Across town, another family --\par \par \uc2 GARTH DAYBELL, CHAD`S SON: My dad holding me on top of a sign.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): -- was about to get lost in the wreckage.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, so --\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: This is a good one. I mean, they`re all good but--\par \par \uc2 MURRY: Yes, that`s all of us together.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): These are the times that Garth and Emma cherish.\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: So there`s Mom, me, Dad.\par \par \uc2 MURRY: It`s a cute family picture.\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: Life was good. Life was happy.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Life looks a whole lot different now.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: How do you feel as you look through these much happier times?\par \par \uc2 MURRY: There`s been so much sadness, that there`s a part of me that feels broken, like it`ll never be fixed.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Before anyone knew Tylee and JJ were missing, the Daybell children became part of the story, A story that began on the saddest day of their lives, October 19, 2019.\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: I was asleep and I heard a thump and heard my dad yell, "Garth, Garth, come quick."\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Garth found his mother, 49-year-old Tammy Daybell, lying half on the bed, half on the floor.\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: I just ran over and picked her up and put her back on the bed. And I said to my dad, I said, "I think she`s dead."\par \par \uc2 LEAH MURPHY, CHAD`S DAUGHTER: It was just so heartbreaking. It just seemed so unfathomable that it could even happen.\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: And my dad was just pacing back and forth, just saying, "Why?\par \par \uc2 "How could this happen?"\par \par \uc2 Pointing at pictures on the wall.\par \par \uc2 "She can`t be dead," like, "how could this be? What do we do?"\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): When the coroner arrived, the family says she told them it looked like Tammy had died of natural causes. The children, who say their mother was in failing health, did not question it.\par \par \uc2 MURRY: She would get out of breath very quickly and would get very tired. And she started going to bed very early at night.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): The family declined an autopsy, a decision that would later cause some to question how Tammy Daybell really died.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: A lot of people think that your father didn`t want an autopsy because he murdered her and didn`t want to be caught.\par \par \uc2 Do you buy that?\par \par \uc2 MURRY: The narrative is that he was going, "No, no; no autopsy."\par \par \uc2 But he was standing there in complete shock, traumatized, letting us make the decision. If he was trying to hide something, you -- I wouldn`t leave something like that up to my kids if I was trying to hide something.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): But even the kids admit, Lori Vallow was a little harder to explain.\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: She just kind of appeared.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Roughly two weeks after their mother`s death, Chad, ukulele in hand, was celebrating on the beach in Hawaii with his new bride.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: How did you process all of that?\par \par \uc2 MURRY: That was really hard and it really surprised us. And it was clear that he already had an emotional connection with her.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: I know this is a difficult question.\par \par \uc2 But was your father having an affair with Lori while he was married to your mother?\par \par \uc2 MURRY: That depends on your definition of "affair." Emotionally, I would say yes. But physically, I think my father thought he was in the right because, "I haven`t had sex with her, so I`m being faithful."\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: Did it ever cross your mind, even for a moment, that your father killed your mother to clear the way, so that he could marry Lori?\par \par \uc2 MURRY: No.\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: No.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: I think common sense, in a lot of ways, would suggest there could be a motive behind that sudden passing.\par \par \uc2 MURRY: I can see where people would say that. It was a very socially inappropriate choice to make. And socially inappropriate doesn`t mean something bad happened, though.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): But something bad did happen and the world was about to learn about it.\par \par \uc2 On November 26, 2019, soon after the newlyweds returned to Idaho, Rexburg police came knocking on Lori`s door, looking for JJ. His grandmother, Kay Woodcock, had been unable to reach him for several months. Lori told them that JJ was in Arizona with a friend. The police recorded their conversation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Who`s the friend he`s with?\par \par \uc2 L. VALLOW: My friend Melanie.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): That same friend, who staying with Lori the last time JJ was seen, Melanie Gibb, she would testify at Chad`s preliminary hearing that Chad told her not to pick up the phone when the police called and that Lori asked her to lie and tell the police that JJ was staying with her in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How did that make you feel?\par \par \uc2 GIBB: I had a really bad feeling in my stomach and I felt very shooken (sic) up by it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): At first, Melanie Gibb wasn`t forthcoming to the police. But 12 days later, she made a call to Lori and Chad and secretly recorded it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GIBB: Is JJ safe?\par \par \uc2 L. VALLOW: He is safe and happy.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Chad said they couldn`t tell her where JJ was for her own security.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 C. DAYBELL: If you knew, that puts you in danger.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Melanie handed that secret recording to authorities and investigators launched a nationwide search -- but not just for JJ. They would soon learn that his big sister, Tylee, was also missing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A search for two children from Idaho.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Listen up, you may know something.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The children mysteriously disappeared.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): It was huge news, especially to Lori`s new stepchildren.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: When did you know that she had two kids?\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: I first heard of her children when a detective came to my work and asked me about them. I had never heard of them before that point.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: So even after your father weds Lori, you didn`t know that there were two kids in the picture at that point?\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: Did not.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): As the hunt for the children intensified, Lori and Chad again headed to Hawaii, seemingly the only two people in America not concerned about the missing children.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Can you tell me where your kids are?\par \par \uc2 Where are your children?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: When you spoke to your father, hearing the mounting criticism, the ongoing and growing manhunt for them, did you ever say to your father, "Just show the kids?"\par \par \uc2 MURRY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: And what did he say?\par \par \uc2 MURRY: He said, "We can`t, for their safety."\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: What were they afraid of?\par \par \uc2 What was making JJ unsafe?\par \par \uc2 MURRY: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: I don`t know, either.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: You`re telling me, at no point here did you think, oh, god, there`s something bad going on here, we need to get to the bottom of this?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: We, of course, had those thoughts, briefly. But it all came down to our trust in our father. We trusted him and he trusted Lori. And I think that`s really how it played out.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Play out it did. But not the way they wanted.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Please be seated.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Lori was arrested in Hawaii on child abandonment charges and extradited to Rexburg.\par \par \uc2 Chad, the loyal husband, by her side. But now, Chad himself was under suspicion. With the children missing, the police took a closer look at the death of Tammy.\par \par \uc2 Could she have been murdered?\par \par \uc2 Authorities exhumed her body.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: It took us calling our grandfather and having him run to the cemetery to verify that the grave was disturbed. And that was probably one of the hardest things we`ve gone through.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Everything just keeps getting piled on us, it feels like.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): In a truth-is-stranger-than-fiction plot twist, yet another death. Lori`s brother, Alex Cox, suddenly dropped dead, an autopsy determined, of natural causes.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: That was a jaw-dropping moment, because that`s somebody who could answer questions.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): They say dead men don`t talk. But this one did. Alex Cox`s cell phone harbored a tale of horrors, waiting to be discovered in Chad Daybell`s back yard.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): On June 9th, 2020, law enforcement descended upon the once peaceful home and property of Chad Daybell, searching for signs of JJ and Tylee.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: According to the prosecution, Alex Cox`s cell phone data placed him here for two hours the day after Tylee Ryan was last seen. Authorities later discovered her remains buried in this same spot.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): It was an area the Daybell family used as a pet cemetery. It now contained Tylee`s badly burned body. And about 50 yards away, JJ`s remains were found in a shallow grave under a tree. He was in his red pajamas. JJ was also covered head to toe in duct tape, as a detective would later describe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He had duct tape continuously wrapped from elbow, all the way around his arms, over his hands, all the way to his right elbow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LOEW: Why do you bind somebody`s feet and hands and mouth in duct tape?\par \par \uc2 You do it to keep them from yelling or talking.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LOEW (voice-over): These were real kids that had these horrendous things done to them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): While investigators were discovering the brutally murdered bodies, Chad had gone to his car. Later, says Emma, as her father started driving off to meet his attorney, they learned the horrifying news.\par \par \uc2 MURRY: When my dad left, they arrested him. Some police officers told me that they had discovered human remains on the property. I was standing in the doorway of the police car as he was sitting inside, handcuffed.\par \par \uc2 And the first thing he asked me was, "Do you know why they arrested me?"\par \par \uc2 My thought was, "How do you not know?"\par \par \uc2 And I was crying.\par \par \uc2 And I said, "Dad, they found human remains."\par \par \uc2 And his face went white and his eyes went wide. He was shocked.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: When the bodies were found in your father`s back yard, after your father was unable to answer the simple question of "Where are the children?" was that a moment where you questioned your father`s innocence?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Not my father.\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: It goes back to what we know about him as a man, as our father.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: If he didn`t kill them, then why were there bodies found buried in your father`s back yard?\par \par \uc2 MURRY: I don`t why they would be there. But I do know that, if he were to commit a crime, he wouldn`t be foolish enough to put the evidence in his own back yard.\par \par \uc2 This is where Tylee`s body was found, this back here.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: It must be hard for you to come and visit this area.\par \par \uc2 MURRY: I don`t come back here. It`s horrific to even think about.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Emma showed us around the property, believing that the area itself helps explain why her family thinks their father is innocent. For them, the notion that Chad would hide anything in the back yard makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 MURRY: You can look around for miles and miles. There`s not houses and neighbors. You could go down to the river that way. There`s land down there that no one would ever touch.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): And the Daybell family says the fact that the remains were found in shallow graves points fingers away from their father, who had worked as a gravedigger.\par \par \uc2 SETH DAYBELL, CHAD`S SON: He knew how to dig graves and that just doesn`t sound believable to me.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: Saying that somebody`s prior experience as a gravedigger means that they wouldn`t just sloppily bury somebody in their own back yard sort of discounts all of the psychological pressure that might be affecting somebody who`s trying to get rid of bodies.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): But maybe the most damning evidence against Chad are his own words, a text message he sent nine months before the bodies were discovered, to his then wife, Tammy.\par \par \uc2 It read in part, "I spotted a big raccoon along the fence. I hurried and got my gun. One shot did the trick. He is now in our pet cemetery."\par \par \uc2 LOEW: But the fact that Chad sent that text to his wife, it was like he was setting up an excuse.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): It was sent on the day prosecutors believe Tylee was buried, 14 minutes after Alex Cox`s GPS placed him on the Daybell property.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: The timeline is very chilling. Alex Cox there and your father texting your mother, saying that he shot and buried a raccoon around the same time, in the same exact place that Tylee was found. This seems a smoking gun to me.\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: Well, that same day, when I arrived home from work, he said, "Hey, Garth, I got that raccoon we were looking for."\par \par \uc2 We were having a raccoon problem. We`d set out traps. The traps were too small, so the next best option was to shoot them.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): However, investigators found no raccoon. The Daybell family says that`s because there`s another pet cemetery, in a different spot.\par \par \uc2 MURRY: Over there is where our secondary pet cemetery was.\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: We had a new section of our pasture that we had designated for burying animals.\par \par \uc2 MURRY: They didn`t go over to that section at all and search it.\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: Yes. They didn`t touch it during the search.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: If there was a second pet cemetery, I would imagine the defense is going to come up with evidence.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): But the evidence prosecutors had against Chad and Lori was mounting. And, in May of 2021, Chad and Lori were charged with murder in the deaths of JJ and Tylee.\par \par \uc2 MURRY: I do know that my father believes that killing someone, murdering someone, is not what God wants.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): But Chad did believe that some individuals had dark spirits, like Tylee. A local FOX TV station uncovered this email from Chad to Lori, where he graded people, giving Tylee a D as a dark spirit.\par \par \uc2 MURRY: I don`t know what to say. It just seems immature. But I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: When you --\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): They also say there was no talk of zombies or cults.\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: He never said a thing to us about zombies. The first we`d ever heard of it was in a news report.\par \par \uc2 MURRY: I think if he was going to start a cult, we would be the prime members.\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: He would`ve started with us. It just didn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: So you don`t believe that your father radicalized Lori?\par \par \uc2 MURRY: No.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: We really don`t know Lori or what she was thinking. But we do know our dad.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Still, investigative reporter Morgan Loew says the suspicion surrounding Chad is hard to ignore.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: There are a lot of pieces of circumstantial evidence that tie Chad Daybell to these murders. The bodies were found in his back yard; the text message about the raccoon. His wife died under mysterious circumstances. And then he asked Melanie Gibb to not pick up the phone when the police called.\par \par \uc2 Those pieces of evidence are hard to get around.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: But we do want there to be justice for whoever did this horrible crime.\par \par \uc2 S. DAYBELL: We don`t want to seem callous, that we don`t feel sad about JJ and Tylee.\par \par \uc2 MURRY: Because we do.\par \par \uc2 S. DAYBELL: Yes, because we do.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: -- very sad. And we just don`t think it was our father.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): And the family has an alternative theory about what may have happened.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: What do you think really unfolded?\par \par \uc2 MURRY: He was framed. This is his property. If there`s bodies buried here, it would be attributed to him.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: Who framed your father?\par \par \uc2 MURRY: I think it`s pretty clear it was Lori and Alex.\par \par \uc2 Alex came and left for periods of time. We don`t know what exactly he was doing.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: I guess it`s possible that Chad was framed. This is either the least lucky person in the world or this is murder.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): The Daybell children are forever marked by what happened in their back yard.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: What goes on in your mind as you stand here looking at what was the gravesite for JJ?\par \par \uc2 MURRY: It`s horrible. I -- it`s hard to even comprehend. It`s a nightmare for me, every single day, to even think about, lives with so much potential and goodness. And they`re gone. They`re ended.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): But Emma still believes their father, Chad Daybell, had no role in the deaths of JJ and Tylee. She remains convinced that he was unwittingly lured into Lori and Alex`s web of murder and deceit.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: How could he be surrounded by such evil and not know it?\par \par \uc2 MURRY: I`ve wondered the same thing. I think he was fooled in the worst, most deadly way possible.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: If your father had no role whatsoever, why do you think Alex or Lori would kill the children?\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: We`d have to get into their head to know that.\par \par \uc2 MURRY: I think we would all like to know that.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): One theory goes back to the death of Charles Vallow, Lori`s husband when she first met Chad. Remember, Charles was shot dead by Alex Cox, who claimed it was self-defense. After further investigation, police now say that it was murder.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Let me just get your information.\par \par \uc2 How old are you?\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Tylee may have been a witness that day. And it`s possible, says Lori`s longtime friend, April Raymond, that she knew too much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: Why do you think the children were murdered?\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND: I feel like they were a liability. Especially Tylee was a liability for what she knew.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: Tylee is dead. JJ is dead. Even Alex Cox is dead, although investigators say he died of natural causes. You can`t ignore the idea that every witness to what happened to Charles Vallow is now dead, except for Lori Vallow Daybell.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Based in part on this police document, prosecutors in Arizona charged Lori with conspiring with Alex to kill Charles Vallow.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: It`s a compelling case that they have. They have electronic evidence. They have text messages. What they say is that this was a planned event. Lori and Alex Cox planned to kill Charles Vallow.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): One possible motive, says Loew, she wanted Vallow`s million-dollar life insurance policy.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: We know that, shortly after Charles` death, Lori tried to claim that life insurance policy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Chandler police department.\par \par \uc2 Anybody inside?\par \par \uc2 Make yourself known.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Police say Alex shot Charles once in the chest and shot him again while he was helpless on the ground. They also concluded that he waited nearly 45 minutes before calling 9-1-1.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Just keep pushing down hard and fast in the center of his chest.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): And even though Alex claimed to be giving Charles CPR, police say he was only pretending to try to save his life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LOEW: When the first responders arrived on the scene and they started performing CPR, blood started coming out of the wounds. And that told the first responders that nobody had performed CPR before.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He shot the (INAUDIBLE) out of him.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): And authorities say Charles Vallow is not the only murdered spouse in this story.\par \par \uc2 In Idaho, Chad and Lori have been charged with conspiring to murder Tammy Daybell. Though the results of Tammy`s autopsy, performed after she was exhumed, have not been publicly released, Garth Daybell says authorities told him how they believe his mother was killed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: They told me that she`d been asphyxiated.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI: What went on in your mind you have police telling you your mother was asphyxiated?\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: It`s-- it`s hard to process. But I just still -- can`t believe that he would do such a thing.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: My dad loved my mom very, very much. And she loved him. He could never do that.\par \par \uc2 G. DAYBELL: We`ve had several interactions with the police, where they`ve told us things before that turned out to not be true.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): But police are convinced that, together, Chad and Lori were a lethal combination.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: There`re a lot of big questions about this case but the biggest is probably, who was the mastermind here?\par \par \uc2 Was it Chad Daybell, the cult leader, who pulled the strings from the sidelines?\par \par \uc2 Or was it Lori Vallow Daybell, the bombshell, the black widow, who used her charm and sex appeal to get other people to do her bidding?\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): Lori and Chad were scheduled to be tried together. But that is now in question. Just after murder charges were announced, Lori`s case was put on hold. A judge ruled her incompetent to stand trial while she undergoes mental health treatment.\par \par \uc2 LOEW: And we don`t know if she`s ever going to be returned to competency. Chad may go to trial without Lori.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All rise. This court is now in session.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): In August 2021, the State of Idaho announced it will be seeking the death penalty against Chad Daybell.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: On count one of the indictment, Mr. Daybell, how do you plead?\par \par \uc2 C. DAYBELL: Not guilty.\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): He has pleaded not guilty to all counts.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: On count two, how do you please?\par \par \uc2 C. DAYBELL: Not guilty.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: On count three, how do you please?\par \par \uc2 C. DAYBELL: Not guilty.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: On count four, how do you plead?\par \par \uc2 C. DAYBELL: Not guilty.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: On count five, how do you plead?\par \par \uc2 C. DAYBELL: Not guilty.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: On count six, how do you plead?\par \par \uc2 C. DAYBELL: Not guilty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): September marks the second anniversary of Tylee and JJ`s deaths. So many, grief-stricken and angry, await the trials, hoping for justice. But in the end, no verdict can do what everyone wants the most, to bring the children back.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FTET000020210902eh9100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Exclusive Interview With Facebook`s \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\b0\par\par\uc2 GAYLE KING, TONY DOKOUPIL, ANTHONY MASON \par \uc2 1,463 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 August 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING.\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and other social media companies are under growing pressure from the Biden administration to stop the spread of vaccine misinformation. We spoke exclusively with Facebook`s CEO and co-founder, that`s \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , about the platform`s efforts to handle this issue and what is \uc2 Facebook\uc2  doing to protect its own employees amid this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Facebook\uc2  just announced last week, you guys were planning to go back into the office on-- in October, and now you`ve just delayed until January 2022. Tell us why.\par \par \uc2 MARK ZUCKERBERG (Facebook CEO): Yeah, that-- that`s right. I mean, I think the numbers that we`re seeing around this Delta variant with COVID are-- are pretty concerning, right, and they`re growing. And it just underscores I think the importance of, you know, everyone getting vaccinated to-- to help get this under control.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Will you lose your job if you`re not vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 MARK ZUCKERBERG: Well, we`re going to allow people to work remotely. I-- I also think that there are-- are some health exemptions. But overall, we want to make sure that-- that our employee population, especially the people who are in the office, you know, in the open floor plan that we have, are-- are both safe themselves and contributing to a safe community.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And how are you guys, Mark, going to manage it? Because, you know, we`ve seen the honor system does not work.\par \par \uc2 MARK ZUCKERBERG: I think that this is an important question. And, you know, within our-- our employee base, we-- we do require that our employees are honest with us. If they-- if they lie to the company about any number of things, then, you know, that-- that can cause serious issues and-- and we-- we terminate people for that. But I-- I don`t know that there`s any bullet proof solution to this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to talk to you about COVID misinformation on your platform. That`s a really big topic these days. I want to know specifically what you and your team are doing to fight against it.\par \par \uc2 MARK ZUCKERBERG: Sure. This is a very important topic, and-- and you know, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is taking-- I think it`s been an unprecedented effort to help encourage people to get vaccinated, to promote authoritative information about COVID and the vaccines, and to fight and take down misinformation. So, you know, millions of people have taken their first step to getting their vaccine through a vaccine finder tool that we`ve built. At the top of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , we`ve put, over the last year, basically links to this coronavirus information hub, sharing authoritative information from government officials, as well as trusted community leaders. Billions of people have visited it. In terms of fighting misinformation, you know, we`ve taken down more than eighteen million pieces of misinformation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, the numbers you talk about are good. I think everybody agrees that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has done a lot to combat misinformation, but we still don`t know how much misinformation has been shared on your platform, how many people have viewed it, how many people have acted upon that. \uc2 Facebook\uc2  seems very reluctant to share those numbers. How come?\par \par \uc2 MARK ZUCKERBERG: Well, I think to some degree, there are also different definitions that people have over what misinformation is. What a lot of the stuff that`s actually the hardest for us to-- to really address is not what I would call misinformation, but instead, another category that I would call hesitancy. When you were first thinking about getting your vaccine, you-- you probably had questions around, you know, which vaccine is the most effective for you and your family. And-- and some of these times when people ask these questions or-- or is this safe yet, should I wait a while, that`s not misinformation, but it`s-- it`s certainly, you know, contributing to an environment where people are asking questions about the safety of vaccines. I don`t think we should ban it. But I-- I do think that that`s a big part of the discourse at this point, and certainly causes people to ask questions about whether they want to get vaccinated. So from a public health perspective, it may not be the most positive thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I almost think, though, Mark, it`s more than hesitancy discourse. There have been some things that are just blatantly not true. Recent studies by Northwestern and Harvard say those who are most reliant on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  for information have substantially lower vaccination rates than those who rely on other sources. Does that surprise you?\par \par \uc2 MARK ZUCKERBERG: There are people who share misinformation, and that`s why I said that we took down and have taken down more than eighteen million pieces of harmful misinformation about this. So, you know, if you look around the world in different countries, right, different countries I think are doing better and worse on getting their citizens vaccinated. And, you know, the U.S. I think has-- has a-- a specific issue on this. People use \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and-- and social media all across the world. So if this were primarily a question about social media, then I think you would see that being the effect in all of these countries where people use it. But I think that there`s something that`s unique in our ecosystem here, whether it`s, you know, some of the political leaders and-- or some of the media figures, that I think is different than what we`re seeing across a lot of Europe or across a lot of other countries that are leading to-- to higher levels of this. So I don`t think pinning this on social media primarily is-- is accurate.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, since our interview with \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2  released new-- new numbers, rather, on misinformation, in its second quarter community standards enforcement report. Now, they now say more than twenty million pieces of COVID-19 misinformation have been removed since the start of this pandemic through June. In our next hour, we`ll talk to \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 . He will show us how virtual reality could transform the way we work remotely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is that a virtual you, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a virtual me and a virtual Mark--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Virtual Mark.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --which is why he agreed to do this interview, by the way. You know, I give him credit for saying look, I want to talk about this, you can--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --you can talk about anything and, you know, I will-- I will answer the questions as best as I can.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he didn`t sit down to talk about misinformation. But it is still an issue.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you know what, they`re kind of related, because the virtual office may be what we go to if misinformation on vaccines means the virus continues to spread.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, just that study, Tony, from Harvard and Northwestern--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes, very interesting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --that`s-- I think that`s very interesting that the people who rely on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  are-- are less vaccinated than other people. And it`s a very small group of people that are putting out the misinformation--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --but it`s getting out. And I think it`s frustrating that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  doesn`t really give the numbers on that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And, you know, they`re-- they`re such a huge company, they`re so talented, they`re so smart, it seems like we could get that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is also a very old problem, right, that misinformation can travel around the world by the time truth puts on its pants.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Pants.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yep.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All right. Coming up, why a man convicted of murdering an elderly couple in 2013 and sentenced to fifty-two years to life could be released next year.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210821eh8j000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Look At The Future Of The American Workplace\b0\par\par\uc2 GAYLE KING, JERICKA DUNCAN, ANTHONY MASON \par \uc2 2,310 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 July 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good morning to you. Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. We`re going to begin with this, that Delta variant, of course, it continues to spread across the country. So the White House is all set to make some sweeping changes to its COVID policy. Today, President Biden is expected to announce that all federal workers and contractors, that`s about two million people, will have to prove that they`ve been vaccinated against COVID or else have regular COVID testing. The administration is also making indoor masks a requirement at federal buildings and areas of high transmission for both the employees and the visitors, too.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Several major companies are also introducing strict new vaccine policies. \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  are now requiring all U.S.-based employees to be vaccinated if they want to come back to the office unless they cannot do so for medical reasons. \uc2 Netflix\uc2  is also requiring vaccinations for all cast members and on-set crew for their U.S.-based productions. We`re joined now by Nicholas Thompson, the CEO of The Atlantic. Good morning to you, Nicholas. When you hear about \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  making these requirements, do you think other companies will follow suit?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON (The Atlantic CEO): I think a lot of companies are going to follow suit very quickly. A lot of companies are doing this. I think one of the things we`ve seen since the beginning of the pandemic is that the tech companies in general have been ahead of everybody else. They were the first to let employees start working from home. And that`s partly because it`s easier for them to take action and to let all their employees work remotely because they`re just working over computers. So I think we`ll see again what we saw back in March.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: What are some of the legal ramifications you could see with this? As you mentioned, those are tech companies so it`s easy to do that work. But what about other companies that might say you have to get mandated if you want to come back in this building?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: Well, the law is fairly clear that you can mandate your employees get vaccinated as long as you take reasonable action to protect people under the American Disabilities Act or people with religious freedom exemptions. So the law is clear that you can do it. Now at some companies, you might get an employee backlash, you might, in fact, see that at tech companies where they have a lot of libertarians, they come a little bit out of the counterculture. You can see it being a contentious issue, but I think legally you`re allowed to do it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Well, Nick, it`s-- but it`s a very competitive workplace right now. A lot of people are having trouble finding workers. Do you think that could factor in in terms of what they`re going to do?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: Yes and no, right? In certain areas it could be a disadvantage to mandate that your employees get vaccinated because maybe a large pool of the people you want don`t want to get the vaccine. In other areas, it could be an advantage because people will feel a lot safer if they know that their colleagues are vaccinated. I also think that the tech companies are taking a bet that this will make it seem that they have moral leadership and this will help their overall reputations, so they probably think it`s a competitive advantage. But in other areas it could work the other way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Nick, I sure hope other companies follow suit. You know, people say they`re confused, the CDC keeps changing or flip-flopping, and I think they pointed out the other day that the CDC isn`t changing, it`s this variant that has changed, the science has changed, and now we have to act accordingly. So how can companies realistically enforce these policies?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: It`s hard to enforce, right? And what we don`t know from the tech companies is whether they`ll just say employees must promise they`ve been vaccinated, or whether they`ll take a further step and say employees must send in their vaccination cards and we`ll verify them. So we`ll get the details on that. There are certainly ways of enforcing it and mandating it, but you`re right, it is a tricky thing to do. I think you are also right that as the science changes, private companies can take action to lead in the response which is one of the thing we`re seeing here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Nick--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, because employees can say, look, you know, you can`t force me to do this. And I-- I think you`re right, you can`t enforce employees to do-- to that. But I think companies have the right to say I want to keep my-- my employees safe so you can`t work here.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS THOMPSON: Right. And so what \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  are going to do-- and this is partly why they can go first--is that they can say, okay, fine, if you refuse to get vaccinated and you can do your job completely remotely and not get in contact with your other employees, which is true for a fairly significant number of their employees, then they`ll let the employee work at home. So they have an easier time doing it than, say, a company where everybody has to come into the office, and if somebody says then, hey, I refuse to get vaccinated, you then have a really hard choice of whether you let that employee go or change your policies. So it`s easier for tech companies to make that choice right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. Nick Thompson, it certainly is the beginning of a conversation that a lot of companies will be having. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has confirmed that the first plane bringing Afghan interpreters and their families to this country left Afghanistan overnight. It`s part of what`s being called Operation Allies Refuge. The chartered \uc2 Qatar Airways\uc2  flight is carrying more than one hundred people from Kabul to Washington. It will arrive at Dulles International later today. Now this is part of the Biden administration`s effort to relocate Afghan nationals who helped the U.S. during the nearly twenty-year war. Many, as you have heard, have faced death threats from the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  which continues to seize territories. American forces continue their withdrawal which, by the way, is almost complete.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Ahead, how dangerous heat is affecting the Olympic Games in Tokyo with multiple athletes feeling the impact.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s eight-oh-seven, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Pitch Perfect 2)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Oh, no, he didn`t.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The very talented Elizabeth Banks directed and starred in the box office hit Pitch Perfect 2. Well, ahead, she`ll tell us about her new podcast where she discusses everything from relationships to sexuality. Find out what she learned when she interviewed her mom.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1 (Courtesy NBC Olympics): Caeleb Dressel for the United States is going to win gold up there in lane five.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2 (Courtesy NBC Olympics): He did it.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: U.S. swimmer Caeleb Dressel continues to make the case he`s the best in the world. And you can understand why. Dressel set an Olympic record in the one-hundred-meter freestyle, beating the defending champion from Australia by a razor-thin margin. Jamie Yuccas is in Tokyo. Jamie, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS (CBS News National Correspondent): Good morning, Jericka. Razor-thin is right. How close, you ask? Dressel out-touched the gold medalist in Rio, Kyle Chambers, by six hundredths of a second. Believe it or not, it`s actually Dressel`s first individual gold. All three of his previous gold medals have been in team relays. The twenty-four-year-old has often drawn comparisons to Michael Phelps, his teammate at the last summer games in Rio. I spoke with Dressel earlier this month, and he said he`s just trying to make his country proud.\par \par \uc2 CAELEB DRESSEL (Gold Medal Swimmer): Michael was a once in a million years swimmer. Four hundred free relay, I mean, two hundred fly, hundred fly. I`m indifferent about the comparisons. I understand it, but I`m not coming to this meet to count medals. I`m coming to this meet to perform for my country.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And he has performed. Well, the competition has been hot. The actual heat here in Tokyo is nothing to joke about. Number two-ranked Russian tennis player Daniil Medvedev actually had to take two medical time-outs and said he felt like he could literally die from the heat. He ended up finishing the match and winning. But Spanish player Paula Badosa couldn`t finish hers and was taken off the court in a wheelchair because of heatstroke. Now the world`s top tennis player, Novak Djokovic, has been playing professionally for twenty years and said these are the worst daily conditions that he has seen in his entire career. And I can tell you they had to move the time later in the afternoon to try to accommodate and make sure the heat doesn`t bother these players. Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Jamie, I thought they were so worried about the heavy rains. You know, what-- what is the temperature? How hot is it?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: It gets up into the nineties, but the real problem I can tell you is the humidity. It`s like sometimes between seventy and almost ninety percent. So you`re talking about a heat index that can be almost a hundred degrees. And-- and, you know, your cooling system when it gets that humid is way off. So we`ve heard about some of these tri-athletes and runners and other people, they`ve got ice packs, their trainers are giving them popsicles, they`re doing everything. The other problem here in Tokyo is that it just kind of all sits. You know, you`ve got that problem where you have lots of big buildings, the heat traps itself, you`ve got air- conditioning units, that kind of thing. So it just creates this kind of heat bubble over the city.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Humidity is really certainly I think worse than the heat. Jamie, you`re doing a good job considering you can`t really get out there the way you normally like to. And it`s so-- I`m so glad you introduced us to Caeleb--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --that we got a sense of him, because he really is fun to watch. I`m now paying attention to everything that he does because of you.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Well, I-- I don`t know if you guys were able to see yet or not, but he gets very emotional when he talks to his family.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: And I have to tell you, there was a-- there was a tweet that went out from Team USA that said, you know, if you cried, retweet, because he started crying. I have to tell you, my producer, Hannah (ph), and I both were watching, we were crying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE YUCCAS: Very emotional. And as he summed it up, it`s been a very hard year. You know, these athletes have been training under such incredible conditions. And to see them when they win and they get emotional about it, it`s really what the Olympics are all about.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And he`s a newlywed, too. He`s twenty-four. He`s a newlywed. His wife was crying. And she said I`m so proud of you. It`s such a great story. Jamie Yuccas reporting from Tokyo. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 I`ve never covered an Olympics. Have you?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah, I have.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve always wanted to. I-- I never have.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And it-- it must be really frustrating to have to do it this way. I can only imagine. And I`m thinking about that heat. I mean, these are the fittest guys in the world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I mean, for them to be floored by, that tells you how bad it is.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I think about the runners coming up.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: How that`s going to look like.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. Jericka`s a runner, you know, Anthony.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I know that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So she`s paying attention to that part.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I used to.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She knows. She knows.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Back in the day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She really does.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, how a serious racing accident left a former Olympic hopeful with around one hundred and fifty thousand dollars in medical bills. Yowza.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. You know we always thank you for that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210730eh7t000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Kansas City, Missouri, Mayor \uc2 Quinton Lucas\uc2 ; Interview With Former FDA Commissioner \uc2 Scott Gottlieb\uc2 ; Interview With Carol Leonnig and Philip Rucker; Interview With U.S. Secretary of Commerce \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2 ; Interview With U.S. Surgeon General \uc2 Dr. Jerome Adams\uc2 ; \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2  is Interviewed about COVID, Infrastructure and Jobs; \uc2 Quinton Lucas\uc2  is Interviewed on COVID in Missouri; Carol Leonnig and Philip Rucker are Interviewed about their New Book on Trump\b0\par\par\uc2 Elizabeth Palmer, Mark Strassmann, John Dickerson \par \uc2 9,130 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 July 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON, \uc2 CBS\uc2  HOST: I`m John Dickerson in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on FACE THE NATION: COVID`s summer surge threatens America`s recovery and brings fresh urgency to the country`s vaccination push.\par \par \uc2 Along with the heat and the fires and the drought comes a fourth wave of coronavirus. Cases, hospitalizations and deaths are all on the rise nationwide, with the contagious Delta variant responsible for more than 80 percent of new infections.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I know this has gotten a bit politicized, but I hope it is starting to change. It is not about red states or blue states. It is about life and it`s about death.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: But severe illness and deaths are almost entirely among those who are still unvaccinated. What will it take to encourage holdouts to get the shot?\par \par \uc2 We will talk with former Surgeon General \uc2 Dr. Jerome Adams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And the debate over face coverings is back, as some cities reimpose mask mandates. Are they an effective tool against the new viral variants? We will check in with former FDA Commissioner \uc2 Dr. Scott Gottlieb\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We know that our economic recovery hinges on getting the pandemic under control.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: We will talk with Commerce Secretary \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2  about how much of a threat the Delta variant poses to the economy.\par \par \uc2 And we will hear from Kansas City Mayor \uc2 Quinton Lucas\uc2 , whose city is grappling with both COVID and crime. Last year, there were a record number of homicides in Kansas City. This year is on track to be even worse. What can be done to stem the tide?\par \par \uc2 Finally, we will get an eye-opening look at the chaotic final year of the Trump presidency with the authors of a new book, "I Alone Can Fix It, " Carol Leonnig and Philip Rucker.\par \par \uc2 It`s all ahead on FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 The highly contagious Delta variant is driving the country into a fourth wave of the coronavirus pandemic. Infections continue to surge. And with less than half the country fully vaccinated, public officials are urgently trying to convince the hesitant, so that the spike doesn`t drag on into the fall.\par \par \uc2 We begin this morning with Mark Strassmann in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN, CBS NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Unvaccinated America is playing COVID roulette, with a mix of reluctance and defiance.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Unmask our kids!\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: And in places like New Orleans, the dominant Delta variant has pounced.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s more aggressive, it`s deadlier and it is in our community.\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: Nationally, new COVID cases have spiked almost 50 percent, rises in all 50 states and 90 percent of U.S. jurisdictions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If you can, please go out and get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: Ominously, week-to-week COVID hospitalizations and deaths both jumped again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re past the tipping point. We`re in trouble.\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: Trouble because of deserted vaccination clinics like this. Everyone has freedom of choice, even when it becomes your problem.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KAY IVEY\uc2  (R-AL): I have done all I know how to do.\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: Governor Kay Ivey`s problem in Alabama, new cases here up 766 percent in the last month, in a red state with one of America`s lowest vaccination rates.\par \par \uc2 IVEY: Folks supposed to have common sense. But it`s time to start blaming the unvaccinated folks, not the regular folks. It`s the unvaccinated folks that are letting us down.\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: Take Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 Since March 1, 95 percent of all COVID cases, 92 percent of all hospitalizations, and 89 percent of deaths were among unvaccinated or partially vaccinated people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, more afraid than anything, I will put it that way, than I would have been in case if I had gotten -- gotten the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: Vaccination rates in the next few weeks may define this fourth phase of the virus.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Like, how I saw my parents struggling to breathe. It took me to watch my parents suffer.\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: And there`s new vaccine pressure. The \uc2 NFL\uc2  warned this week that outbreaks among unvaccinated players could lead to forfeited games and paychecks.\par \par \uc2 And more universities and employers have started to impose vaccine mandates.\par \par \uc2 GOV. STEVE SISOLAK\uc2  (D-NV): Vaccines are how we end the pandemic, how we protect our communities, and we -- how we retire our masks for good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: But, for now, masking is back, not everywhere, but increasingly recommended, even required.\par \par \uc2 Across the South, school starts in a couple of weeks. And when it does, Atlanta`s system will require students and teachers to wear masks -- John\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Mark Strassmann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  is warning that countries are in the early stages of another global wave of COVID infections and deaths. This comes as demonstrators in France, Italy and Australia took to the streets this weekend, protesting against vaccination passes and new lockdown measures.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Palmer has the latest from London.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER, CBS NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 The idea of vaccine passports is gaining traction in Europe. Six countries now have some version of them. And France has the strictest rules. There, they will be necessary to even enter many public places.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALMER (voice-over): And that kicked off violent demonstrations in Paris. Riot police tussled with protesters on Saturday, which was dramatic, but actually a sideshow, because most people have accepted the new rules.\par \par \uc2 In the line for the Eiffel Tower, some showed bar codes, others hard copies. And there was even on-site COVID testing, so anyone without a passport could get in too.\par \par \uc2 There were also demonstrations this weekend in Australia. Police in Sydney ducked flowerpots hurled by protesters furious about a new lockdown. Australia had kept COVID at bay by closing its borders, but the virus sneaked in anyway. And infections are on the rise.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, thousands of demonstrators hit the streets, but they`re demanding more vaccinations and blame President Jair Bolsonaro`s bungled COVID response for one of the highest death rates anywhere, though, currently, Argentina`s is even higher.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the countries of South America, where it`s now winter, are together losing more people to COVID than anywhere else on Earth. Asia is also hard-hit. The Olympics got under way in Tokyo with virtually no spectators, and dozens of athletes have had to pull out after testing positive.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia is overtaking India as the current Asian hot spot. In Jakarta, families queued in the streets to buy oxygen. And with ICU beds full, victims often fall ill and die at home.\par \par \uc2 But there are stories of remarkable COVID recoveries. How about 102 year old Nikolai Bagayev from Moscow, a World War II veteran who left hospital this week, bursting with pride to have battled the virus and won?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALMER: And there`s some global good news too.\par \par \uc2 Over a quarter of the adult population of this Earth has now had at least one dose of coronavirus vaccine, and, overall, deaths are on their way down -- John.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Liz Palmer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And now we go to former FDA Commissioner \uc2 Dr. Scott Gottlieb\uc2 , who is also on the board of \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 . He joins us from Las Vegas, Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Dr. Gottlieb.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 , FORMER FDA COMMISSIONER: good morning.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: So, let`s start.\par \par \uc2 People are hearing about increasing cases, maybe wearing masks even if you`re vaccinated. There`s also talk of breakthroughs if you`re vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 You always want to start with the risk decisions that people have to make for themselves. So, how should Americans think about what`s happening right now and how they should make their own risk judgments based on what you know?\par \par \uc2 GOTTLIEB: Well, the first thing I would urge Americans to do is to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 We know the vaccines are highly effective, even against this Delta variant. There was data in "The New England Journal of Medicine" out this week showing that the vaccines are 88 percent effective against symptomatic disease. And, certainly, anyone who goes out and gets vaccinated right now is going to have a vaccine that protects them through the fall and the winter.\par \par \uc2 So they`re going to have broad, durable protection from that vaccine. Whether you`re vaccinated or unvaccinated, if you want to add an additional measure of protection, if you`re in a high-prevalence environment where there`s a lot of infection, a mask can still be helpful against this new variant.\par \par \uc2 The physical characteristics of this virus has not changed. The reason it`s more transmissible is that there`s simply more of it. When people get infected, they get more virus, higher viral levels, and they exude more virus, so they`re more contagious.\par \par \uc2 But the characteristics of the virus haven`t changed, as far as we know. So it`s not more airborne. It`s not more likely to be permeable through a mask. So, a mask can still be helpful.\par \par \uc2 I think, though, if you`re going to consider wearing a mask, the quality of the masks does matter. So if you can get your hands on a KN95 mask or an N95 mask, that`s going to afford you a lot more protection.\par \par \uc2 Initially, at the outset of this epidemic, we were encouraging people not to use N95 masks because there weren`t enough of those masks for medical workers. Now there`s plenty of masks. There`s plenty of N95 masks in the system. The Biden administration has done a good job ramping up supply. There`s also KN95 masks available.\par \par \uc2 So I would encourage people to look at the quality of the mask and try to get their hands on a better quality of mask.\par \par \uc2 The final piece of advice I would give and the question I get a lot is, if you are vaccinated, can you still spread the virus? We had pretty good data with the old strains of the coronavirus that, if you were vaccinated, you were far less likely to transmit the virus.\par \par \uc2 That`s probably still true. You`re probably less likely to transmit the virus. But if you are vaccinated and you do -- you do develop an asymptomatic or mildly symptomatic infection, there probably is a higher chance that you can transmit this Delta virus than some of the old strains because there`s just more of this virus.\par \par \uc2 The viral levels are higher earlier in the course of the infection. So if you`re around vulnerable people, if you`re taking care of a newborn or an elderly patient and you`re vaccinated, you don`t feel well, you should probably get yourself checked out, and not assume that you`re impervious to any kind of infection, even if you`re vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: So, when you use the word probably, it seems like the moment we`re in, do we have a handle on this Delta variant in terms of how much there is, and some of the questions you just -- you just raised?\par \par \uc2 Is it a little bit more fuzzy than we might hope?\par \par \uc2 GOTTLIEB: Yes, look, what we know is that the virus levels that you develop early in the course of the infection are significantly higher with this Delta virus than with the old strains. And that`s why people are more contagious. That`s why this is spreading more rapidly.\par \par \uc2 But the other thing we know about this is that those virus levels go up very quickly. And that`s why we think that the virus is more -- it`s easier for this virus to overwhelm the immune system.\par \par \uc2 So, for example, if you have a vaccine that you got seven or eight months ago, and your antibody levels are declining, if you get this new Delta variant, it`s easier for this Delta variant to overwhelm low antibody levels.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why we -- we`re considering whether or not some people might need booster -- boosters to increase their antibody levels. So we know that you have more virus on board earlier in the course of the infection. We know that you`re likely to shed more virus. That makes you more contagious.\par \par \uc2 It also makes it more likely that you`re going to develop an infection from this new variant.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: There was a study done by the COVID-19 Research Consortium that said, basically, we may be in this period we`re in now all the way through into mid-October.\par \par \uc2 The CDC then came out with numbers and said, over the next three weeks, there may be between 90,000 and 800,000 new cases. That seems like a very big range, between 90,000 and 800,000.\par \par \uc2 What`s your sense of how long we`re going to be in this period we`re in?\par \par \uc2 GOTTLIEB: Yes, people are having a hard time modeling this outbreak.\par \par \uc2 And the CDC relies on outside modelers to do their own estimates. And they put out the models that they got from that consensus group. But you`re right. If you look at heading into the week of August 14, the models that the CDC puts out or put out estimated that there`s going to be anywhere from 10,000 infections a day over the course of that week to 100,000 infections a day, which really is an indication that they don`t have a good sense where we`re heading with this outbreak.\par \par \uc2 I believe that there is more virus than we`re picking up right now. There`s probably a lot of people with mild to subclinical infection, since more of the infection is happening in a younger population that`s less likely to become very symptomatic.\par \par \uc2 We`re not doing a lot of testing. More of the testing that we are doing is antigen -- antigen tests that are being done at home and not getting reported. So I think we`re much further into this epidemic than we`re picking up and hopefully further through this epidemic.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the U.K. right now -- and we`re probably about three weeks, maybe four weeks behind the U.K., perhaps a little less than that -- if you look at the U.K., they do in the last seven days appear to be turning a corner. You`re starting to see a downward trajectory on the cases.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s unclear whether that`s going to be sustained. They just lifted a lot of the mitigation that they had in place. But if the U.K. is any guide, we are perhaps further into this epidemic, and hopefully going to turn a corner in the next two or maybe three weeks.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: In hindsight, would it have been better if the CDC tracked those breakthrough cases on vaccines, so that we`d have a clearer picture of those questions you raised, which is how possible is it for a breakthrough and how symptomatic or how much you might be able to spread, even if you`ve got a very mild case once you`ve been vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 GOTTLIEB: I think that`s absolutely the case.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the CDC is only tracking breakthrough infections when people get hospitalized. And so we know the vaccines are very effective at preventing hospitalization. In Florida, which is really the epicenter of the epidemic right now, 95 percent of the people who are hospitalized are people who are not fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 But we need to understand whether or not vaccinated people are developing subclinical and mild infections and whether or not they can spread the virus, because that`s going to inform the kinds of decisions that they make. And we`re just simply not tracking that.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re dependent upon data from other countries like Israel for trying to answer those important questions. It`s a question I get a lot from vaccinating individuals is whether or not they can spread the infection.\par \par \uc2 Again, we know that they were far less likely to spread the infection with the other variants. We don`t know a lot about this Delta. This is a question that can be answered, and CDC should be looking at this.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Finally, in the last 40 seconds we have, here, and those who have kids going back to school, under 12 years old, the vaccine is not available.\par \par \uc2 President Biden said he hoped it would be soon. What`s your sense of when that might be available for those under 12?\par \par \uc2 GOTTLIEB: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , the company I`m on the board of, is one of the companies developing this vaccine.\par \par \uc2 It`s a 10-microgram vaccine, so it`s going to be a lower dose in children under the age of 12 than the 30-microgram vaccine that we have for people who are 12 and above that we`re also using in adults.\par \par \uc2 The clinical data from this trial looking at this 10-microgram vaccine for children ages 5 through 11 should be available in September. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  recently indicated that it`s unlikely to be available before midwinter, because I believe that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is likely to require at least six months of follow-up, four to six months of follow-up of the children in that clinical trial. So, that puts it more in the winter timeframe.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: All right, \uc2 Dr. Scott Gottlieb\uc2 , thanks, as always.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be back. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: We go now to Vice Admiral \uc2 Dr. Jerome Adams\uc2 , the U.S. surgeon general in the Trump administration. He joins us from Indianapolis.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Doctor.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEROME ADAMS\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SURGEON GENERAL: Good morning, John.\par \par \uc2 Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Let`s start with those comments by Governor \uc2 Kay Ivey\uc2  of Alabama, which is a state that`s had very low vaccination rates.\par \par \uc2 I have seen you out talking, trying to cajole, convince people patiently. She obviously took a different approach and hit them right between the eyes. She said: "Folks are supposed to have common sense, but it`s time to start blaming unvaccinated folks, not the regular folks. It`s the unvaccinated folks that are letting us down."\par \par \uc2 How do you think that`s going to go over?\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: Well, I think everyone has a different approach and different levers.\par \par \uc2 I still think there are a lot of people out there who have legitimate questions and who have legitimate barriers. For instance, there are people who have access issues. There are gig economy workers. There are people who are scared that if they take the vaccine and have side effects, they`re not going to be able to go into work the next day and they`re going to miss out on a paycheck or may lose their job.\par \par \uc2 You also asked Dr. Gottlieb, I heard, about kids getting vaccinated. Here`s what worries me, John. We still have no clear timetable on when we can expect \uc2 FDA\uc2  licensure of these vaccines for adults. And a lot of people say that that is still causing their hesitancy, number one.\par \par \uc2 But, number two, I can tell you the quickest way to get people vaccinated is through mandates. And we can`t have mask mandates. We won`t -- you`re hearing this from the military and from other businesses -- until you have full licensure of these vaccines.\par \par \uc2 So if you want to get a bunch of people vaccinated really quickly, get the vaccines licensed and then you`ll see the military make it mandatory. You`ll see businesses make it mandatory.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Let me ask you about the governor`s -- she said that people are letting us down.\par \par \uc2 Can we do just a brief public service announcement here? A lot of people have talked about the vaccine as individual choice. It`s my choice. Nobody else`s business. Others who`ve been vaccinated say, what do I care if people don`t get vaccinated? I`m protected.\par \par \uc2 Can you just remind people why it`s important, beyond just the boundaries of your own body, to get vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: Well, I always say to people, it is your choice, but choices come with consequences to you and to other people.\par \par \uc2 The consequences to other people are that you may put my 11-year-old daughter, who can`t get vaccinated, at risk. You may put my wife, who is going through cancer therapy, at risk, or my mother, who had a stroke last year in the middle of a pandemic, at additional risk, even though both she and my mother are vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 But there`s also real harm to you, because guess what? More mitigation is coming, whether it`s masking or whether it`s closures or whether it`s your kids having to return to virtual learning. That is coming. And it`s coming because this pandemic is spiraling out of control yet again.\par \par \uc2 And it`s spiraling out of control because we don`t have enough people vaccinated. So get vaccinated because it helps your neighbors, but get vaccinated because it`s going to help every single American enjoy the freedoms that we want to return to.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: You wrote a piece this week saying that the CDC should change their guidance on masking, that, even if you`re vaccinated, you should get masked.\par \par \uc2 What made you -- why do you hold that position?\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: Well, we need to learn from what happened in the past.\par \par \uc2 And in the past, I was reflecting back on my own statements about masking. And I wish, in hindsight, I hadn`t been as definitive. I wish I had been humble enough to understand that this is a new virus. I wish that my message had been heard differently.\par \par \uc2 And what I said and what \uc2 Tony Fauci\uc2  really were saying was save the medical masks for the medical workers. But what people heard, what they heard was that masks don`t work, which was not the message that we were trying to send moving forward.\par \par \uc2 The CDC put out new guidance in May. And that guidance, quite frankly, has confused citizens, it`s frustrated businesses and public health officials who I continue to hear from. And it`s been, by any quantification, a failure.\par \par \uc2 Vaccinations are down and cases are up. So it`s time for the CDC, if you don`t want to call it -- if you don`t want to say we`re changing the guidance, let`s call it a clarification. Let`s go out there and clarify that, yes, if you are vaccinated and you`re around other people who were vaccinated, you are still probably safe.\par \par \uc2 And I still, when I`m around vaccinated people, I still don`t wear a mask. But if you`re out in public, if you`re around people who you don`t know whether they`re vaccinated or not, and especially if you`re in a community where prevalence is going up, as Dr. Gottlieb said, it is probably going to be safest for you to mask it whether or not you`re vaccinated or not.\par \par \uc2 And your public health officials, who don`t have a good way of knowing, or your businesses, who don`t have a good way of knowing who`s vaccinated or not, they`re going to find that they have no other choice but to call on more people to mask it.\par \par \uc2 And the CDC needs to give those businesses, those health officials a little bit of cover by clarifying the guidance that they have out there.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: How effective do you think that will be, though?\par \par \uc2 I think of Florida and Governor DeSantis there, who has basically built an argument on pushing back against these mandates. When the CDC suggested masking for those under 12 in schools, Governor DeSantis said, no, we`re not going to do that here.\par \par \uc2 So the CDC can issue guidelines, but there are a lot of people in unvaccinated areas who are only too happy to push against those guidelines.\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: Well, there`s always going to be exceptions.\par \par \uc2 And one of the great things about America is that we have the ability state by state for people to do what they feel is best for their state. But the fact is, for every person you point out like a Governor DeSantis, there are a number of other governors and health officials who say, if you give us cover, we`re going to move in this direction.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Let me...\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: And businesses still will be able to lean on CDC guidance and say we`re doing this because of the CDC guidance, whereas \uc2 Walmart\uc2  said, we have no way of telling who is vaccinated or not, so we`re just going to say no masks for anybody.\par \par \uc2 You have the L.A. County sheriff saying, we`re not going to enforce a mask mandate because CDC guidance says we don`t have to.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got to give those folks some cover.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Doctor, I want to get to the question of overdoses, a record number of overdose deaths last year.\par \par \uc2 This is an issue you`ve worked on that`s very important to you. Why is the overdose number up?\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: John, this is personal to me.\par \par \uc2 As I have talked about before, I have family members, my own brother, who`s suffering from substance use disorder. And, in 2020, we saw the numbers skyrocket. We had a record year in 2019; 70,000 people sadly died of drug overdoses. We blew that away last year because of COVID; 93,000 people died.\par \par \uc2 And we know that both isolation from COVID, stress from COVID, but also cutoff of resources -- 43 percent of people reported decreases in services available through syringe service programs because of the pandemic -- have combined to cause this explosion. And it`s disproportionately impacting black and brown communities, a 20 percent rise in whites, a 30 percent rise in blacks and Hispanics in overdose deaths last year.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: We have 20 seconds.\par \par \uc2 What`s the next step? What can be done?\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: Well, I`m working with the Bipartisan Policy Committee. I want to applaud Congress for combining their mental health and addiction task forces.\par \par \uc2 And we need to really ramp up telehealth services which came about because of COVID, which are really the silver lining here in terms of making services more available. There is hope, but we`ve got to be able to pay attention to it.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: All right, Dr. Adams, I`m sorry. We`ve run out of time.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so very much for being with us today.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be right back in a moment. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: If you`re not able to watch the full FACE THE NATION, you can set your DVR.\par \par \uc2 We`re also available on demand through your cable service. Plus, you can watch us through our \uc2 CBS\uc2  or Paramount+ app.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And we will be right back with Commerce Secretary \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2 , Kansas City, Missouri, Mayor \uc2 Quinton Lucas\uc2 , and authors of "I Alone Can Fix It" Phil Rucker and Carol Leonnig.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to the economy and the impact that the COVID resurgence could have on the recovery.\par \par \uc2 Commerce Secretary \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2  joins us.\par \par \uc2 Good morning and thank you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 GINA RAIMONDO\uc2 , SECRETARY OF COMMERCE: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: You said this week, if you`re asking me, what can we do to get our economy back on track, get vaccinated. Give me your sense -- you talk to businesses all the time, what are some specific ways in which -- I saw that Apple is delaying reopening their -- their headquarters a month because of delta. What are some specific ways that it`s affecting business?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: You know, we -- at this point this is a pandemic of the unvaccinated. You know, 97 percent of people in hospitals are unvaccinated. So what every business leader that I know is just encouraging every employee and every member in their family to get vaccinated so that when they come back to work, a lot of businesses are planning on full back in office post-Labor Day. They`re saying, you want to feel safe. You should feel safe. You should feel comfortable. We want you back in the office collaborating. So that means get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 I don`t sense -- I will say this, you know, we are not where we were a year ago or six months ago by any stretch. We have highly affective vaccines. So the push now is, get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: I want to talk about going back to the workplace in a minute, but tourism is one of the focuses you`ve -- you tried to help the tourism industry that was hurt. There was a big selloff on Monday and a lot of the stocks that were hurt were tourism stocks. You don`t see any issues there with people being nervous about traveling because of this summer surge we`re seeing?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: Again, I just -- it really does come back to making sure everybody`s vaccinated. You know, I think that people are not nervous because they know these vaccines are incredibly affective on -- on the different variants. There is, you know, I think a frustration by those who are vaccinated with those who aren`t vaccinated, saying, it`s so simple, it`s free, it`s easy, limited side effects, let`s get this done.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Speaking of "let`s get this done," there`s an infrastructure bill that`s bouncing around. Where do things stand -- this is the -- in the bipartisan negotiations to get infrastructure passed?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: Very close. We`re in the final strokes. We`re in the final days. We`re optimistic. We are all engaging daily, multiple times a day, with members of the Senate. And we are feeling really good about it.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: So if it gets through the Senate, you get the 10 Republicans you want and the 50 Democrats you want, but \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  has said she`s not going to send a bill over until another piece of legislation passes and then passes the Senate. That seems to add a lot of complexity. You`ve got to do that before you get this infrastructure thing you want.\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: How is that going to work out?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: This -- this is complex, you know. This is unusual and complex. But it`s what Americans want and it`s what Americans deserve.\par \par \uc2 I`ll tell you, when I was governor, if I ever worked on an issue where I had both the Chamber of Commerce and the local AFL/CIO, saying they supported it and get it done, I knew it had to happen because it was the right thing to do. And that`s what we have here.\par \par \uc2 I hear from business leaders every day, get this passed. We need everyone to have broadband. We need improvements in roads and bridges. You hear the same thing from labor and progressives.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, it`s complicated. No, we`re not there yet. But it`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: So -- but the hurtles are not just the Republicans working out on this infrastructure deal. It`s also got to be worked through with a Democratic leader of the House?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: Absolutely. But it -- I know -- I know this, if there`s one person in Washington who knows how to get things done, it`s Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . And she knows this is what the American people want. And so it`s -- I`m not going to pretend. This is not easy. It is complex. But we make progress every day and I -- we feel optimistic.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Let me ask you about the jobs picture. The president was asked in the CNN town hall, a restaurant owner said, I`m having trouble hiring people. And it was really interesting. The president was pretty candid. He said that basically people are looking to make more money and they`re looking to bargain. And he said, so I think your business and the tourist business is really going to be in a bind for a little while.\par \par \uc2 So how`s that going to work out? Wages are going up. People want mow. But if hiring is not happening, those businesses are -- especially the small ones are going to have trouble staying in -- staying afloat.\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: There is some of that to be sure. But the thing that I think we all really have to keep our eye on, in addition to wages, is child care. Like, a lot of people who work in these jobs are women, waitresses, working at a hotel. So, schools have to open. Child care has to come back online. And we have to pass the president`s families plan which provides for child care tax credits, investments in pre-k, investments in home care. Women are in these jobs, and they`re struggling to juggle between taking care of their kids and going to work.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And is that the answer to the small business question, because small businesses say, look, the massive businesses, they can offer child care, they can give inducements to help women back into the workforce, but our small businesses, we can`t do it.\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: Absolutely. Absolutely. That`s why, you know, what the -- what the president`s calling for is every four-year-old in America ought to be able to go to public pre-k. It`s good for the kids. Its good for businesses. You know, the families plan provides for home care.\par \par \uc2 I read a story recently about a woman who had to give up her job as a paramedic because she couldn`t find home care for her autistic son, and she started working for Uber so she could keep her kid in the car with her.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: You mention work places, people coming back to work after Labor Day. Do CEO -- there`s -- you know, it seems to be some tension between CEOs who want people back and surveys which show people want to stay with these hybrid environments.\par \par \uc2 Thinking of child care as a part of that, how do you see that tussle getting worked out?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: We need better, higher-quality, affordable child care. You know, I do hear from businesses all the time that women are turning down promotions. You know, some of their star employees, who are women, they want to get a promotion, and women say, I can`t do it because I have child care needs, or women aren`t coming back into the workforce. We still have 2 million women who fell out of the workforce who haven`t re-entered.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, you talk about infrastructure, and, yes, we need bridges and roads to get to work. Women need child care so that they can get to work and be productive and it needs to be affordable. And so I think this is -- it`s core to our competitiveness, to have a better kind of family care infrastructure in America that`s affordable. That`s affordable.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: All right.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Raimondo, we`re out of time.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: We go now to Kansas City, Missouri, Mayor \uc2 Quinton Lucas\uc2 , who joins us from our \uc2 CBS\uc2  station KCTV in Fairway, Kansas.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Mr. Mayor.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS\uc2  (D), KANSAS CITY, MISSOURI: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: The chief medical officer at the University of Kansas Health System said that they are past the tipping point. We talked last week with the Springfield mayor who said he was asking the governor for extra space to handle the COVID patients because they had already passed the tipping point as well.\par \par \uc2 Is that something you`re considering with the uptick in COVID cases in Kansas City?\par \par \uc2 LUCAS: Absolutely. We continue to work with our hospital leadership and ask our friends in Jefferson City to help us out, not just with outreach on vaccines, but to make sure with it we`re ready to handle any type of crisis that would come to our hospitals. None of us wanted to be in this position. That`s why we have been pushing and clamoring for months for positive messages about vaccines, but certainly now making sure that we`re ready with hospital resources in case we need more and more ICU space to address the current crisis.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: You -- last month you said you were concerned about the inability to break that 40 percent threshold of vaccination. There`s been some help from the Department of Housing and Urban Development, that Health and Human Services have helped in Kansas City to get people vaccinated. But the rates are still low, 38.8 percent of the population has been fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 What`s been the sticking point to improve those numbers?\par \par \uc2 LUCAS: You know, I think they`re painfully low. We have continued to message perhaps to populations that haven`t needed to hear that messaging. We need to make sure that we`re reaching out to young people. Young men of color is a place where we have incredible weakness right now. That`s why we`re not only being as creative as possible in how we reach out through traditional media, but also concerts. We`ll be appearing and giving away tickets at hip-hop concerts, country music concerts, any number of things that say to people that the vaccine is around, the vaccine is safe and it`s important for you to take.\par \par \uc2 But another part of that is that we continue to have to push back against negative messaging. Some of that messaging from people from this state running for \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  that are saying that the federal government`s trying to kick down your door, force you to get a vaccine. What we`re really trying to say is it is safe, this is not compulsion, this is, instead, something that`s important for yourself and your family.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: You`ve talked about compulsion. Over in St. Louis they are reinstating the mask mandate in some instances. Are you rethinking that as the delta variant spreads? And how does the mask mandate question coincide and conflict with the message you`re trying to pass about getting vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 LUCAS: Well, you know, I think every mayor of a major city in America right now is wondering if it`s a time to return to mandates. We had thought at this point thus far that it is not necessary for Kansas City. As the secretary of commerce just noted, 97 percent of the cases that we`re seeing in hospitals are from those who are unvaccinated. So breaking through to that population.\par \par \uc2 And I think when you think about that population, maybe about half of our unvaccinated folks are those that want to fight and think that this was all just fake. But there`s another half of folks that just haven`t done it yet for whatever reason. So we are trying to make sure that we push that message.\par \par \uc2 In terms of compulsion, we are giving more consideration to requiring all of our 5,000 city employees to be vaccinated. I think that is something. I do encourage more American businesses, more American local and state governments to consider that as an important step for how we can show how important it is to our jurisdictions.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: I want to switch to the topic of crime and violence in -- when you came into office, you -- you were -- said you were committed to having fewer than 100 murders in Kansas City. The homicide rate went above that and set records.\par \par \uc2 What`s been the big challenge for you in terms of combating those homicides?\par \par \uc2 LUCAS: You know, we can say it forever, but -- but firearms. Firearms trafficking into our state. Most of the guns used in crimes in Kansas City, indeed all of the guns used in crimes in my city, Chicago, St. Louis, so many others, don`t start out in Kansas City or St. Louis or Chicago. We need an ATF that will work with us in terms of fighting firearms trafficking.\par \par \uc2 But I will not something. In my state, and it`s happening in more American states, red states in the south and the Midwest, they`ve passed new laws that prohibit or make it a crime for our local law enforcement to work with federal agencies on certain issues, particularly as it relates to gun trafficking. We`re running into more and more hurtles in terms of how we fight violent crime in our major cities. These are thing that have not been helpful in turning around this crisis. We will continue to work with our young people and opportunities and everything under the sun, but most of our murders are committed by firearms. We have too many flooding the streets of our city. Too many that are sold illegally in gun shows. And we need to make sure that we limit that.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: The attorney general was in Chicago. They`re focusing on five cities. Kansas City is not one of them. Could you use a little -- a little more federal attention on that issue of gun trafficking?\par \par \uc2 LUCAS: You know, we`ve had a wonderful relationship with the White House so far. I`ve appreciated this administration. And I will note something different than from the last administration. When they`re working with us, they call us in advance. We have a chance to have a long conversations about what our cities need. We`re not just sending federal agencies and federal forces in to say you must take this.\par \par \uc2 And so we will continue to reach out to the White House and many others to make sure that we can have more resources.\par \par \uc2 But back to the point from before, we just want an ATF that`s able to do what it is supposed to do, stopping the illegal trafficking in firearms. We need an \uc2 FBI\uc2  and others that are focused on preventing violent crime. Those are the things that can help us in all of our cities because, look, we`ve had generations of doing the same thing. Traditional enforcement efforts don`t work the same way that actually working with communities, getting the tools of weapons, working with young people are in terms of changing our violent crime numbers in Kansas City.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: One of the ways to -- you`ve talked about in terms of changing those crime statistics is community programs, getting -- particularly with teenagers that were decimated or shut down during COVID. How many of those are coming back online and how much time was lost? How much was harmed that will -- that will last long after the pandemic is gone, in terms of those community efforts to help with teenagers?\par \par \uc2 LUCAS: You know, I don`t think any of us would mislead the viewers in saying that this was not a tough year in terms of losing very important community programs with young people who were excited to be a part of them. Here in Kansas City, we brought back Mayor`s Night Hoops, a basketball program. We`re trying to engage with young people as much as possible.\par \par \uc2 But this is kind of the challenge of COVID-19, which is that, as we have a delta variant rising, as we`re seeing more concerns with community events, particularly indoor events as we, at some point, get to the cold-weather months, we want to make sure we can keep doing them. And so we are bringing them all back. We hope we get to keep having them. And so part of that is getting people vaccinated, including these very young people, so that we can make sure we`re looking out for them.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: All right, Mayor \uc2 Quinton Lucas\uc2 , thank you so much for being with us. Good luck to your community.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be right back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: We turn now to a new book that chronicles Donald Trump`s tumultuous final year in office.\par \par \uc2 Carol Leonnig and Phil Rucker are the authors of "I Alone Can Fix It."\par \par \uc2 Good morning to both of you.\par \par \uc2 Carol, let`s start with you.\par \par \uc2 Your first book ended after the first impeachment. The second book has another impeachment. Is there a through line for President Trump`s administration between those two impeachments?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEONNIG, CO-AUTHOR, "I ALONE CAN FIX IT": Absolutely there is, John.\par \par \uc2 What`s striking about that timeframe is, the president learns, as a result of the first impeachment, that he`s untouchable. There`s no consequence for his behavior. There`s no consequence for stretching a law, bending it, breaking it. You know, in a criminal situation, if he weren`t president, there are a lot of people who would argue he violated the law the first time around and as well the second time around.\par \par \uc2 So as he becomes more emboldened, he rejects the advice of experts. He`s the expert now. He rejects the suggestions of his closest advisors if it`s not what he wants because he knows best in his mind.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, in 2020, it`s no longer a PR spin game. You know, it is actually a crisis that puts all of us at risk. And American lives are lost as a result.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: So he learned exactly the wrong lesson before the pandemic?\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Phil, you -- you both covered this in the daily minutia of -- and its zingyness (ph). Was there anything that you saw as you went over this and looked at it from a slightly higher perspective that comes when you have to sit and write a book?\par \par \uc2 PHILIP RUCKER, CO-AUTHOR, "I ALONE CAN FIX IT": You know, John, when we were covering this day in and day out for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " in real time, we thought we knew it all. We thought we were in the room with our reporting. But it wasn`t until Trump left office and Carol and I did this deeper excavation and sat down with 140, you know, cabinet members and senior administration officials and advisors to get the full story that we learned how much worse it really was. How much -- how close the country came to the brink, not only of chaos, but of democracy falling. The fears that General Milley, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, had that Trump could actually use the military in those final weeks as president to execute a coup to stay in power despite the popular will of the voters to elect \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . It was a harrowing year and an especially harrowing, you know, final few months.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Carol, when -- when the president, after surviving the first impeachment, had this new armor, this new sense of nothing can touch me, how did that affect the people around him? I mean did they -- were they -- were they unable to do anything or did they say, yes, I`m getting behind that armor and we`re going to go do great things?\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: I sort of see the presidency in two halves as a result of the reporting that Phil and I did. That first half, there are a lot of adults, there are guardrails that are holding the president back from some of his worst impulses. And they are not always successful, but largely successful.\par \par \uc2 After that, however, and sadly, in 2020, when real crisis hits, most of those guardrails have been ground through. And a lot of the people around the president know that they`re not going to succeed unless they say yes or unless they nod politely.\par \par \uc2 There`s another thing Phil and I learned that is -- sort of made our jaws drop, which is that we didn`t know this in real time or we would have told the American people, many people who were the most ardent supporters of the president were afraid to counter him because they were afraid who would replace them. Would it be worse? Would there be no bulwark at all against orders to vaccinate people before the vaccine was ready? Would there be loyalists installed at the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  who would do what President Trump wanted all of 2020, which was to sic U.S. active duty military on civil rights protestors during the Black Lives Matters summer.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: That was, Phil, when you interviewed the president afterwards, he said that was his greatest regret, that he didn`t -- well, what -- what did he say about the Black Lives Matter protests?\par \par \uc2 RUCKER: John, actually, he said it was his only regrets from the year 2020. He has no regrets, according to what he told Carol and me, about how he handled the pandemic, about the hundreds of thousands of lives lost on his watch. His one regret was that he didn`t send active U.S. military troops into Portland, into Seattle, into Minneapolis, into Washington to go after the Black Lives Matter protestors. These were people demonstrating injustice in -- in the law enforcement system here in this country, and Trump wanted a show of force. He wanted to send the military into the streets to silence them.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Carol, when you look back at the administration and talk to aides, you get kind of two sorts. There are the ones who say, oh my gosh, it was worse than you thought. And then the other kind who are still loyal. I get e-mails from somebody in the Nixon administration who still -- it`s like it`s 1972.\par \par \uc2 What was the general temperature of the people you talked to who had served in the Trump administration?\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: Almost to a person, even those who were so, so loyal had left with such disappointment and sadness about what happened. The deaths that could have been avoided.\par \par \uc2 I`ll give you an example without naming sources. And again, as you know, we interviewed more than 140 people who were on the front row to this -- this -- this rollicking ride. But we learned in our reporting that former CDC Director \uc2 Robert Redfield\uc2 , his biggest disappointment that he confided to aides was that he could not convince \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to wear a mask. And he knew that not only would it protect the president, it would send a message to the country. And he believes that American lives, thousands of them, could have been saved if he had been successful. He tried convincing the president`s physician, Sean Connelly, you know, please reach out to him, get him to do this. The president resisted. He thought it made him look weak. And Connelly, the doctor, said to Redfield, I`m so sorry, you know what it`s like when you don`t have a -- when you have a patient that doesn`t listen to you.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Phil, what was the governing principle of -- what was the principle that drove \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 RUCKER: It`s really simple. The principle was , how do I better myself and how do I better my political fortunes. That`s what Trump talked about and thought about every minute of every day in making decisions throughout the four years of the presidency, but especially in his final year. As the crisis hit the American people, the pandemic, the economic crisis, the racial justice crisis, he thought every single moment of that time, how do I look better for November 3rd for the election? And he set aside the other concerns. And that`s what was really troubling to the people who served him and confided this in us.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Help me figure out this puzzle, Carol, which is, if that`s the mindset, why isn`t the president talking about vaccines more? He could say, Operation Warp Speed, this is a huge success. Get vaccinated. Why has -- why has he responded, do you think, the way he has?\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: In terms of good governing, sadly, this fits the pattern for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You know, none of his answers are exactly consistent.\par \par \uc2 For example, everyone who served him told us -- well, I should say, everybody with interviewed on this subject told us it would have been so easy for him to protect American public health if he had just listened to his doctors. If he had just said, according to his medical experts, wear the mask, the vaccine works. If he had just continually said that.\par \par \uc2 But now -- but what he was focused on was the daily, hourly, weekly news cycle. I`m going to win the optics war. This was the Trump tool kit that was so inadequate for a real crisis. And what Phil and I were warned about by sources when we wrote the first book, this guy`s tool kit is going to be inadequate.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: We`ve got to wrap this. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: Oh, I`m so sorry.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Got to stop you there. No, we`ve run out of time.\par \par \uc2 Carol, Phil, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 RUCKER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: That`s it for us today. Thank you so much for watching.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be here again next week. Join us then.\par \par \uc2 For FACE THE NATION, I`m John Dickerson.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020210725eh7p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Daniel Pink is Interviewed about Lessons from the Pandemic; Iran Elects new President; \uc2 Fiona Hill\uc2  is Interviewed about the U.S./Russia Summit; Lonnie G. Bunch III is Interviewed about Juneteenth and the \uc2 Smithsonian Institute\uc2 .\b0\par\par\uc2 John Dickerson, Liz Palmer \par \uc2 8,991 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 June 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON, HOST: I`m John Dickerson in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on FACE THE NATION: Back from his trip abroad, President Biden returns home to face familiar problems.\par \par \uc2 The symbolically neutral Switzerland was the site of the first summit between a U.S. president and a Russian leader in almost three years, the scenery spectacular, but the atmosphere chilly. President Biden says he did what he came to do, put Putin on notice about Russia`s escalating aggression, particularly with what is increasingly turning into a new Cold War.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have significant cyber capability, and he knows it. He doesn`t know exactly what it is, but it is significant. If, in fact, they violate these basic norms, we will respond.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: We will talk to the chairman of the House Intelligence Committee, Adam Schiff. And we will hear from \uc2 Fiona Hill\uc2 , the former National Security Council official who was present at the last meeting between U.S. and Russian leaders.\par \par \uc2 Then, as we plunge into summer, the country is reopening, but closing down the virus in some regions remains a struggle.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Unfortunately, cases and hospitalizations are not going down in many places in the lower vaccination rate states.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: With more than 600,000 Americans now dead from COVID-19, new mutations on the rise, and the vaccine rate slowing, what does this mean for the return to normal?\par \par \uc2 We will check in with former FDA Commissioner \uc2 Dr. Scott Gottlieb\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus, is it time to go back to the office, or is remote work here to stay? We will talk with author Daniel Pink.\par \par \uc2 Finally, a look at gathering history as it happens...\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LONNIE BUNCH, SMITHSONIAN INSTITUTION SECRETARY: History is not about yesterday, but it is about today and tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: ... with the head of the Smithsonian Museum`s Lonnie Bunch. He will tell us why it is important to collect now for the sake of later.\par \par \uc2 It is all just ahead on FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We begin today with the chairman of the House Intelligence Committee, Congressman Adam Schiff.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you here in person.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: It`s a rarity, but hopefully one that won`t be so rare in the future.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with cyberattacks.\par \par \uc2 There`s been a lot of cyber activity recently, the \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2 , the largest in the country, the largest meat producer. How good is our intelligence in understanding and knowing the scope of these threats?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Well, our intelligence is very good, but it`s not predictive.\par \par \uc2 So, we seldom have insight about who`s going to attack us and when, when it comes to these ransomware attacks. But we`re pretty good at developing attribution. But, of course, that doesn`t prevent the injury from taking place.\par \par \uc2 I do think that a lot of these hacking groups operating on Russian soil, some of them operating on Chinese or Iranian soil, they have a synergistic relationship with those states, which means we need to hold those states accountable for what these criminal gangs do to attack our industry.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: How well do we really know where these are coming from? Because the relationship that you talk about, that synergistic relationship, might be the pretext for retaliatory action.\par \par \uc2 So, how well do we really know this is emanating from Russia and China?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Well, enough to both take action to disrupt these attacks, disrupt the financing of these attacks and the remuneration that they earn from these ransomware efforts, but also to hold these nations accountable.\par \par \uc2 And I think we need to develop an international rule of the road, where, if a nation doesn`t take action against cyber groups operating on its soil, we hold that nation responsible, which means we sanction that nation, which means we use that nation`s resources to indemnify against any losses.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: So, it`s not credible for the Russians, for President Putin to say, oh, I don`t know where the...\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: No, it`s not at all credible.\par \par \uc2 It`s also not credible for him to suggest that, even if he knew they were operating on his soil, that he was powerless to do something about it. These criminal actors don`t go after the Russian oligarchs. They don`t go after the Russian state or Russian industry because they know they`d end up in a gulag somewhere.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: So they have easier local targets they could hit, but they don`t hit them?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: So, I`m interested in something President Biden said.\par \par \uc2 He asked the Russians to -- in the meeting with President Putin, he asked the Russians to imagine what could happen if a ransomware outfit, as he called it, sitting in Florida or Maine, took action to disable a Russian oil pipeline.\par \par \uc2 It sounded a lot like the president was saying, that`s a nice pipeline you got there. Shame if something should happen to it.\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: I`m not sure that was exactly what he was suggesting.\par \par \uc2 But I do think when he was saying, Putin understands we have tremendous cyber capabilities, he was sending a message: We won`t hesitate to use them if we need to, to protect our industry and to protect our government.\par \par \uc2 And that`s an important message to send. I think a big point of the purpose of this summit was for Biden to say, here are some of my red lines, and don`t expect to get a pass.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: As you look at the entire effort to stop these cyberattacks, you mentioned that we have pretty good ability to tell who did them or to chase after the person who did them.\par \par \uc2 The president has asked companies to harden their targets. Is there a way in which intelligence now has to go on the offense or go on the offense even more than it has in this area? And if the U.S. is on offense in the cyber realm, does that mean we`re entering an age of greater volatility?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: I think we do have to go more on offense.\par \par \uc2 And I think that means that when we identify cyber groups that are working in conjunction with foreign states, that we treat them as an arm of the state and that we use our cyber capability to destroy or disrupt the infrastructure they`re using to raid whatever funds they`re accumulating from these attacks.\par \par \uc2 Does that yield to greater instability? Very possibly, because we would be taking action against foreign parties. But, look, this is happening. And it`s been happening for years and years.\par \par \uc2 And what we have been doing thus far hasn`t worked. One of the hospitals in my district was ransomware-attacked years and years ago. And by then, it was a pretty paltry amount in Bitcoin that they were required to pay.\par \par \uc2 But, since then, the amounts have gone up. Since then, the sophistication of these groups has increased. They`re outsourcing some of their technology. And unless we get more serious, both about our defense, which has to be number one, but also our offense, we`re going to see more of this.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Going back to a conversation we used to talk about, which was another way in which Russia was destabilizing the United States, which was misinformation.\par \par \uc2 We talked a lot about that in the past, but update us on, are they still meddling in our social media, still spreading propaganda? What`s the state of Russian meddling in the propaganda front?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: They are still meddling. They are still trying to sow division and discord.\par \par \uc2 I think that they may have been deterred somewhat from crossing further lines during the last presidential election because they recognized that, were \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to be elected, they would pay a price for it. And they have. But that doesn`t mean that they won`t intervene in the next election.\par \par \uc2 I think that we can expect they will use certain tactics like social media to help favored candidates. But I do think they`ll be a little more risk- averse when it comes to very overt things, like hacking institutions, dumping documents. They have to know that that would prompt a very serious response from this president, unlike what we had over the last four years.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Speaking of elections, there was a new one in Iran, a hard- liner elected.\par \par \uc2 What did you make of that outcome?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: What I thought was most interesting -- I mean, the outcome was predetermined.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: But what struck me was the fact that this was, I think, the lowest turnout in an Iranian presidential election, perhaps in history.\par \par \uc2 Iranians voted with their feet by not showing up at the polls. And millions who did show up to the polls cast white ballots -- that is, they didn`t check off a candidate for president. It was a protest vote to say, you have essentially stripped us of any choice of a more moderate leader, and, instead, it`s just ratifying who the supreme leader wants.\par \par \uc2 So that, to me, was significant. It was a protest vote.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And do you think that will have any impact on our relationships with Iran?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Well, I think that we have a window of opportunity to reenter the JCPOA before Raisi takes office.\par \par \uc2 There are advantages to Iran and to Raisi to having that deal, if it`s going to be made, if the supreme leader is going to bless that, to have that done on Rouhani`s watch, so that, if it goes well, Raisi can take credit, if it goes badly, they can blame the prior regime.\par \par \uc2 So I do think this gives us a window of opportunity now. But, in the future, it looks like hard-line Iranian politics and world positioning for the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: In the last minute or so we have left, I want to ask you about something that affected you personally.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice under the Trump administration subpoenaed your e- mail records. What`s the latest on that?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: This is something I found out from Apple a month ago.\par \par \uc2 And that`s one issue, one I had to hear from Apple, and not the Justice Department, about what had gone on in the last four years. The inspector general is doing an investigation. I talked with the attorney general about going beyond that.\par \par \uc2 I think he really needs to do a wholesale review of all of the politicization of the department over the last four years. What happened to our committee, what happened to members of the press, that`s just a subset.\par \par \uc2 The direct intervention by the president and the attorney general in specific criminal cases implicating the president, like that of Roger Stone, one of his aides whose sentence was reduced before he was pardoned, \uc2 Mike Flynn\uc2 , another presidential national security adviser whose case was made to completely go away, these are gross abuses of the independence of the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 And we don`t know how far they run. And our new attorney general has to find out.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: All right, Congressman Schiff, we`re out of time.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And we`ll be back in 60 seconds with \uc2 Dr. Scott Gottlieb\uc2  and a lot more FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: The U.S. passed a grim milestone this week, 600,000 lives lost due to COVID-19. That`s one death for every 550 Americans.\par \par \uc2 And with the dangerous Delta variant on the rise, experts say getting vaccinated is more important than ever.\par \par \uc2 Our senior national correspondent, Mark Strassmann, reports from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN, CBS NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): No, no, never, vaccine hesitancy, vaccine hostility.\par \par \uc2 On summer`s first day, every state reports plunging rates of first shots, like Louisiana, with America`s second lowest overall vaccination rate.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOHN BEL EDWARDS\uc2  (D-LA): Variants continue to pop up and to spread.\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: The Delta variant now identified in 41 states looks menacing. It`s the most contagious corona strain to date.\par \par \uc2 Sometime next month, the Delta variant could become America`s dominant COVID threat.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER DILLAHA, \uc2 ARKANSAS DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH\uc2 : The variant could win this race if we do not increase the proportion of people in the state who are fully vaccinated more quickly.\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: Especially in states like South Carolina. Its vaccination rate ranks in America`s bottom 10.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Brannon Traxler directs public health here.\par \par \uc2 DR. BRANNON TRAXLER, SOUTH CAROLINA DIRECTOR OF PUBLIC HEALTH: As weather warms up, people may be going inside the air conditioning. That`s why by needs to be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: The Biden administration keeps pushing to make its goal, 70 percent of American adults with at least one shot by July 4.\par \par \uc2 But unless the pace of vaccinations more than doubles every day until then, we`re not going to make it.\par \par \uc2 Another sign of the times, help wanted.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s a nightmare right now.\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: As America reopens, employers can`t find help. America has 9.3 million open jobs right now. One reason? Nearly four million people quit their jobs in April, the highest quit rate in 20 years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s hard to hire people. You can interview people, but they`re not coming back later on.\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: Smaller staffs has meant smaller menus in some restaurants.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Telling a guest that we`re out of chicken, it`s just a sentence I never thought I would say, but it`s -- we legitimately just couldn`t get the chicken.\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: This weekend, eight more states cut off extra federal jobless benefits, hoping to fill some of those open jobs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STRASSMANN: Health officials like to say that COVID should not be about politics, but vaccine resistance is both political and personal.\par \par \uc2 Here in Georgia, the vaccination rate is well below the national average -- and the two groups who most say no to shots, people of color and white Republican men -- John.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Mark Strassmann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to a former FDA Commissioner \uc2 Dr. Scott Gottlieb\uc2 . He is on the board of \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and author of an upcoming book, "Uncontrolled Spread: Why COVID-19 Crushed Us and How We Can Defeat the Next Pandemic."\par \par \uc2 And he joins us from Westport, Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Dr. Gottlieb.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 , FORMER FDA COMMISSIONER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Let`s start with that goal.\par \par \uc2 President Biden said 70 percent of American adults by July 4. He will miss that. Is that important that he missed it? Put that in context for us.\par \par \uc2 GOTTLIEB: Well, look, the administration set a stretch goal, and they`re going to miss it by a little.\par \par \uc2 I think, as a practical matter, from a public health standpoint, it`s not going to have an impact whether we hit 68 percent or 70 percent. The reality is, we are vaccinating a large portion of the American population. We have to remember where we started and where we are right now. It took us a full month to vaccinate -- to fully vaccinate the 1.34 million residents of nursing homes.\par \par \uc2 In the next five months, we vaccinated 300 million people -- we delivered 300 million vaccines. So it`s a substantial achievement. I think we need to think about where we go next. Right now, we are on sort of 1.0 in terms of making vaccine accessible.\par \par \uc2 And the goal was to open up mass vaccination sites, make it accessible in the community. For people who wanted to go get vaccinated, they can go to a point and access a vaccine. Now we need to think about trying to push out the vaccine into community sites, where people could get it delivered to them through a trusted intermediary.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to mean doctor`s offices, schools, places of employment. We need to think about a different vaccine delivery strategy to get the people who are still reluctant or who still face challenges getting into those access sites.\par \par \uc2 I think the vaccine administration is going to decline over the summer as prevalence declines. People aren`t going to be seeking out a vaccine in July and August. But as people contemplate going back to school and back to work in the fall, they will be seeking out vaccines. And I think that`s what we need to think about that 2.0 campaign and a different strategy for delivering vaccine to those who remain unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: So, if the 2.0 campaign has to meet people where they are, if they`ve been reluctant, that`s one part of it.\par \par \uc2 Is another part that might induce people to get a vaccination the Delta variant, and that it appears to be hitting in areas, not surprisingly, where people have not been vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 GOTTLIEB: Yes, I think that that`s right.\par \par \uc2 This variant is probably 40 to 60 percent more effective, more contagious than the 117 variant, that U.K. variant that became prevalent in the United States and caused that surge in the late spring.\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t necessarily appear more pathogenic, meaning more dangerous, but it`s infecting people more easily, and it`s starting to become very prevalent in the U.K. in communities that are unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 So, kids, for example, young people seem to be the population that`s spreading it in the United Kingdom. And when we look across the United States, we see wide variance in terms of vaccination rates. Some states, like Vermont or Connecticut, have very high vaccination rates, above 80 percent.\par \par \uc2 Other states are struggling to get to 50 percent. And when you look at the modeling that`s circulating right now among epidemiologists about what we face in the fall, they are predicting that, in a scenario where we only get to about 75 percent of the eligible population vaccinated and have a 60 percent more transmissible variant, which this new Delta variant may be 60 percent more transmissible than 117, they do show an upsurge of infection and reaching a peak of around 20 percent of the infection that we reached last winter.\par \par \uc2 So, about 20 percent of the peak in January, we will hit in the fall at some point. I think that`s probably an aggressive estimate. I don`t think it`ll be quite that dire. But when you do look at those estimates, you see it varies widely between states.\par \par \uc2 So, Connecticut, for example, where I am, shows no upsurge of infection, but Mississippi, Alabama, Arkansas, Missouri show very substantial upsurges of infections. That`s based entirely on how much population-wide immunity you have based on vaccination.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: So, another reason to get vaccinated might be what we`re learning about the long COVID or the long-term effects of COVID.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned the U.K. and their results with -- or they`re struggling with the Delta variant. There was a U.K. study about brain tissue. What did that show this week?\par \par \uc2 GOTTLIEB: Right.\par \par \uc2 So, the U.K. does very good prospective studies of people. They have a Biobank, where they follow people over time, and they take blood samples and do MRIs. And they basically do that over many years to be able to go back and look and say, were there things that we could have seen in an MRI or a blood test that could have predicted an outcome that someone developed five or 10 years later, a lot like our Framingham Heart Study.\par \par \uc2 So they went back to that Biobank, and they looked at people who had had MRI scans of their brains in the past three years. And they looked at how many developed COVID. And about 300 people, a little over 300 people developed COVID. And when they compared those individuals who developed COVID against matched controls, people who were similar who didn`t develop COVID, they saw a pretty persistent decline in certain brain tissue matter.\par \par \uc2 Certain areas of their brain showed a decline in actual tissue, a shrinkage of parts of their brain. It`s not clear how the virus caused that. It`s not clear if the symptoms of the virus could have caused it or it was a direct effect on the brain. But it`s very concerning, because it does suggest that the virus could be having a direct effect on certain portions of the brain.\par \par \uc2 And the parts of the brain that showed the shrinkage actually are responsible for things like taste and smell and memory, the kinds of conditions, the kinds of complaints that we see COVID patients having after their recovery. They`re still complaining about difficulty with smell or taste or problems with memory.\par \par \uc2 So this is concerning. And I think what it suggests is that the balance of the information that we`re accruing does indicate that COVID is a disease that could create persistent symptoms.\par \par \uc2 There`s this long COVID. Some people clearly have persistent symptoms after recovery. And it does appear to affect the neurological symptom system, both the brain and perhaps the autonomic nervous system. You see people with persistent tachycardias, persistent fast heart rates, for example.\par \par \uc2 That could be explained by some -- some damage to the nervous system. So, this isn`t a benign disease. This is something you want to avoid. And the bottom line is, we have the tools to avoid it through vaccination.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: In the last 40 seconds, another tool that might be online is, the Biden administration announced a $3.2 billion investment in experimental antiviral drugs to treat COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 GOTTLIEB: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 And I think that this could be a real game-changer. This is a virus that we should be able to drug. The machinery that this virus uses to replicate are things that we`ve drugged before. It`s not a -- it`s not a very wily virus. It`s not a virus that should evade our drug development tools.\par \par \uc2 So I think that we will have a drug that inhibits viral replication. \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , the company I`m on the board of, is working on one. Merck is working on another one in advanced development. There`s a number of other companies also engaged in this pursuit.\par \par \uc2 I think we will get a drug that inhibits viral replication that could be taken on an outpatient basis and is basically like a Tamiflu for coronavirus that you could take when you first have symptoms, when you first have a diagnosis to prevent the progression to disease.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: All right. \uc2 Dr. Scott Gottlieb\uc2 , as always, we`re very grateful that you`re here.\par \par \uc2 And stay with us. We`ll have a lot more FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: This summer, American companies and their employees are thinking about and making plans to return to the office.\par \par \uc2 To help us understand what that process might look like, we`re joined by a bestselling author Daniel Pink, who writes about business and human behavior.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL PINK, AUTHOR: Good morning, John.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Dan, I want to start with, it feels like there is kind of a blank sheet of paper that business and employees have after this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you think employers and employees should be thinking about, about the new work world as we come out of the pandemic?\par \par \uc2 PINK: Well, I think the paper is somewhat blank, John, but I think we have some initial scribblings on it.\par \par \uc2 If you think about this, for years, companies said we can`t have widespread remote work. Why? Because the technology won`t work and people will shirk. And then, in about four days, tens of millions of people converted that -- to that way of working and proved that they could be trusted. Productivity went up.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that what we`re going to see coming out of this is not a return to 2019, but actually a fundamentally new configuration of work, even a new configuration of what an office looks like.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: What did we learn about remote work that we were wrong about or that people were wrong about when they previously thought people couldn`t handle it?\par \par \uc2 PINK: We were completely wrong about -- about productivity and about trust.\par \par \uc2 It turns out that most people in the work force, you can trust. And I think that`s an enduring lesson of this. And if we go back, as some CEOs are saying, saying, you know what, it`s been a fun little experiment, everybody, but you better be back in the office or else -- or else you`re going to get fired, I think that is tone-deaf and a massive misreading of both the moment and the market.\par \par \uc2 So we learned about trust. We also learned that some face-to-face interaction is essential, but not all face-to-face interaction is necessary every single day.\par \par \uc2 And I think what we`re going to end up with on the other side of this is an enduring form of hybrid work.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: You mentioned what some CEOs are saying.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , the CEO said: "If you can go to a restaurant in New York City, you can come into the office. And we want you in the office."\par \par \uc2 So, does that -- is that going to stick, or might some people who can work at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  say, I`m going to go somewhere else?\par \par \uc2 PINK: You know, I think that -- John, that kind of comment from the CEO of Morgan Stanley, I think, in honor of Father`s Day, it`s going to earn him a massive eye roll throughout the workplace.\par \par \uc2 First of all, people can be trusted. You have to default to autonomy, so that people can configure the work the way they want to. It`s also, especially for a finance CEO, a massive misreading of the market. What we have now is, we have 5.8 percent unemployment rate, 3 percent unemployment rate for college-educated workers.\par \par \uc2 As your earlier package said, we`ve got the highest quit rate in this entire century. And so when -- I think when a lot of talented workers hear that kind of comment, they`re going to say, OK, boomer, I`m going to go work for Citi, I`m going to go work for some other bank.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: All right, Dan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Hold on just a second. We`re going to take a commercial, and we will be right back, boomer, with Dan Pink.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And we will be right back with a lot more FACE THE NATION, more with Daniel Pink, and a new look at the new president of Iran, plus a conversation with the director of the Smithsonian Institution, Lonnie Bunch.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 And our conversation with author Daniel Pink.\par \par \uc2 So, Dan, we were talking about what was discovered about work during the pandemic for those who could work at home. What -- what`s your read on the essential workers who couldn`t work at home, who had to go into the office?\par \par \uc2 DANIEL PINK, AUTHOR: Yes, I mean, I think we have to really, as a country, both as consumers, as businesses, and as leaders, pay off on that word "essential." I mean we pay lip service to it, but I think that one of the most important things we can do coming out of this pandemic is really focus on the dignity of work. And dignity means decent wages. Dignity means safe conditions. Dignity means an opportunity to grow.\par \par \uc2 And I feel like among the enduring lessons of that, of this pandemic, is that we have a two-tier workplace. And that`s not going to -- that`s -- that`s morally indefensible and it`s also economically unsustainable.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And in who hands do you think that moral obligation rests? I mean we see so much of what`s happening in Washington --\par \par \uc2 PINK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: But do you see any businesses, and is it in their interests to answer some of those issues you say have been raised by the pandemic?\par \par \uc2 PINK: I think that there are some. I think there`s -- I think it`s -- I think it`s a collective issue, John. I don`t think it`s like -- it`s the job of any single institution or any single person. I mean I think part of it comes to us as consumers and as -- you know, for instance, simply treating essential worker well, saying thank you, respecting their work, honoring their work, but that`s not enough. And you have some companies now moving toward higher wages, in part because of labor market pressures. You have some companies, not a lot, moving towards subsidizing daycare because one of the other legacies of the pandemic is that four million American women dropped out of the workplace. We only have about half of them coming back to the workplace right now.\par \par \uc2 So one of the -- one of the interesting things about this moment, and I think we should consider it a moment, is that it reflects a turning of the page of this country. And when we turn the page, we have an opportunity to write the story afresh. And the most important story that we can write in the U.S. workforce is ensuring truly that every job has dignity, decent wages, safe conditions and an opportunity to grow.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: To the extent that workers are moving around, and we hear stories about people just saying, look, I want a better quality of life --\par \par \uc2 PINK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Do American workers have to become more entrepreneurial in selling themselves because they`re, you know, going to be going into this new workforce?\par \par \uc2 PINK: I think that`s a very good point. I think -- I think that, at some level, a lot of the risk and a lot of the responsibility, especially in the last 50 years, has -- has gone from organizations to individuals. We`re now responsible for navigating our careers. We`re increasingly responsible for managing our education and training. Certainly the world of retirement savings has put more of an onus on people. And so -- and so, yes, I -- I do think that it`s smart for most workers to consider themselves, to think of themselves as entrepreneurs, as navigators of their own career.\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s easier for some people, harder for other people, which is why we need to give everybody a platform where they can -- where they can flourish.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: When you and I last spoke, your book, "When: The Scientific Secrets of Perfect Timing" had come out, and it felt like to me, during this pandemic, with so many people working at home, where every day felt like Tuesday, that -- that -- that figuring out, you know, the time you spend during the day was a challenge for all of us.\par \par \uc2 Did you learn anything about timing during this pandemic?\par \par \uc2 PINK: Yes, I mean, I think that one of the -- I think with -- there are two important lessons there. One of them is -- is that structure can be liberating. It sounds like a zen coand (ph) but it ends up being true, that when we have a lot of autonomy and freedom to configure our schedules, it actually helps to have some amount of structure, that structure can liberate us.\par \par \uc2 The other thing, which I think is really urgent when you look at some of the numbers about burnout, even some of the numbers about depression or anxiety, are the importance of breaks. John, we have a huge amount of evidence that -- that breaks should be part of our performance rather than a deviation from our performance.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And --\par \par \uc2 PINK: And so if we start -- if we start encouraging people to take more breaks, I think people will be happier and more productive.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And, Dan, we`ve got to take a break now.\par \par \uc2 Thanks so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: While major world powers are in talks to revive the 2015 nuclear deal with Iran, there`s soon to be a new player in the negotiations. \uc2 Ebrahim Raisi\uc2 , a hardline judge, secured a landslide victory in Iran`s presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Our senior foreign correspondent Elizabeth Palmer reports from Tehran.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER, CBS NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: \uc2 Ebrahim Raisi\uc2  may be a newcomer on the world stage, but inside Iran he`s already a well-known figure.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALMER (voice over): Last night his supporters came out to celebrate his victory. Head of the judiciary and a protege of the supreme leader, Raisi talks the talk of a veteran regime insider.\par \par \uc2 I thank Almighty God for the people`s trust, he said.\par \par \uc2 But it wasn`t so much people`s trust that got him elected, rather a stage- managed process that virtually guaranteed he`d win. It looked legit, with TV ads reassuring voters that polling stations would be COVID safe and candidates` debates. But with the backing of religious authorities, Raisi was a shoo-in.\par \par \uc2 That said, turnout was the lowest in history. Fewer than half of eligible voters cast a ballot.\par \par \uc2 Laila (ph) won`t tell me who she voted for.\par \par \uc2 PALMER (on camera): You won`t tell me?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No.\par \par \uc2 PALMER (voice over): But like so many people, she is fed up with the grind of life under U.S. sanctions and sees better relations with America as a way out.\par \par \uc2 PALMER (on camera): So you would like to see Mr. Biden and Mr. Raisi connect.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why not?\par \par \uc2 PALMER (voice over): Here`s why not. Raisi, who is already under a Trump- era sanction for alleged human rights abuses, is allied with the Revolutionary Guards, enemies of the U.S. and backers of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , designated a terrorist organization by Washington.\par \par \uc2 Also in command of the Iranian navy boats that harass U.S. warships in the Persian Gulf.\par \par \uc2 But there is one area of probable cooperation, a revived nuclear deal with the U.S. and its allies. Raisi has said he will respect it. In part because he`ll benefit politically if Iran`s battered economy gets a boost from the lifting of American sanctions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALMER: Negotiators have been working on the final wording of a deal that the Iran side says could come in a matter of weeks.\par \par \uc2 John.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Liz Palmer in Tehran, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We want to take a closer look at what that U.S./Russia summit accomplished last week. \uc2 Fiona Hill\uc2  was the National Security Council`s senior director for European and Russian affairs during the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 FIONA HILL\uc2 , FORMER NSC SENIOR DIRECTOR FOR EUROPEAN AND RUSSIAN AFFAIRS: Good morning, John.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Dr. Hill, I want to start with news this morning that the -- the U.S. is preparing new sanctions against Russia over the poisoning of \uc2 Alexey Navalny\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What do you make of those sanctions?\par \par \uc2 HILL: Well, those are actually obligatory because Navalny was poisoned, as we all now know, with Novichok, which is a banned nerve agent, essentially an illegal chemical weapon. It`s the same substance that was used in a different form against \uc2 Sergei Skripal\uc2  and his daughter Yulia in Salisbury back in 2018, which some people might recall. And on that same occasion, it also triggered a similar set of sanctions and other responses.\par \par \uc2 The Russians undertook to actually destroy all of their chemical weapons stockpiles, but, of course, as we`ve discovered, they kept a lot of the novel agents. Probably betting on the fact that they were so secret and so highly classified when only western governments knew about them that nobody would ever actually get to the bottom of the use of this substance and certainly wouldn`t divulge it but they were caught out when they used the same thing against Skripal in Salisbury. So, you know, they were at it again.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: So would this have been something that President Biden would have brought up in the meeting with Putin, or is it something kind of, because it`s automatic, kind of is outside of their -- of the exchange of carrots and sticks?\par \par \uc2 HILL: Well, I don`t think it`s outside of the exchange. And President Biden, by his own account, did raise \uc2 Alexey Navalny\uc2  and not just his poisoning but the fact that he is now in jail having returned to Russia after recovering in Germany. And, you know, the Russians have been already put on notice that the death of Navalny would be seen as a really serious event that would basically get some responses as well.\par \par \uc2 So I think, you know, the Russians were certainly forewarned and, you know, probably well aware that these sanctions were coming.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Speaking of forewarnings, President Biden presented President Putin with a list of 16 areas in infrastructure. And he said they were off- limits to cyberattack. And then I was interesting, the president said this, I pointed out to him that we have significant cyber capability and, if, in fact, they violate these basic norms, we will respond with cyber.\par \par \uc2 So was essentially President Biden saying that`s a red line, cross it and we retaliate?\par \par \uc2 HILL: Absolutely he was. And, you know, we`ve made those red lines clear in the past to Russia on a number of fronts, not just in cyber, but also in the military realm. And there have been some read lines that when the Russians have crossed them, we have responded.\par \par \uc2 I mean an important point is to make that response credible. So it`s not so much that we`re telegraphing it publicly, but that, you know, some response happens behind the scenes. There`s a clear message sent to the Russians that they understand this.\par \par \uc2 I think the dilemma is, you know, not being entirely comprehensive in your list of what`s off limits because what the Russians are, they`re very much testing the guardrails at all points, they`re testing the limits. So I think we can also expect that there might be some covert actions that might go beyond those 16 areas that are off limits, perhaps ransomware attacks, criminal attacks, something that`s hard to attribute, and then we`re going to have to go back to them with additional lists of things that are off limits as well. So this is going to be a back and forth, I suspect.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: You -- speaking of covert information, do you think -- since you`re so familiar with these kinds of meetings -- that President Biden would have said to President Putin privately, given him some evidence of what the U.S. knows and proof that the U.S. has, not only about what Russia has been doing, but about how far our reach of retaliation could have been? Privately, would he have shared that kind of information to back up his threats?\par \par \uc2 HILL: I don`t think that he would have said something quite explicit because, look, there`s a real concern that the Russians can get ahead of everything that we`re wanting them to do. I mean that`s why people are very cautious about talking to the media because Putin is the master of preemption. If you think he`s going to do something, he`ll get ahead of it, he`ll head that off or he`ll do something himself that then will require some other different form of retaliation.\par \par \uc2 But, look, our militaries have been very effective at this. We`ve had a long-standing channel between the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, General Milley on our part, before that it was General Dunford, with their counterpart, General Gerasimov. And we`ve been very clear that if something happens in the military realm, we will responds. And that did happen in Syria in 2018 when Russian paramilitaries were pretending to be Syrian rebels and shot on our forces and there were very heavy casualties on their side after there was a U.S. response.\par \par \uc2 That`s the kind of message that you send. You do it privately. It doesn`t have to be the president. It can be at the professional level by intelligence operatives or envoys or, you know, our military officers, and then they see the response and then the message is reinforced.\par \par \uc2 So, again, it`s -- it has to be very carefully calibrated. But I think that President Biden, by sitting opposite Putin, looking him in the face, as he said, and then being very clear about what the red lines are is already setting the tone for next sets of meetings which I would imagine would be at the other professional levels in the U.S. and Russian governments.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: The world -- the word "Cold War" gets thrown around a lot. Can you characterize whether that`s implacable with respect to this cyber dance that is going on or how should we really think about the nature of that conflict?\par \par \uc2 HILL: Well, the cyber war is a hot war, same with the information war, because actions are already happening, right? I mean we know that the Russians have had this massive penetration of our systems, the \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2  hack. We`ve seen that they have interfered in our election by trying to get on to critical systems. We`ve already seen all these ransomware attacks which, you know, many of us suspect have been criminals for hire, or at least have not been reined in by Russia, which is, you know, tantamount also to certainly allowing attacks to take place on -- you know, everything from pipeline to hospitals and other systems.\par \par \uc2 And in terms of information war, I mean we know that the Russians have been out using social media platforms, all kinds of propaganda. So we`re already there. I mean what we`re trying to do is try to rail that back and to basically -- reign that back, that is, and try to get some kind of restraint here.\par \par \uc2 So we`re basically I guess now with the dilemma of, how do we do that? Can we get a comprehensive cyber agreement like we do in the nuclear weapons realm or is that just going to be too difficult?\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: All right, we`ll have to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Hill, thanks so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 HILL: Thanks so much, John. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And we`ll be right back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Late Thursday, President Biden signed a bill that made June 19th a federal holiday. And it went into effect on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Juneteenth is the day in 1865 that the last slaves were notified they had been freed under the Emancipation Proclamation, which had been signed two and a half years earlier.\par \par \uc2 The process of making Juneteenth a holiday followed a similar timeline, long advocated for and privately celebrated, but the final stage was brisk.\par \par \uc2 We asked the secretary of the Smithsonian Institution, Lonnie Bunch, to reflect on the importance of Juneteenth.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LONNIE G. BUNCH III, SECRETARY OF THE SMITHSONIAN INSTITUTION: Juneteenth is really a special moment. It`s a moment that is really the first holiday we have that celebrates freedom. That is really about helping us understand the fragility of freedom, the importance of freedom, and the struggle for freedom.\par \par \uc2 There`s always been this moment. But to suddenly say, this is really about helping a nation remember something that it often doesn`t want to remember, and helping a nation honor those people who were enslaved, but they believed in an America that didn`t believe in them, this is special moment for me. So I`m very emotional about Juneteenth.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: You`ve dealt with the federal government, were you surprised how fast this happened at the end?\par \par \uc2 BUNCH: I`m always surprised when things move quickly with the federal government.\par \par \uc2 I think what it tells us is the impact of the last year, of people recognizing that issues of race are not owned by a single community, but, rather, they shape us all. And I think there was a profound sense that it was important to remember that America, as a nation, has grappled with some of the great -- some of its most difficult moments and has made effective change. So this is both a celebration of change, a celebration of freedom, a celebration of a community and, quite candidly for a historian, it`s a wonderful embrace of the importance of history.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: There is a grappling with how to tell, and how much of the story to tell, about slavery that was knit into the very beginning of the country. How do you look at that debate and where it stands?\par \par \uc2 BUNCH: I believe strongly you cannot understand America without understanding slavery. That our notions of freedom, our notions of liberty are juxtaposed with our notions of enslavement. And so I think that it`s not about pointing blame. It`s not about remembering difficult moments just to hurt. What it is about is to say, a great nation understands itself, understands itself in a complex way. And by not grappling with slavery, we are really not telling not only a true history, but we`re not telling a truer story of ourselves.\par \par \uc2 The debates show that history is not about yesterday, but it`s about today and tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUNCH: And so what I`m grateful for, as an historian, is the opportunity to teach, to share these stories, to share these histories. Because I have to be honest, when you look at the story of slavery, one of the things that always struck me is that there were people in many communities, didn`t want to talk about it, were embarrassed by it. But I`ll tell you, I wish I was as strong as my enslaved ancestors. If I was as strong as they were, what a world we could make. And so I want us to honor that strength by remembering and telling their stories.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Because a truer story is a more nourishing story.\par \par \uc2 BUNCH: A truer story is a story that helps us do a couple things. It helps us as a nation embrace ambiguity. We far too often look for simple answers to complex questions, and slavery is nothing but complex. But it`s also a way to understand notions of where people crossed racial lines to try to help the country live up to its ideals. You have people who sort of struggled for generations but believed that freedom was important.\par \par \uc2 The thing that always -- that always struck me as I did research on slavery is, they`d always say that in the quarters, in the -- in the small cabins, they were always talking about freedom.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUNCH: It really was this sort of thing people believed in when they shouldn`t have believed in it.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Let me move now to the present day. What are the museums here collecting today for tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 BUNCH: What I think is important for the Smithsonian is to recognize that we have to make sure that America remembers. And one of the ways to do that is to collect these stories, to collect these important artifacts, so that maybe we won`t even use them for the next 20 years, but somehow a curator or an historian will be able to really look and any January 6th is important.\par \par \uc2 We recognize the need to have kind of what I call the rapid response team, to really think about, are there issues that are really important that we should collect. So whether it was January 6th, having people out there collecting posters and banners that people put together, but later collecting broken bits of furniture, things that reflected the damage, because I think that`s an important story.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: How fast did that rapid response team get out there?\par \par \uc2 BUNCH: Oh, they were out there within a day, you know.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BUNCH: And so it was really important to do that.\par \par \uc2 And we did that for many other things. And what we were able to do is to bring people together and both collect rapidly, but to collect in a way that was cognizant of the people that we were dealing with.\par \par \uc2 So, for example, we collected a lot of the demonstrations around Black Lives Matter that happened down here in Washington, in Lafayette Square. But rather than just go in and take everything, we worked with the community. We talked to people who were leading these demonstrations and say, we want to make sure we collect stories that reflect your points of view. And so we left things up. We didn`t go in and just take because we want to be a fair partner.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 BUNCH: And then we did the same thing with the pandemic. We knew that this notion of, obviously, America being shut down over a virus is crucially important. I remember, you know, seeing pictures of the flu epidemic in 1918 and thinking, we need to make sure we document that. And so the brilliant staff collected amazing things. The first vial where the vaccine was given in New York City. They have that. One of the early nurses who got one of the early shots, we have her scrubs and her vaccine card. And Dr. Fauci gave us sort of a model of the coronavirus that he used.\par \par \uc2 So the key is to think about, how do you document today so that people who don`t know this story will be able to understand it a generation, two or three generations from now.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Is the goal not just to get an artifact, but to curate something that has embedded in it the meaning of thing itself?\par \par \uc2 BUNCH: Artifacts don`t speak by themselves. And so if you collect the stories, if you work with community to understand why certain posters were so important to people, I think that gives you a richness that allows you to engage people, to bring history to life. We need to make sure that we`re telling stories from multiple points of view.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Now, it used to be with historians that you had to wait 50 years. \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  used to say, I don`t care, history will figure it all out after I`m dead.\par \par \uc2 What happened to that view of history or where does that fit in a world of rapid response museum collectors?\par \par \uc2 BUNCH: I was trained that way. You know, you don`t have to (INAUDIBLE) unless it happened 50 years ago. I became a better historian when I began to talk to the living community. And so it made me realize that if we collected things today and talked to the community, there`s a richness that that has, there`s documents, there`s facts that that has. So while I think there is something very powerful about having distance, I think it`s also important to make sure that you have the stuff, you`ve collected the oral interviews that somebody then could use 50 years from now with their own distance. But, for me, it`s about making sure we collect today for tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Has something changed about collection? Because we now have, in our hands, in these smartphones, that are turning us all into curators and collectors, I think particularly, of course, of George Floyd, the video of that.\par \par \uc2 BUNCH: Right.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: How does that shape and change the job of the museum?\par \par \uc2 BUNCH: It -- it makes you realize that there are a lot of historians out there. There are a lot of people that can capture images, that can help us think about things in different ways. And I think this new technology has challenged us to be more flexible, to be more nimble, but it also allowed us to have more interaction with a variety of communities because, you know, they`re not thinking about how this is going to be written in 50 years, they`re thinking about what happened right that moment. And it`s powerful to have that.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Bunch, thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 BUNCH: Thank you. My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: You can watch our full interview with Secretary Bunch on facethenation.com.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: That`s it for us today. Happy Father`s Day to all the fathers and there and the grateful children making them dinner. We`ll see you next week.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020210620eh6k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Businesses Reimagine The Post-Pandemic Workplace\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Mola Lenghi \par \uc2 639 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 June 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And we`re going to turn now to the reopening of America. As the COVID pandemic recedes, workers are being asked to return to their offices. But this is also raising a slew of questions and concerns about everything from work schedules to vaccine mandates. Here`s CBS` Mola Lenghi.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI (CBS News Correspondent): Essential workers never left. The service industry has slowly returned. And now, with more than forty percent of Americans fully vaccinated, it`s the office employees` turn to go back.\par \par \uc2 NEIL BLUMENTHAL (\uc2 Warby Parker\uc2  Co-CEO): Our office is open, and we expect mandatory return after Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: Neil Blumenthal is co-CEO of eyeglass retailer \uc2 Warby Parker\uc2 , which employs about three thousand people nationwide. He says the pandemic has forced companies to be flexible, like providing more opportunities to work from home.\par \par \uc2 NEIL BLUMENTHAL: I think what COVID did was enable executives to look back and say, hey, do all these policies really make sense? Is it going to ensure productivity? Is it going to ensure engagement?\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: A new survey of business leaders obtained exclusively by CBS News finds twenty-seven percent of their workforce will work remotely. Thirty-eight percent will work at a physical location, and twenty-nine percent will go hybrid, alternating between both. While some may expect a new, reimagined workplace when they return, it may be more familiar than you think.\par \par \uc2 What about the COVID-specific changes that people will see in the workplace?\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY C. TAYLOR\uc2  JUNIOR (Society for Human Resource Management President and CEO): Employees are ready to get rid of the mask. People will respect distance a little bit more. We are absolutely not going to be as underneath each other as fellow human beings as we were pre-pandemic. Other than that, and the flexibility that we`ve come to see and come to expect, I think things are going to be a lot like they were pre-pandemic.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: But a new question companies are wrestling with is whether to require employees to be vaccinated. The U.S. Equal Employment Commission says employers have the legal right to do so, but employees can claim medical or religious exemptions. Potentially, creating new tension in the workplace after a stressful year Zooming from home.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: Well, companies that require their employees to get vaccinated could expose themselves to lawsuits, like the one filed by one hundred seventeen employees against Houston Methodist Hospital. The hospital insists it`s simply trying to protect its patients. Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: All right. Mola Lenghi, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And there is still much more ahead on tonight`s CBS EVENING NEWS, including the mystery behind the fire that destroyed the largest ship in Iran`s navy.\par \par \uc2 And a huge announcement from the legendary Coach K shakes up college basketball.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, a mystery in the Middle East. Iran`s largest navy ship caught fire and sank today in the Gulf of Oman. It is not clear what caused it to go up in flames. Photos on Iranian social media showed sailors evacuating the ship as it burned behind them. The ship was more than forty years old.\par \par \uc2 All right. Tonight, there`s a major changing of the guard in college basketball. Duke Coach Mike Krzyzewski is retiring after next season, his forty-second with the Blue Devils. Coach K has won five national championships and more games than any coach in Division One men`s basketball history. He`s expected to be replaced by associate head coach, Jon Scheyer.\par \par \uc2 All right. Up next, the teacher with big shoes to fill and how those shoes saved the day for a graduate.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020210603eh62000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The TSA Says It Counted More Than 7 Million Air Travelers Between Thursday And Sunday, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Mola Lenghi \par \uc2 1,051 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 June 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The TSA says it counted more than seven million air travelers between Thursday and Sunday, a huge jump over last year, but still twenty-four percent less than Memorial Day 2019 before the pandemic. Even so, many businesses are scrambling to meet expected demand when the summer gets really busy. Mola Lenghi is at Point Pleasant Beach, that`s in New Jersey. Mola, good morning to you on the boardwalk.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI (CBS News Correspondent): Well, good morning, Tony. It was a busy holiday weekend here on the Jersey shore for businesses. You know, folks are still waking up right now, so things are getting in the gear. But this boardwalk was bustling over Memorial Day. But still, we spoke to one family business who says they can`t quite get back to normal because they can`t find enough employees to hire.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: After a difficult year, crowds on the Jersey Shore are a welcome sight for local businesses like restaurants and hotels, hoping to maximize the summer season.\par \par \uc2 This has maybe been the toughest start to a-- to a season?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RANURO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: Christina Ranuro and her family have owned and operated the White Sands Resort and Spa in Point Pleasant Beach, New Jersey, for thirty- five years. They typically hire about a hundred and fifty seasonal employees to work at the resort and restaurants. But this year, they are having trouble.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RANURO: We really have only a third of what we normally hired.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: A third?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RANURO: A third. We`re really, really short-handed.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: Everyone, including Ranuro is working double time. She says she`s posted "help wanted" ads all over the web and has increased the starting wage from twelve dollars an hour to fifteen dollars an hour. But there are few applicants.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RANURO: We`re at hundred percent. We have waiting lists for the first time in ten years. We just don`t have the staff to turn the rooms over as fast as we would like to.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: And they are not alone. Businesses across the country are desperately looking for workers. From this bakery in Philadelphia--\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I don`t have help. I had help, they went on to other jobs.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: --to this Los Angeles restaurant.\par \par \uc2 FRANCES TARIO (Restaurant Owner): It`s been a-- a tough ride. It`s been difficult.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE SIEKERKA (New Jersey Business and Industry Association President and CEO): We have an unprecedented hiring crisis going on right now.\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: New Jersey Business and Industry Association President and CEO Michele Siekerka says the challenging job market has been a perfect storm, people struggling with child care, foreign students unable to get work visas and entry-level seasonal work not paying as well as unemployment benefits.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE SIEKERKA: They are pulling out all of the stops. We have sign-up bonuses. We have, you know, free food, free entertainment, a lot of incentive going on.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MOLA LENGHI: Because they are short-staffed, so many of these businesses have had to make adjustments. The White Sands Resort where we visited is closing off some of their rooms at the moment. They are closing the restaurant for a couple of days a week, so missing out on some potential revenue there. But still, Anthony, they say they are hopeful for the summer.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I hope so, too. Mola in Point Pleasant Beach, New Jersey. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of people gathered in Tulsa, Oklahoma, last night to mark the one hundredth anniversary of the 1921 massacre that killed and injured hundreds of people. Devastated that city`s black community and then was hushed up for decades. President Biden will travel to Tulsa later today. He`s expected to unveil a series of initiatives to help communities of color throughout the U.S. Omar Villafranca is in Tulsa. Omar, good morning.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (CBS News Correspondent): Good morning. I`m standing in front of the Greenwood Cultural Center in the area that`s called Black Wall Street. And President Biden will visit here. He`ll be the first President to come here and remember the victims of the Tulsa Race Massacre. He`s going to meet with some of the survivors at the cultural center behind me, but he`s also expected to talk about some new policies. The President is expected to announce actions such as using federal purchasing power to contract with small, disadvantaged businesses and new fair housing rules to combat racial inequality. And also a ten-billion-dollar community revitalization fund aimed at supporting underdeveloped communities like those here in Greenwood. Now, the administration says those efforts will help communities of color. But people here in Tulsa say they`ve already lost so much, even though the massacre happened one hundred years ago. Listen to what Viola Fletcher had to say. She`s one hundred and seven. Listen to her conversation with Gayle on our Tulsa Race Massacre special last night.\par \par \uc2 VIOLA FLETCHER (Tulsa Race Massacre Survivor): Good education and a nice home, and-- and a quiet neighborhood and family, that-- all of that was taken. There`s so many things, you know, that-- that I`m still living without now.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: And she says she is still afraid after what she witnessed one hundred years ago. Now earlier, she actually went and testified before Congress, talking about reparations for the community here. But it`s worth noting that President Biden is not expected to address reparations today. Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. She was six years old at the time. She`s told me that she thinks about it every single day, imagine that. And she`s now a hundred and seven. Thank you, Omar.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210602eh6100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 For The First Time Joe Biden Has Expressed His Support For A Ceasefire In The Israeli-Palestinian Conflict, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Anthony Mason, Imtiaz Tyab \par \uc2 950 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All right. We begin this morning in the Middle East, though. And for the first time, President Biden has expressed support for a ceasefire in the Israeli-Palestinian conflict. But the violence shows no signs of letting up. Early this morning, Israeli forces launched a series of new strikes on targets in Gaza. That follows another night of heavy firing from both sides. More than two hundred Palestinians have been killed since the fighting started, including sixty-one children. Ten people, including two children have been killed in Israel. Imtiaz Tyab is on the southern Israeli border. Imtiaz, good morning.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB (CBS News Foreign Correspondent): Anthony, everyone, good morning. That`s right, I`m here in southern Israel. Well, we were brought by the Israeli military. And this, of course, is an area which has really suffered the brunt of Hamas` rocket strikes. Now, the Israeli military has showed us what`s known as the Iron Dome, which is a missile defense system. But first, we start in Gaza, and a warning, some of these images are hard to watch.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Mohammed al-Hadidi speaking foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: It`s a conflict he has no way of understanding but will bear the scars for the rest of his life. Five-month-old Omar is cradled by his father, Mohammed al-Hadidi, the only family he has left after his mother and four brothers were killed in an Israeli bombing. Pulled from the rubble by rescuers, his tiny body is covered in injuries including a broken leg. Al-Hadidi returns to the wreckage of what`s left of his home.\par \par \uc2 (Mohammed al-Hadidi speaking foreign language)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: He says, "Israeli warplanes targeted my family without warning, without calling them. With no regard for humanity or babies." Across Gaza and the scale of the devastation caused by Israel`s bombing campaign is hard to fathom. Neighborhoods in ruins, hospitals at breaking point and vital infrastructure like roads, water treatment plants and telecoms are on the brink. According to United Nations, Gaza`s only power plant will likely run out of fuel later today, potentially plunging the Palestinian territory into darkness. It`s an uncertainty that`s only bringing more misery to an already desperate situation. In Israel, and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  rockets continue to rain down, badly damaging this street in the southern town of Ashdod, injuring at least three people. More than three thousand \uc2 Hamas\uc2  rockets have been fired into Israel since fighting began over a week ago. The vast majority have been intercepted by the Iron Dome missile defense system. The Israeli military took us to see the multibillion-dollar technology, which is partially funded by U.S. taxpayers. Brigadier General Doron says, without it, Israel would have suffered far more casualties.\par \par \uc2 DORON GAVISH: This is really a life-saving system. What we`re doing here is-- is purely saving lives of Israelis.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: But in Gaza, there are no such defenses.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Now earlier this morning, Israel has allowed some humanitarian aid and some aid workers into the Gaza Strip, a move that`s being welcomed by the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Unbearably heartbreaking. Imtiaz, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The reasons for this latest round of violence are complicated. But one of the many factors is the situation in East Jerusalem, and especially in area called Sheikh Jarrah. Before the Israeli state was founded both Muslims and Jews lived in the neighborhood. But it has been a long source of dispute. In 1956, Palestinian refugees moved there while East Jerusalem was still under Jordanian control. In 1967, Israel occupied the area after the six- day war. In 1972, Jewish settlers began challenging Palestinian claims of the land, launching a decades-long legal battle, and for at least thirteen years now. Palestinians have been fighting eviction. The issue came to a head with the recent court decision which could force eight Palestinian families to move. U.N. Commission for Human Rights calls this a potential war crime. Israeli officials have called it a real estate dispute. Since Israel was founded in 1948, wars in the building of settlements have led to the displacement of some five million Palestinians, that`s according to \uc2 U.N\uc2 . estimates. And, of course, among Israelis there is a robust debate about whether the actions of the government have been just.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m so glad you did that explainer. It`s so much bigger, of course, than the real estate dispute, that you just see all these innocent people dying on both sides, very, very difficult.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, I covered the region--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So ongoing--\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --you have Anthony--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --it`s a complicated situation. It has been--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is complicated.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --for many, many years, I think everybody`s hope is that this will come to some resolve. There will be a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And we won`t have to see images like little five-month- old Omar.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And end soon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very tough.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210519eh5i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Neel Kashkari\uc2  is Interviewed about the Jobs Report and the Economy; \uc2 Rep. Adam Kinzinger\uc2  (R-IL) is Interviewed about the Republican Party; India Struggles with COVID; Michael Lewis is Interviewed about his New Book on the Pandemic.\b0\par\par\uc2 John Dickerson, Chris Livesay \par \uc2 8,888 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON, HOST: I`m John Dickerson in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on FACE THE NATION: A surprisingly weak jobs report makes it clear we`re still digging our economy out of the deep hole created by COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 Experts were predicting a flood of new jobs in April, one million or more. When the numbers came in, they were a dismal quarter of that, just 266,000.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We knew this wouldn`t be a sprint. It would be a marathon. We`re still digging out of an economic collapse that cost us 22 million jobs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: But with some sectors of the economy desperate for new hires...\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our restaurants are getting busier, and we`re finding it really harder to find staff.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There is definitely a glut of jobs and a lack of hands to fill positions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: ... why are Americans still struggling to get back into it`s workplace? And can the government speed things up?\par \par \uc2 We will talk with Commerce Secretary Gina Raimondo and \uc2 Neel Kashkari\uc2 , the president of the Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 And, as we slowly turn the corner in the coronavirus pandemic, should public health policy be moving the country to return to normal more quickly? We will talk with former FDA Commissioner \uc2 Dr. Scott Gottlieb\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus, with the Republican Party in fresh turmoil over former President Trump and the big lie about the 2020 election, will believers be able to roll over the nonbelievers? We will talk with Illinois Republican Congressman \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We will also hear from author Michael Lewis. His new book is "The Premonition: A Pandemic Story."\par \par \uc2 And, finally, our tribute to a very special group of moms.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just a head on FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to FACE THE NATION. Margaret is on maternity leave.\par \par \uc2 The country appears to finally be turning the corner on the pandemic and there is good news to report. One-third of American adults have been fully vaccinated. Children between 12 and 16 will likely be able to receive the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine soon. And there are drops in every way we measure the virus, new cases, hospitalizations and deaths.\par \par \uc2 But we`re still having trouble rounding the bend with the economy, as that disappointing jobs report made for sobering headlines Saturday, and reminded us that the road to recovery is going to be a long and winding one.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got a lot to get to today.\par \par \uc2 And we`re going to begin with former FDA Commissioner \uc2 Dr. Scott Gottlieb\uc2 . He sits on the board of \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 . And he joins us from Westport, Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Dr. Gottlieb.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 , FORMER FDA COMMISSIONER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: So, I -- every time we talk, we talk about where things are, and then you kind of tell us where we`re going to be.\par \par \uc2 A few weeks ago, you were saying they should change the mask restrictions for outdoors if you`re vaccinated. Your feeling now about maybe changing those mandates for indoor masks? You think they should be lifted, do you?\par \par \uc2 GOTTLIEB: Well, look, COVID won`t disappear. We`re going to have to learn to live with it.\par \par \uc2 But the risk is substantially reduced as a result of vaccination, as a result of immunity that people have acquired through prior infection. And so I think we`re at the point in time when we can start lifting these ordinances in a wholesale fashion. And people have to take precautions based on their individual risk.\par \par \uc2 They have to judge their own individual risk and decide whether or not they`re going to avoid crowds or wear masks based on their circumstances. But we`ve always said, from a public health standpoint, that we would set as a metric maybe when we get down to 10 cases per 100,000 people on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 Well, half the country`s there right now. If you want to be more conservative and say five cases per 100,000 people, well, this week, by this week, probably about a quarter of states will be there. So we`re at the point right now where we could start lifting these ordinances and allowing people to resume normal activity.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, outdoors, we shouldn`t be putting limits on gatherings anymore. We should be encouraging people to go outside. In the states where prevalence is low, vaccination rates are high, and we have good testing in place, so we`re identifying infections, I think we could start lifting these restrictions indoors as well on a broad basis.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: When you talk about people`s individual risk calculations, those have been all over the place.\par \par \uc2 One of the things we`ve seen in these economic numbers on Friday was that people are still scared. Help people understand how they should think through making their own risk calculations, after more than a year of kind of stabbing at trying to get some clarity to decide how to keep themselves safe.\par \par \uc2 GOTTLIEB: Well, look, I think part of the hesitation right now is cultural. We have spent a year apart. We`ve spent a year being told to stay away from people and wear masks.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s going to take some time to get back in the normal swing of things and get that socialization back. But if you`ve been vaccinated with the mRNA vaccines or the J&J vaccine, your risk is very low from having a bad COVID outcome, and your risk of getting an asymptomatic infection that you could spread to others is also substantially reduced.\par \par \uc2 The data now does support that. So we could start drawing some firm conclusions and basing our public health advice on that. You know, there`s one model that`s out that says that, if you were 65 and above, your risk of succumbing to COVID, if you were infected with it, was around one in 100 during the peak of the infection and probably a little worse than that at the outset.\par \par \uc2 That same model now predicts it`s about one in 20,000 if you`ve been fully vaccinated and you`re an immunocompetent person. So, the risk is substantially reduced from vaccination. And I think you can sort of conclude that probably your risk from COVID, if you`re fully vaccinated, is comparable, maybe even less than your risk from flu if you`re fully vaccinated in older individuals.\par \par \uc2 So, you can start resuming normal activity. I think the only residual risk for someone who`s fully vaccinated is, could you have asymptomatic infection that you don`t know about? And if you`re around someone who`s immunocompromised, is vulnerable to COVID, you still want to be careful in those circumstances.\par \par \uc2 But you know when you`re in those circumstances, and you always want to be careful in those circumstances because you don`t want to pass an infection to someone who can`t defend against it.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: So, is it fair to interpret what you`re saying is that, if I have been vaccinated twice, even if I`m in an elderly population, that, essentially, the risk for me, I shouldn`t think about it as a new thing, but I should think about it basically the way it would be with the regular old flu, that where we are now is comparable to something we know before, in terms of the kinds of risks we would take when we operated in regular life?\par \par \uc2 GOTTLIEB: Look, I think that that`s right.\par \par \uc2 People get uncomfortable when you start comparing the rate of death and the risk of COVID to flu, because of some of the comparisons that have been made in the past. But I think, for most consumers who need something to anchor against, I think that that`s a fair assessment, that, if you`re fully vaccinated against COVID with one of the Western vaccines, your risk of having a bad outcome from COVID is about comparable to flu and maybe less, because the vaccines for COVID are more effective than the vaccines for flu.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that that`s a reasonable way for the average consumer to anchor their thinking about COVID right now. Again, the only residual concern I think a fully vaccinated person should have is, are they -- are they themselves immunocompromised?\par \par \uc2 I mean, and you know if you are. If you have a chronic disease that makes you more vulnerable, you`ll know that. And the vaccines won`t be as effective for you. And are you going to be around people who are immunocompromised?\par \par \uc2 And, there, you want to be more careful. That`s where I would still exercise some caution. But outside of those circumstances, I think we can get back to doing normal things right now against the backdrop of a summer when prevalence is going to decline very quickly.\par \par \uc2 I talked at -- in the outset about 10 cases per 100,000 or five cases per 100,000. We`re dropping about one point every two or three days. So, by the end of the next two weeks, most of the country is going to be around five or maybe a little bit more. It`s going to be quite low, the prevalence.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: When you look at the map of places that have not had a lot of people getting vaccinated, do you expect that those will be, in the future, the places where we might see some occasional flare-ups between now and the next big stage of this?\par \par \uc2 GOTTLIEB: I think that`s probably right.\par \par \uc2 And as vaccine effectiveness perhaps declines as we head into the fall and the winter for people who were vaccinated a full year ago, I think we`re going to have to look at the data closely on that, how much of a decline you get in protection.\par \par \uc2 But that`s a long ways off. We`ll worry about that in the fall and the winter. I do think we`re going to have to contend with COVID again this winter. And we may have to reimplement mask ordinances in certain settings where we have outbreaks or even close schools in certain settings.\par \par \uc2 But I think it`s going to be much more reactive. We`re going to be reacting and putting public health measures in place based on measures of spread and not these sort of widespread ordinances that we have right now.\par \par \uc2 I think we need to start lifting these things as the situation improves also to demonstrate that we can do that and that we maintain our integrity and our ability to reimplement these things when we have to. The public has to trust that public health officials are going to lift these restrictions as quickly as they put them in place as the conditions improve.\par \par \uc2 And that`s probably partly what`s holding back the economy right now. People are not going back to work, not just because of these benefits -- I know there`s been a lot of talk about that -- but also because they`re being told they have to wear masks and still have to exercise cautions that probably in many parts of this country you don`t have to do.\par \par \uc2 You look at San Francisco, 20 cases a day, more than 70 percent of the population vaccinated, very good testing in place. They don`t need mask ordinances indoors anymore, and certainly not outside.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: All right, \uc2 Dr. Scott Gottlieb\uc2 , we really appreciate you every week. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GOTTLIEB: Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: We turn now to jobs and the economy and Commerce Secretary Gina Raimondo.\par \par \uc2 She joins us from Providence, Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and happy Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 GINA RAIMONDO, U.S. SECRETARY OF COMMERCE: Good morning, and happy Mother`s Day to all the mothers and grandmothers out there.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: I want to start with the jobs report.\par \par \uc2 There were all kinds of expectations there would be a million jobs in April. The number came in at 266,000. What do you think happened?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: I think we have a long way to go to recover from the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 It`s -- there are so many Americans still struggling, eight million fewer jobs than there were pre-pandemic. So, we are working very hard. In fact, we had a meeting with the president on Friday, and that was his direction to us, which is, we are making bold moves, but there`s a long way to go and we have to be there to help Americans find jobs.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Did he say do X, Y or Z to speed this recovery up?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: Right now, we`re obviously very focused on working with Congress to get the president`s jobs package passed and his families package.\par \par \uc2 The reality is, we have -- we have fallen behind with our investments in the economy, and people are still struggling, women, in particular, still struggling to find affordable childcare, to break down some of the barriers necessary to find a job, to have access to the skills they need to get a good job.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Women, in particular, between three and four million lost jobs in this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 It fell on their shoulders because, in most families, women bear the burden of the pandemic more than any others. Is there a particular friction to women getting back into the work force that you`ve seen that makes this recovery particularly difficult for women?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 So, first of all, women are clustered in the industries that were hit the most, the lower-skilled service jobs, waitresses, working in hotels. And we all know that those industries were hit the hardest. So women were put out of work in greater numbers.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is, as you say, women are more likely to be the caretakers. So, lack of affordable childcare hits women the hardest. The fact that schools were closed and many still remain closed hits women harder.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: On unemployment, there are a lot of people who believe that the unemployment relief that people are getting is hurting in the job market. Essentially, people are keeping unemployment, instead of going to look for work.\par \par \uc2 Is there any discussion within the administration about tweaking that or doing anything to remove that as a barrier to jobs?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: Obviously, we are monitoring that, but, at this point, there`s nothing in the data which would suggest that that`s the reason people are out of work.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we have to remember that, when the president moved to make this happen, this unemployment insurance has been a lifeline, a survival lifeline for so many Americans. The number one reason now that people aren`t going back to work is what you said, fear, or they can`t find childcare or schools are still closed.\par \par \uc2 So, we`ll monitor it as necessary. But, right now, we don`t think that`s because people aren`t able to go back to work.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: In South Carolina and Montana, they`re getting rid of unemployment because they think it has this effect on work.\par \par \uc2 And even in Rhode Island, your successor has pushed for legislation that allows people to keep some of their unemployment benefits if they go back to work. So, it does seem that a lot of people out there think this really is an impediment to getting people -- that unemployment benefits are an impediment to getting people to go look for work.\par \par \uc2 So it does seem there is some evidence out there.\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: So, there`s certainly anecdotal evidence.\par \par \uc2 By the way, this is regional, and it`s appropriate that governors in different regions would respond to what`s going on in their regional labor market. But if you look nationally, wages aren`t going up. People are still telling us the number one reason they`re not going back to work is fear due to the virus.\par \par \uc2 And more people were looking for work last month than the month before. So, we are -- I am engaged with businesses constantly, listening, monitoring. But at the moment, it doesn`t seem to be that that`s the major impediment\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: In conversations with business, one of the reasons the numbers were down this last month is that so-called supply chain. Manufacturers couldn`t get the materials they needed to make what they need.\par \par \uc2 How much of a worry is that to you, and how long might that delay a robust economic recovery?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: It`s a significant factor. It`s a significant worry.\par \par \uc2 You see supply chains having been disrupted across the board. An area that I am particularly focused on, as commerce secretary, is the semiconductor industry. For decades, we`ve allowed America to fall behind and we don`t produce enough semiconductors in America.\par \par \uc2 And the president`s jobs package calls for $50 billion investment, so we can reshore that supply chain, make semiconductors in America, so we`re less vulnerable. And, of course, semiconductors are -- they`re the building blocks of a future economy.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s a top priority and something we`re working aggressively to address.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: But the timeline on that is very long.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the Intel CEO said that he expects the supply chain to affect -- to be -- the shortage to last for a couple of years. So this is something that looks like it`s going to be with us for a while. And, also, it seems to me that the president has offered a $50 billion plan on semiconductors over five years.\par \par \uc2 But TSMC, the Taiwanese manufacturer of chips, is spending $28 billion a year. So, this both seems like a very long-term solution and also maybe not enough.\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: Well, our -- the $50 billion the president is calling for has to be matched by the private sector. You know, it is my hope that the $50 billion would be matched by another $50 billion or $100 billion from the private sector.\par \par \uc2 But the point is we`re finally taking action. You know, the -- President Biden`s plan, which -- which, by the way, we see a lot of bipartisan support for in Congress -- I have had many, many discussions with senators -- have -- we have to get going.\par \par \uc2 As you say, this will take years. We`re behind, and it`s time to take action and get going on the plan now.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: As the administration`s liaison to business, I wonder what you`re hearing about the president`s proposed tax increases on corporations.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , the CEO of \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2 , said those taxes were -- tax increases were a little crazy. What have you heard from business in your conversations about the future of their taxes being increased?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: So, in this regard, I have been actually pleasantly surprised.\par \par \uc2 I have talked to nearly 100 CEOs personally. And what they all agree on is, they support the president`s calls for bold, big infrastructure investments, which are necessary for their competitiveness. They all agree we need investments in broadband, so every American has broadband, major investments in roads and bridges and childcare.\par \par \uc2 The question, of course, is, how do you pay for it? And the reality is, they knew increased corporate taxes were coming. And I have been very pleased by how many CEOs have come out to support the president`s plan. So, listen, there`ll be room for compromise, for sure.\par \par \uc2 And the president will be meeting with members of Congress this week hoping to find compromise. But businesses need these investments to be competitive, and that`s why there`s no time to waste.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: I want to ask you about a piece of news on the \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2  that was hit with a cyberattack.\par \par \uc2 This supplies roughly 45 percent of the gas to the East Coast. A, do you think it will have an economic impact? And then, B, what -- is this what businesses now have to worry about? Because this isn`t the first business to be hit by a ransomware attack.\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: This is what businesses now have to worry about.\par \par \uc2 And I will be working very closely with \uc2 Ali Mayorkas\uc2  this. It`s a top priority for the administration. Unfortunately, these sorts of attacks are becoming more frequent. They`re here to stay. And we have to work in partnership with businesses to secure -- secure networks to defend ourselves against these attacks.\par \par \uc2 As it relates to Colonial, the president was briefed yesterday. It`s an- all-hands-on-deck effort right now. And we are working closely with the company, state and local officials to make sure that they get back up to normal operations as quickly as possible, and there aren`t disruptions in supply.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: All right, Secretary Gina Raimondo, thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Happy Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 And FACE THE NATION will be back in one minute with the head of the \uc2 Minneapolis Fed\uc2 , \uc2 Neel Kashkari\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: We go now to Minneapolis Fed President \uc2 Neel Kashkari\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thanks so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 I want to jump right in here to the first question, which is the April jobs report. Help us understand what it means about that specific report and then what you think can be determined, if anything, about the larger state of the economy.\par \par \uc2 NEEL KASHKARI\uc2 , PRESIDENT, FEDERAL RESERVE BANK OF MINNEAPOLIS: John, I think we shouldn`t overreact to any one report, either the really good report that came out the month prior or last week`s report as well.\par \par \uc2 But I think the bottom line is, we are still somewhere between eight and 10 million jobs below where we were before the pandemic. Roughly eight to 10 million Americans ought to be working right now if the COVID crisis had not happened. So, we still are in a deep hole and we still need to do everything we can to put those folks back to work more quickly.\par \par \uc2 Let me just give you one example. When the financial crisis hit in 2008, it took 10 years to rebuild the labor market and put the economy fully back together. That is far too long. And so we at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  are doing everything we can to accelerate that job market recovery, because it`s good for the economy and it`s good for families all across the country.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Well, that specter of a slow, plodding 10-year recovery is what has everybody worried.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of worry, the question of risk, individual risk perception, we do see that people have more money than they seem to be spending. Give us your understanding of personal risk choices that people are making. And can that really be changed, and, if so, how?\par \par \uc2 KASHKARI: I think your discussions with Dr. Gottlieb and the commerce secretary were exactly right.\par \par \uc2 You know, the -- it wasn`t simply the lockdowns from the government that put a damper on the economy. It was each of us, each of your viewers, each family, taking actions to protect themselves. Yesterday, for the first time in over a year, I got back on an airplane because my wife and I had been vaccinated, and we felt comfortable.\par \par \uc2 But it`s going to take time for that psychology to change. And so I do think that there is some truth to the unemployment benefits maybe being a disincentive. I see that in the data. And I see that in anecdotes as we talk to people.\par \par \uc2 I also think that childcare shortages are a big impediment to people coming back in, with schools still being partially closed, and I think that fear, the risk of, hey, I don`t want to get back onto a crowded bus if that`s what it`s going to take to go to my job.\par \par \uc2 Now, the good news is, over the next three or four months, each of those factors should get better. As the vaccine continues to penetrate, as the virus continues to slow down, schools reopen and people regain their confidence, those things should get better, which should lead to strong growth in the second half of the year and strong labor market recovery, I hope.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: It feels like you`re putting -- telling all of us to look to September, when two of those things are happening, school restarts and those federal unemployment benefits will go away.\par \par \uc2 So, is that a smart way to think about this, maybe a little bit sluggish until we get in September, people are more prodded to work because they`re no longer getting unemployment benefits, and those who worry about having kids at home, well, the kids will be off at school?\par \par \uc2 KASHKARI: I think those two factors, and I`m assuming continued vaccine penetration, will give all of us more confidence.\par \par \uc2 So, for me, I`m thinking about myself. My wife and I are vaccinated. When are we going to go back into a crowded restaurant, where we might be around other people who are unvaccinated? That gives me some pause, even though I know how effective the vaccines are.\par \par \uc2 As the overall population vaccine penetration goes up, I`m going to feel more confident. So, I think all three of those factors are all going to trend in a better direction over the next few months. And, of course, we have to watch -- Dr. Gottlieb and others can advise us on the variants, but the trend is obviously very good, which should boost confidence.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: We have 30 seconds before the break.\par \par \uc2 Anything else in the April jobs report that stood out to you?\par \par \uc2 KASHKARI: Well, women continue to be disproportionately affected by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 I think the women -- the data went down for women, actually. There was no job growth. I don`t want to overreact to one month, but the childcare issues continue to be paramount. And that`s why getting schools fully reopened, getting kids vaccinated, that`s also going to be key to really restoring our economy and getting back to full productivity.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: OK, we`re going to take a break really fast right now.\par \par \uc2 But, \uc2 Neel Kashkari\uc2 , stay with us. We`ll talk to you on the other side of the break.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: If you`re not able to watch the full FACE THE NATION, you can set your DVR, or we`re available on demand.\par \par \uc2 Plus, you can watch us through your \uc2 CBS\uc2  and Paramount+ apps.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And we will be right back with more from Minneapolis Fed President \uc2 Neel Kashkari\uc2 , plus Congressman \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  and author Michael Lewis.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We continue our conversation with Minneapolis Fed President \uc2 Neel Kashkari\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I want to underscore what you said when we started, which is, don`t overdo it on looking at one month report. However, the projections were that there was going to be a million jobs in this one month. So that`s an enormous miss.\par \par \uc2 What I wonder is, is there something about this response to the pandemic, coming out of it, that has kind of thrown off all of our abilities to measure economic activity and -- and to project what economic activity will be?\par \par \uc2 NEEL KASHKARI\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO, FEDERAL RESERVE BANK OF MINNEAPOLIS: Absolutely. I mean this is unlike any other economic shock in any of our lifetimes. This is very different than the financial crisis. And as we talked about earlier, before the break, a lot of this is being driven by people`s only feelings of personal safety, their own health for themselves and their families. It`s very hard to model that out and to know.\par \par \uc2 You know, Dr. Gottlieb talked about, we spent a year conditioning all of us to wear masks, to be safe, to be cautious. Now we have to start to change what we`ve been telling people and get people to change how they feel inside.\par \par \uc2 And so we can put out economic forecasts of how people are going to respond to different incentives, but it`s really uncertain. And so it is still the virus and people`s -- the reality of the virus and people feeling safe about the virus that`s ultimately going to drive this economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 I think the hope is that Congress has been so aggressive in the past year, and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has been so aggressive in the past year, that we have positioned the economy for a fast recovery, not a 10 year recovery, but there`s still a great deal of uncertainty about the virus, but also about how we are all feeling and the confidence that ultimately we need to have to fully restore the economy.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: What does a recovery look like, actually? So if it`s not 10 years, how many years are we talking about? And is it just replacing the eight million jobs or is it replacing the 10 million that we would have if we hadn`t been hit by this pandemic?\par \par \uc2 KASHKARI: It`s the -- for me, it`s the 10 million, because our population continues to grow and so those are new people potentially entering the workforce, hopefully entering the workforce. So you are exactly right, if the pandemic that not hit, we estimate there would be 10 million more Americans working today, both people who already had jobs and new entrants into the labor force. We need to put all of them back to work.\par \par \uc2 And so, at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , we`re focused on achieving what we call maximum employment, as many Americans as possible, gainfully employed and contributing to our economy.\par \par \uc2 And so, for me, I`m looking at, when do we recover those 10 million jobs? I think it`s going to take a few years before we can fully get back to that place. But, you know, two years is much better than 10 years. If we can do it faster, so much the better.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: The Fed thinks about maximum employment and prices.\par \par \uc2 Tell us in this strange world we`re in right now what we should think about inflation and prices, when we should be nervous, when we should not remember stories from the 1970s and overreact?\par \par \uc2 KASHKARI: Yes, well, we aim for an average of 2 percent inflation over time. For 10 years we`ve been undershooting that 2 percent inflation target.\par \par \uc2 Now, we know, just based on math, that in the next few months the inflation numbers are going to look high because the way inflation is calculated, it`s a rolling 12 months. And so last year, last March, the inflation numbers plummeted, oil prices plummeted. Those data are going to now fall out of the calculation. So just based on math, we know the inflation numbers are going to look high in the next few months.\par \par \uc2 We also know there are short-term supply chain issues that you talked about earlier with semiconductors. So what we`re very focused on the -- in the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is looking deep at the data to determine, are these short- term mathematical supply-chain issues that should resolve themselves, or are they longer-term inflation issues? And so, for me, I`m very skeptical that we`re going to have sustained, high inflation if we still have five or 10 million Americans out of work. We think the labor market is really what`s going to drive inflation over the long-term.\par \par \uc2 And so we look at a lot of different data. Right now I`m not concerned about a repeat of the 1970s. I do think inflation is going to pop in the near term, but that is likely going to be transitory. But if we`re wrong, and if high inflation comes because of a lot of government spending over the next few years, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has the tools to make sure that we do not have a repeat of the 1970s. That is certainly not my base-case scenario. But if that were to happen, we know how to deal with that.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: All right, \uc2 Neel Kashkari\uc2 , thank you so much for putting it all into perspective for us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 KASHKARI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Former President Trump continues to be the central figure in the ongoing drama over the future of the Republican Party. This week, House Republicans are expected to remove Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  from her leadership position. She`s been consistently critical of Mr. Trump for perpetuating the lie that the election had been stolen from him and inciting the riots on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 Illinois Congressman \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  is one of the few Republican members supporting Cheney and joins us from his district in Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (R-IL): Good morning. How are you?\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Before we get to the Republican Party, I want to ask you about \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2 . You are on the Energy and Commerce Committee. What do you know about this attack and how important is it?\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: It`s really important in terms of, you know, understanding the role that cyber is playing in the future. Things like critical infrastructure, energy. I don`t know much more than what you all know. Maybe I will this week when I get back to D.C. But this needs to redouble our efforts as a country to -- to get past our internal divisions as the end all be all of everything and -- and focus on things like critical infrastructure in the future because this is only going to continue to happen more often if we`re not careful.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Speaking of internal divisions, the Republican Party in the House, it looks like \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the number three in House leadership on the Republican side, is no longer going to hold that position. What is that debate about?\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: Yes, look, it`s incredible. So, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is saying exactly what Kevin McCarthy said the day of the insurrection. She`s just consistently being saying it. And a few weeks later, Kevin McCarthy changed to attacking other people. And so I think what the reality is, is, as a party, we have to have an internal look and a full accounting as to what led to January 6th. I mean right now it`s basically the Titanic. We`re like, you know, in this -- in the middle of this slow sink. We have a band playing on the deck, telling everybody it`s fine, and, meanwhile, as I`ve said, you know, Donald Trump`s running around trying to find women`s clothing and get on the first life boat.\par \par \uc2 And I think there`s a few of us that are just saying, guys, this is not good, not just for the future of the party, but this is not good for the future of this country. We`re four months after January 6th, an insurrection, something that was unthinkable in this country, and the message from the people that want to get rid of \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is to say, it`s just time to focus on the future and move on. Like, this was 10 years ago and we`ve been obsessed about it since. It`s been four months and we have so many people, including our leadership in the party, that has not admitted that this is what it is --\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: But --\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: Which was an insurrection, led by the president of the United States, well-deserving of a full accounting from Republicans.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: But if you are a leader of a party, and 70 percent of the members of your party think the last election was stolen, though they`re wrong about that, you`re a leader of that party, you can`t change the party you`re the leader of. So why should a person be a leader of a party that is fundamentally at odds with what \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  believes?\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: Because truth matters. Right now -- and we have to look and understand why. You know, yes, 70 percent of the base believes that the election was stolen because they`ve been told it was. They`ve been told by the president of the United States. They`ve been told, in many cases, by Republican leaders, or at least Republican leaders in the last have not counted it, on something so vastly crazy as the election is stolen.\par \par \uc2 You know, and -- and this is why you have this real battle right now in the party. This idea of, let`s just put our differences aside and be unified. You cannot unify truth with lies. The lie is that the election was stolen. The truth is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And I`m sorry that 74 million people voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They were disenfranchised, they were simply outnumbered. And as a party, let`s focus on now, how do we go out and win more people?\par \par \uc2 And that`s why, actually, I started the Country First movement at country1st.com is just to say, tell people the truth and quit pedaling in conspiracies because that`s what we`ve seen in this party. And they`re going to get rid of \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  because they`d much rather pretend that the conspiracy is either real or not confront it than to actually confront it and maybe have to take the temporary licks to save this party in the long term in this country.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Speaking about number, though, you and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and maybe \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  in the Senate are outnumbered by the current Republican Party. It is Donald Trump`s Republican Party. The numbers are on their side.\par \par \uc2 So, why stay in it?\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: Well, look, it`s -- it`s -- I was a Republican far longer than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and I`m only 43 years old. He became a Republican just a few years ago and -- and tried to change the whole definition of conservatism. So I think it`s worth a fight.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, the reality is, over time we`re going to see who -- who wins this battle. And -- and then there will be a lot of decisions to make everywhere as we see parties realign and change. For me, I`m a conservative. I`m going to fight for the soul of this party. And -- but every member, not just -- not just, you know, leadership, every congressman, every state representative, every member of the party that pulls a ballot in the primary has to decide, are we going to exist on lies or are we going to exist on truth? And to everybody that grew up in Sunday school, like me, that thinks somehow accepting a lie is OK because maybe we can win the bigger battles, I`ve got to tell you, the -- you know, the Christ I follow, the Jesus I know never says anything about, it`s OK to lie to the people as long as the end state is the same. Truth matters and that`s what this party has to come to grips with no matter the cost.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Do you think Donald Trump`s power in the Republican Party comes from his ability to grow the electorate, reach out to new voters, or because members are scared of crossing him?\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: I think it`s the latter. I think at the beginning, look, he was able to reach to a sector that we should naturally win. People that are struggling to make ends meet. We also lost a lot of people in that process. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was done after January 6th. When Kevin McCarthy said, you know, this is Donald Trump`s fault, make no mistake, he was done. He was sulking away to Mar-a-Lago, didn`t even go to the inauguration.\par \par \uc2 And two weeks later, when you look at the financial side of it, and you look at the fact there`s an election in two years and we want the majority, Kevin McCarthy went and so did Steve Scalise, and they put the paddles on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and resurrected him in the party. And everybody, after that, became scared to death of who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was again. And that`s what empowered him. And everybody went quiet. That`s why the Country First movement I started, country1st.com, is trying to push back, because we have to fight for the soul of this party and country.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: All right, \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2 , the fight continues. Thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: We turn now to the coronavirus crisis in India, where nearly half of all cases and a quarter of the deaths around the world were reported last week according to the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CBS News foreign correspondent Chris Livesay reports from New Delhi.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY, CBS NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 India`s stratospheric death toll shows no signs of slowing. New Delhi announced this morning it`s extending its lockdown at police checkpoints like these for another week as cases creep throughout the country and even across India`s borders.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LIVESAY (voice over): The River Ganges is revered as a goddess, cleansing sins and ushering the dead to paradise. But in recent months, it was a gateway to India`s pandemic inferno. Millions of Hindus gathered on its banks at the Festival of Kumbha, thought to be the biggest super spreader event ever. Due to religious gatherings, political rallies and letting down its guard, India is now surpassing 400,000 daily infections for four days in a row, says Princeton epidemiologist, Romanon Laxminarian (ph).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There`s still a huge underestimate of where they are. And every death is -- is just a tragedy because these were all avoidable deaths for the most part.\par \par \uc2 LIVESAY: The virus now shows terrifying signs of catching fire in rural areas, where 75 percent of Indians live, with abysmal health care. Hidden cameras show a hospital in this holy city of Varanasi, relatives, not doctors, look after the sick, mixed among patients with other ailments.\par \par \uc2 If my wife had gotten the right medicines and treatment, she might have survived, but she didn`t get it here, says Pankash Maria (ph).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They really need to restrict the movements and the gatherings in those places.\par \par \uc2 LIVESAY: But so far the government has ruled out a nationwide lockdown despite deaths on pace to reach 1 million by August. India is one of the world`s biggest producers of vaccines, but only about 3 percent of Indians have been vaccinated, prompting the government to ban exports and causing a crisis in Africa. Kenya says it will run out of vaccine within days at the continent braces for a second wave, hopefully not as ferocious as India`s.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LIVESAY: And cases are also beginning to spike in neighboring countries like Pakistan and Nepal. Now, here in New Delhi, a leading epidemiologist tells me that the mere local lockdown is too little, too late.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Chris Livesay reporting from India.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And we go now to Michael Lewis, author of a new book, "The Premonition: A Pandemic Story." He joins us from Berkeley, California.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEWIS, AUTHOR, "THE PREMONITION: A PANDEMIC STORY": Good morning, John.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: I want to start with a quote from Bill Parcells, the NFL football coach, you are what your record says you are. You quote that in your book to explain your view about the American record on the coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 Why is that quote important?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: It`s important -- I mean the starting point for this book is, look, we were sitting at a little more than 4 percent of the world`s population with 20 percent of the world`s death. And before the season, before the pandemic, we were -- we were -- we were rated by experts as most prepared for this sort of risk.\par \par \uc2 The -- and -- and -- but the games get played, and as Bill Parcells said after a losing season, you know, you can make all kinds of excuses, you can point fingers at whoever you want to point fingers at, all of it is avoiding looking in the mirror because you are who -- who -- you are what your record says you are. And our record -- our record`s appalling. I mean what we did is appalling and reflect kind of a deep negligence of our own population.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And you went into this project looking for the roots of that negligence, and you chose a number of different characters to illuminate something. What were you trying to illuminate?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: A system. Like the system that -- that -- I was really worried because of the work I had done previously, "The Fifth Risk," the book I had written about the federal government, that the take-away was -- from this whole event was, like with a -- a losing sports team, was going to be, let`s point the finger at the quarterback or -- or the coach or whoever. And -- and people would think, oh, if we just changed the -- if we just changed a person or two here, it`s going to -- it -- that it`s going to -- it`s all going to be better.\par \par \uc2 And -- when, in fact, the characters in the story that I tell, you could tell by the shape of their frustration -- I mean they have these extraordinary people who had gone to unbelievable lengths with a kind of obsession to prevent just this thing from happening that -- that you could tell by how they fail what`s -- that this is a bigger problem, that you`ve got -- we`ve got these systematic things that we need to address.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And one of the big things you identify as a systematic problem is that the people who know don`t -- aren`t able to get their information to the heads of the people who can act, either because they`re not heard or because they`re not listened to.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: That`s part of it. That`s part of it. I mean we had -- we had, you know, very persuasive evidence. Carter Mesher (ph), one of the main characters in the book, could have sat you down on January the 20th and persuaded you really pretty quickly that we were going to -- that what would happen was going to happen. And that`s -- that`s a month before the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2  acknowledges that, you know, this is in a risk -- a risk to American lives.\par \par \uc2 And in that month, you know, the delay of the response cost thousands and thousands of lives. So -- so it -- part of it is -- part of it is that. It`s like -- it`s like, yes, the wrong people are -- are being listened to, but part of it is, like the system was designed for them never to get there. That -- that we have built -- especially at the federal level, a government that operates in a defensive crouch and that punishes risk taking and punishes error mercilessly but doesn`t punish inaction in the same way. And so anybody who`s -- who`s got, you know, wisdom, news, insight that -- that implies action and implies taking some risk is naturally going to be shunned the -- the -- because the -- because -- because the government really doesn`t want to do that.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And I want to stay -- linger on -- on this important point that you`re making. What your characters all have is that kind of risk-taking muscle. They`ve been in this fight for their whole careers. They can see things. They have, title of the book, a premonition because it`s informed and yet the political instinct in politics, which is to say in -- in -- in an administration is at odds with that. Is that -- is that a fair way to think about this?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: It`s a totally fair way to think about it. And then you think about, well, what does that mean, right? I mean it -- what does it mean, especially when you`re facing this sort of risk? And -- and a disease risk requires you to get out in front of it in -- in ways that are just kind of unnatural to the -- to the structures we have because by the time you have like proof, like by the time you have data, that this is a deadly thing that`s going to kill hundreds of thousands of Americans, it`s -- it`s too late. The disease is -- has -- is moving among you. You have -- you have almost to be clairvoyant. And you have to do things and explain to people why you`re doing them, which don`t seem obvious that -- that, you know, that -- that -- that -- that, yes, there`s this thing in Wuhan, but actually it`s effectively here. And -- and so -- so that -- it`s true, all my characters, the three people at the center of this book, I mean they`re amazing people, but they -- they all have -- they prefer to commit a sin of co-mission than a sin of omission. They prefer -- they`ve preferred -- they -- they -- they default to action.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: And I want to go back to a point you were making earlier, which is, if it`s a systemic problem then blaming \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which is what you were hinting at earlier, actually is an impediment to improving systems so that they`ll be able to handle another one of these.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: Yes, so I don`t want to let \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  off the hook. I mean that`s not really the point. But the point is that no matter how you feel about him, even if you despise him, if you -you`re your mind comes to rest at, oh, it was all his fault, we are never going to address the underlying problem that actually -- that actually drove our response and we are -- we are never going to -- we`re going to be -- we`re going to be similarly at risk the next time this happens. And I tell you, every single one of the characters in the book make a very persuasive case that this was a dry run, that this could have been much worse, that -- that you could have killed children, it could have been more lethal, and that -- and that we`re -- we`re just fools if we think this isn`t going to happen again.\par \par \uc2 So -- so to just point a finger -- it`s like -- like a losing football team, to point a finger at a single person involved and say that`s what we lost, that`s catastrophic.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: All right, Michael --\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: It`s not -- it`s not how you -- it`s not how you fix the team.\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: Right?\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Michael, we are -- we are out of time. We really appreciate it. Thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 And this programming note. Former First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  sits down with Gayle King for an exclusive interview tomorrow on "\uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DICKERSON: For some number of ions, on a show like this, Happy Mother`s Day was something the moderator might wish others to have. No more. We are full of mothers at FACE THE NATION. Our moderator, Margaret Brennan, just had her second child. Our executive producer, \uc2 Mary Hager\uc2 . Senior producer, Avery Miller. Booking producer, Carol Joynt. Digital producer, Emily Tillett. Operations manager, Laura Foran. Associate directors, Sharman Boyle and Janice Huey. Graphics operator, Janette Regnier. Editor, Akura Marshall. And teleprompter operators, Pat Coney (ph) and Cynthia Miller. Mothers all.\par \par \uc2 And my mother, Nancy Dickerson, worked on the first broadcast at FACE THE NATION, and, today, I`ll see my mother-in-law, Betty McKean (ph), for the first time in more than 18 months. Fifty years ago she adopted my favorite mother of all, Ann Dickerson, her daughter.\par \par \uc2 Bless all of you mothers from all of us grateful sons and daughters.\par \par \uc2 That`s it for us today. Thanks for watching. Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m John Dickerson.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020210509eh5900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Some Tax Season Advice By \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\b0\par\par\uc2 ANTHONY MASON, JILL SCHLESINGER, GAYLE KING \par \uc2 877 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: In today`s Eye on Money, we are talking about taxes, unfortunately. The filing deadline was extended, though, to May 17th this year. Thanks to the American Rescue Plan Act, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  has already processed more than a hundred and five million tax returns and given out more than seventy-seven million refunds. The average refund is two thousand seventy dollars. Here with some tax season advice is CBS News business analyst \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2 . Jill, good morning. This is not my favorite time of the year. But here`s a first question, if someone collected unemployment benefits, if you received the stimulus check, is that taxable income?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2  (CBS News Business Analyst): So there`s a really interesting change for this tax year. For unemployment benefits, which are usually taxable, if you earn less than a hundred fifty thousand dollars last year, ten thousand two hundred dollars of unemployment benefits are not taxable to you. And that`s a big deal. Twenty thousand four hundred if you both got it as a married couple. Now the stimulus checks, whatever you received, that is not taxable at all. So for those who received that emergency assistance, the tax situation should be a lot better than you probably thought six months ago.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All right. That`s good news. But there`s a lot of confusing elements this year, particularly-- here`s a question that-- that stumps me, if someone worked remotely in another state, if your office is in one state, you worked remotely from another, do you pay taxes in-- in that state?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : This is so confusing. And I am sorry. I contacted the great folks at the AICPA, big organization of CPAs, and they say this comes down to you counting the number of days where you worked. And what you`re going to have to do is find out what the rules are in the local jurisdiction as well as in your usual place of domicile. I know if your head is spinning right now, you may want to contact a CPA or a tax preparer to help you out. You know, in some cases, Anthony, it could be good news. You might have worked in a place with a lower tax rate. So, it is worth going through the process.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All right. We were given-- obviously the deadline was pushed back to May 17th. That`s good news. But if you still haven`t gotten your act together, can you file for an extension?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : You can. But get your act together. You know, those numbers that you put up in the beginning are very instructive. You know, about eighty-five percent of tax filers, they receive refunds. And don`t you want that money? That`s thousands of dollars. But, yes, if you have a problem, you can absolutely, positively file for an extension. It`s Form 4868. And that will give you an extension until October 15th. The extension comes with a caveat, and that is if you think you owe taxes, you`ve got to pay those taxes now, by the 17th, whatever you think you owe. The actual filing can take place by October 15th. One other thing, if you plan on making a Roth IRA or an IRA contribution, you don`t delay that. That deadline is May 17th, you-- even if you file for an extension. So get that money in so you can start saving more for retirement.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But, Jill, what if you just can`t afford to pay your taxes right now? What are the options? You throw yourself on the mercy of the court?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, you know, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  has plenty of options. And that`s really good news. Because there`s an option to actually say give me a little help for, say, four months, that`s a short-term plan. There`s a longer term installment plan. There`s also a way that you could actually pay taxes with a credit card. I don`t suggest that because there are fees associated. But you probably can get yourself on a plan that`s pretty easy at irs.gov. There`s a lot of options there. And they`re not really so expensive. So I encourage you to do that. One thing also, never hide from the \uc2 IRS\uc2  because even though they`ve been underfunded for the last twenty years--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They will get you.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : --they tend to find you. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They will get you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And-- yeah, you don`t want the \uc2 IRS\uc2  coming after you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And again, filing deadline, May 17th, a week from Monday. Thanks, Jill.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210515eh57000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Former Facebook Employee Who Claims The Company Has A Diversity Problem When It Comes To Hiring\b0\par\par\uc2 MICHELLE MILLER, JERICKA DUNCAN \par \uc2 1,728 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: First on \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING, we`re hearing from a former \uc2 Facebook\uc2  employee who says the tech giant has a diversity problem in its hiring practices. Rhett Lindsey claims the predominantly White leadership at \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has created a toxic work environment focused on quotas based on race. Separately, the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2  is looking into Facebook`s hiring methods. The social network tells \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING that increasing diversity at the company is a priority. Our national correspondent Jericka Duncan is here with more on that story. Jericka, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN (\uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING National Correspondent): Michelle, good morning to you. Lindsey worked at \uc2 Facebook\uc2  for just under a year. He was hired in 2020 to recruit diverse talent. But Lindsey says he quickly found the company`s recruiting tactics troubling.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RHETT LINDSEY (Former Facebook Employee and CEO of Siimee): The goal was to truly work at a company where I could have great impact. And I thought at the time that Facebook`s platform was the place where I can do that.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: But when fully embedded in his new position, thirty-two- year-old Rhett Lindsey says he discovered what he calls a problematic workplace and a culture focused on quotas.\par \par \uc2 RHETT LINDSEY: It`s the issue of not reaching out because you`re possibly the best fit for the role. It`s reaching out because you`re a Black person and all I`m caring about is trying to hit my metric goal for the week on how much outreach I`ve had to diverse candidates.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And does that translate, though, into people getting jobs?\par \par \uc2 RHETT LINDSEY: So, not necessarily.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And is that the problem for you?\par \par \uc2 RHETT LINDSEY: That`s the problem.\par \par \uc2 And what I would normally do is start off by the skill set.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Lindsey says he reached a breaking point last August when a song repeatedly using the N-word was played during an online diversity recruiting meeting.\par \par \uc2 RHETT LINDSEY: It goes back to leadership. They deem that okay to play that in a diversity recruiting meeting. Where the (EXPLETIVE DELETED) at. We`re talking about trying to find Black talent, and you`re saying a song that says where the (EXPLETIVE DELETED) at.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: How hurt were you by that?\par \par \uc2 RHETT LINDSEY: I was extremely hurt. I felt betrayed by the company that I was proud to work at.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: CBS News reviewed the video and saw messages Lindsey posted that everyone could see voicing his frustration. In one message, Lindsey wrote, "Why are you playing a song that is literally saying the N-word? Come on."\par \par \uc2 RHETT LINDSEY: I was leaving live comments that the person that led the meeting could see. People could see. And there was people leaving reactions to my comments.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Lindsey says his comments were not addressed until the meeting ended.\par \par \uc2 And the apology didn`t come from the White man who was playing the song.\par \par \uc2 RHETT LINDSEY: No. It did not.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: It came from a Black woman who was on that meeting?\par \par \uc2 RHETT LINDSEY: Correct. She spoke the majority. It was "we apologize, we`re sorry."\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: In recent years, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has publicly pledged to put a greater emphasis on diversity and inclusion. The company`s 2020 diversity report shows the social network has been slowly increasing representation over the past seven years--from two percent of employees being Black to now 3.9 percent. \uc2 Facebook\uc2  tells CBS News it plans to increase the number of Black people in leadership roles in the U.S. by thirty percent by 2025. Still, Lindsey claims the hiring process is flawed due in part to what he says is Facebook`s use of "culture fit" when assessing candidates. The company denies using the practice.\par \par \uc2 What is a culture fit?\par \par \uc2 RHETT LINDSEY: Culture fit is identifying someone that blends well with what`s already established.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Evelyn Carter is managing director at Paradigm. The strategy firm works with over six hundred companies seeking to diversify their workplaces.\par \par \uc2 DR. EVELYN CARTER (Paradigm Strategy Inc. Managing Director): The problem is that culture fit often turns into what some companies literally call the beer test, right? Would I want to go out and have a beer with you after work? That`s a great criterion for finding a new friend, not so much for evaluating a candidate for hire.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Since leaving \uc2 Facebook\uc2  last November, Lindsey started his own consulting company with a focus on diverse hiring.\par \par \uc2 RHETT LINDSEY: Diversity is not just someone`s skin color and gender. Diversity is everyone`s background, their experiences, their culture, their aspirations, their identity.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Are you encouraged at all by telling your story that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  will see this and make changes?\par \par \uc2 RHETT LINDSEY: My hope is that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  leadership can reflect because it`s my story and my perspective, but there`s also other people`s perspectives of their experiences at \uc2 Facebook\uc2  that they need listen to.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: And the expert we spoke to says hiring bias is not unique to \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and that we all have biases that we`re unaware of. In a statement, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  told CBS News they ".take seriously allegations of discrimination and have robust policies. in place for employees to report concerns.." And late last night, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  also told us they have reviewed the song incident and are taking appropriate action.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. So many companies facing this issue right now.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What`s remarkable is that song was playing, and he is in the chat saying--\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Live.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --what`s up?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Like, who`s doing this? And it was only after the meeting ended that he had somebody respond to his concerns.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Correct.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All right. Jericka, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we catch up with an old friend and colleague, a former CBS News cameraman who is up for an Oscar on Sunday for his work on Judas and the Black Messiah. That`s ahead on \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (8:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. It`s that time again, time to bring you some the stories that are the Talk of the Table this morning. Mister Duthiers will lead it off.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Merci beaucoup. So my story is about a little six-year- old girl, her name is Edie. She lives in Chester, in the United Kingdom. And she`s getting a lot of love on social media because she was given an art project, right?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yep.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And this is what she painted. Take a look at her art project. When she submitted this to the art teacher, the teacher told her that she did it wrong.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: What?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: What?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly. I mean--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I love this painting.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Dude, this painting is going viral. People are downloading it, wallpaper on their phones--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Uh-Huh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --on their computer screens. She got so much love on social media and \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Listen to what she has to say about it. Her mom posted is.\par \par \uc2 EDIE (internet video): Thank you for all your kind comments. I`m putting this up on \uc2 Etsy\uc2  called Edie`s Art.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Edie`s Art.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She-- Edie`s Art.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Edie`s Art.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You can buy it on \uc2 Etsy\uc2  now. There`s no--\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: What`s wrong? What was--\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --art is in the eye of the beholder.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Right, but what was wrong about it?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nothing. I-- I mean, like I`m sure more people have seen Edie`s art than the art teacher`s art.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Ooh.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: At this point, I`d say yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. Michelle Miller.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Well, my-- my little story is from Edmond, Oklahoma, and we just have to give a cheer out to Yanet Lopez. She shared a special day with her family and hundreds of students at the school where she is a cafeteria manager. How about that? Those are Deer Park Elementary schoolers cheering on their beloved cafeteria maven yesterday via live stream as she was sworn in as a U.S. citizen. She passed the test last month, I believe, with four other members of her family. That`s when some of the five hundred students at the school surprised her with a standing ovation, down the hallway of the school. Here`s her reaction.\par \par \uc2 CROWD (in unison): USA. USA.\par \par \uc2 YANET LOPEZ: I`m overwhelmed with love from everybody at the school. I think they love me. And we are a big family.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: How about that? Congratulations to you, Yanet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yanet Lopez. Way to go. And great for school to come out and-- and give her a big cheer.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: When I see stories like that--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --I am hopeful.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Well, you know, like, you-- who doesn`t love their cafeteria manager? I mean, come on.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Well, you didn`t eat the food at my cafeteria.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, right, looking back to my days.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Oh, I love them.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love the manager of the food.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: That and the cheese toast and the pizza, come on.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Okay. All right. (INDISTINCT).\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. Anthony, what have you go?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Okay. Go ahead, Anthony.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210425eh4n000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 This morning, we are taking a look at the barriers confronting Asian Americans in the workplace and the controversial phrase, "bamboo ceiling," used to describe the hurdles that they face, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King \par \uc2 2,462 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Cue, Gayle. I love the control room shot. This morning, we are taking a look at the barriers confronting Asian Americans in the workplace and the controversial phrase, "bamboo ceiling," used to describe the hurdles that they face. I`ve never heard that phrase before. 2018 analysis by the Harvard Business Review found that Asian Americans are the least likely group to be promoted into a management position in the United States. Nancy Chen shows us why this phrase first coined back in 2005 is coming up for debate now.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Excerpt from Minari; A24)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN (CBS News Correspondent): Breaking the "bamboo ceiling" is how Hollywood reporter-editor Rebecca Sun described Steven Yeun`s historic Oscar nomination for Best Actor. The first Asian American ever nominated in that category. But the controversial term quickly came under fire.\par \par \uc2 What were the criticisms of the phrase?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SUN (The Hollywood Reporter Senior Editor of Diversity and Inclusion): A lot of people thought that I had invented it. That I was using a lazy pun, you know, shorthand, bamboo, panda bear, there`s a lot of Orientalized or exoticized imagery that people use to reduce Asians to symbols.\par \par \uc2 JANE HYUN: It`s great to be here. Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Inspired by the glass ceiling, executive coach Jane Hyun coined the phrase nearly two decade ago.\par \par \uc2 JANE HYUN: Nobody really knew that Asians had any barrier. Most of the time when I talked about it, they were like, what kind of challenges do they have anyway? I thought they were doing pretty well. And so it was a way of making concrete something people were ignoring.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Hyun blames the model minority myth, the false stereotype of overarching success among Asian Americans. Cultural differences and microaggressions also play a role. Microaggressions can vary from commenting on a person`s English to asking where they are from.\par \par \uc2 BILL IMADA (IW Group Founder, Chairman and Chief Connectivity Officer): You`re very-- very valuable to the team, so please stick around. And your time will come. That time never arrived.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Bill Imada has worked in advertising for more than thirty years. He says he felt his career stall hen bosses praised his hard work while promoting others.\par \par \uc2 BILL IMADA: And I think this happens to a lot of Asian Americans, is that we have a tendency to work hard. We put our nose to the grindstone hoping that somebody will notice. And it`s not in our DNA necessarily to brag about ourselves or to boast.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: What happens to an Asian American person when all these things constantly happen? One small thing after another?\par \par \uc2 BILL IMADA: They become reality. They become part of our psyche. And then we start to doubt ourselves.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Susan Im is a former retail merchandising executive. Assumptions about her character, she says, were evident during a meeting with the senior manager.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN IM (Professional Diversity Coach): And at the end of the conversation, he said to me, you know, hmm, well, you`re not shy. And I said, well, why would you think I was shy? He had a perception about me and was making assumptions about me not having ever met me in person before.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: What did you say back?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN IM: I think I just laughed it off and left.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And those assumptions often make Asian Americans feel like outsiders. The stereotype known as perpetual foreigners.\par \par \uc2 JANE HYUN: If you`re constantly mistaken for another co-worker or because there`s maybe three or four Asian Americans there, I think that`s a-- it`s- - it`s difficult to-- to feel like you have some level of influence and credibility in that team. When I go to speaking engagements, afterwards people come up to me and they said things like, you speak English so well.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Hyun works with companies to get them to be more inclusive with diversity programs beyond hiring.\par \par \uc2 JANE HYUN: You really actually need to have those people come in-- not only come in the door but have the opportunity to reach their full potential in an organization.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Sun says the phrase, "bamboo ceiling," isn`t ideal but believes this is an opportunity to reexamine it.\par \par \uc2 It`s been around for quite some time. Why such a strong reaction right now?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA SUN: There is finally increased sensitivity among the general public to racist headlines, to racist portrayals in the media, and specifically against Asians and Asian Americans. And so I think that people were just at a heightened level of awareness, and so this seemed to play right into that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: As you can see, it is complicated. Interestingly, Rebecca Sun told us a lot of the criticism she received online came from people outside the Asian American community. Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hmm. That`s interesting. Nancy, thank you very much. It`s a fascinating piece.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. It`s so important to have this conversation.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Like I said, I`d never heard the phrase, "bamboo ceiling," then you hear it`s been around for a very long time. It just shows how--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Twenty years. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --yeah, how many of us were not paying attention.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So important to have the conversation.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the assumptions that people make where-- where they said to her, oh, you speak English very well or you`re shy, it`s just so stupid.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know. I hope--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Never assume.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --I hope-- I hope it`s changing. I think it is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People-- awareness is going to lead people to make different decisions.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It`s a start at least.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. Coming up, we hear from a college gymnast who is going viral for doing this--not the-- not the move, wait, wait, wait for it. That, whipping out his--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There it is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --vaccine record after a near-perfect vault routine. Why he pulled out his vaccination card, surprising everyone, that is coming up.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS (\uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING Correspondent): This morning, we`re hearing from a college gymnast who`s getting national attention for the trick he pulled after landing a near-perfect vault routine last month. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1 (NCAA/Big Ten Network): Top scorer, 14.75 against the Buckeyes.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2 (NCAA/Big Ten Network): Same vault as the (INDISTINCT). Oh, and he sticks the landing. Not sure what that is.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1 (NCAA/Big Ten Network): I think it`s vaccine.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2 (NCAA/Big Ten Network): Vaccine card?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, that`s exactly what that was. \uc2 University of Illinois\uc2  sophomore Evan Manivong told \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING the idea started out as a joke.\par \par \uc2 EVAN MANIVONG: I think I initially came up with it, just kind of gained traction in my head as the meet went along because we were having like a really fun time during the meet, we`re dancing, we had sticks and kind of all these fun celebrations. So, like, maybe I actually do this for a really, really fun celebration.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Evan earned a 14.75 for his vault and later tweeted out, "Go get vaccinated, everyone." Illinois is now headed to the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  championships next week. Pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And he did that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that he did it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I think we all feel like that, you know?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Want to go around?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah, you want to flash your vaccination card.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great advice not to, though, right? It`s got-- it`s got personal information.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Be-- be careful.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Don`t put it on social media--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --but next time I see somebody--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So true.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And as the nurse told me, don`t laminate it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He feels so grateful.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Or you might need a booster shot down the road.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Feels so grateful.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a good tip--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That is a big tip. All right. Thanks, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Brooke Baldwin, CNN anchor, will join us with her new book, "Huddle," to explain how women can tap into their collective power.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (8:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING.\par \par \uc2 It`s that time again, time for the stories that are the Talk of the Table this morning. Gayle King is going first.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. I`ve got an update on a very disturbing story that we reported-- that we told you about last week. You`ve seen the video. The elderly Asian woman who was attacked--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ooh. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --outside a New York City apartment building. It is very difficult to watch. This video comes from the lobby and it shows--this is the thing that bothers me--the two doormen who were standing there, they just watched this happen, and the thing that really got to me was that they closed the doors. Two-- look at these two guys.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They closed the doors and didn`t even try to help her. Well, we`re here to report to you today they`ve now been fired. That`s according to the building`s management company. Listen, I never like to see--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --anybody lose their job, but when you look at something like that, it`s the inhumanity that bothers me. We are now living in a time where we`ve got to be kind and we`ve got to help each other. And I get the reluct-- reluctance of--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --not wanting to intervene. But they didn`t call 911, they didn`t yell at the guy, they didn`t, you know, try to help the woman, bring her inside.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And they closed the door and not go out and help?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s the thing that bothered me. Well, they have now been fired by that management company.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And there`s a GoFund woman-- GoFund-- Fund to help the woman who was assaulted that day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hope she gets some help.\par \par \uc2 All right. Forbes is out with its latest list of billionaires. And the number of billionaires all around the world has increased during the pandemic. The company`s newest list has two thousand seven hundred and eighty-five, I think, billionaires. Fifty-five, in fact. I`m sure it will be eighty-five by the end of this read actually.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a thirty percent increase over the number a year ago, and I went through the list very carefully and I did not see my name on it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah, I checked. I`m not there either.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But it could be some sort of an error. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Either.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s no error. We`re not on it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Kim Karda--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But who`s on it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Here`s who is it on it, Kim Kardashian West, Tyler Perry--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --Apple CEO Tim Cook.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want to give people a feel for which is what a billion dollars is compared to what the typical person has.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So net worth for the average person is about a hundred thousand dollars. So if that person finds a twenty in like an old pair of pants or something or a jacket, you know that feeling of, like, feeling good?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s-- that`s nice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you see a twenty?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You find a twenty.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You find a twenty, I get excited.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For the billionaire--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --to feel equally excited based on their net worth--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Oh, my.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --they would have to find two hundred thousand dollars.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Petty cash. Petty cash for a billionaire. Also, Whitney Wolfe Herd, founder of Bum-- Bumble, is on there. I was really excited about that.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To see another woman joined the list.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s a--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nice. Nice, nice.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: --that`s a nice club to be in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All right. The Baylor Bears got a huge hometown welcome. The team returned to Waco, Texas, yesterday, after winning the men`s \uc2 NCAA\uc2  basketball championship. Local firefighters sprayed water over the planes, fans were there to greet the players come off. Baylor coach Scott Drew walked off holding the championship trophy. He received the key to the city, and play-- player-- players paused to sign autographs. I love what the coach said, he said, right now I just want to get my text messages under five hundred. They were above seven hundred, but when I think I`m making progress, I just get more. He deserves more.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, I`m so fascinated by this team. Dana Jacobson was telling us the coach is a really great guy--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --and that the team-- we interviewed the player yesterday--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --really, really nice people.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. Yeah. I hope they-- I know they`ll be celebrating for ages, and they deserve it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, very nice.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But-- but amazing game.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Sure did.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210408eh47000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Impact Of The Pandemic On Women In Work\b0\par\par\uc2 GAYLE KING \par \uc2 1,141 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 March 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now to our coverage of Women`s History Month. And this morning, we`re looking at the impact of the pandemic on women in work. Nearly three million women have left the workforce during the pandemic. Among working mothers, a survey shows that two in five have taken a step back from work or at least they`re considering it. Many cite childcare as the reason. Tami Burch-- she is from Chanute, Kansas-- is one of them. She`s a single mother who says she had no option but to leave her job because she could not afford childcare for her two children after school.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TAMI BURCH (Single Mother): (INDISTINCT).\par \par \uc2 It was the scariest thing I`ve ever done to leave my job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before the pandemic, Tami Burch was able to bring her ten-old twins to the assisted living facility where she worked.\par \par \uc2 TAMI BURCH: I had to quit because the facility went on lockdown. I couldn`t bring my kids to work anymore. And I didn`t have anybody for childcare. So, of course, I had to stay home with them. I`ve looked for temporary jobs online where I could work from home, but there really isn`t anything around here that I can do without college degree.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Kansas Department of Labor\uc2  unemployment benefits line.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Burch received unemployment benefits for several months. But they ended abruptly last October. She was told her account was flagged for fraud and provided all requested documents. Tami says the \uc2 Kansas Department of Labor\uc2  told her in January she still qualifies for benefits and that they are sorting it out. But she has still not received any new unemployment checks.\par \par \uc2 TAMI BURCH: I spend a good three, four hours on the phone every day, if not more. I have no money. I have zero dollars.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With no income or savings, Burch is now ten thousand dollars in debt.\par \par \uc2 TAMI BURCH: It`s hard for me to keep a smile on my face and to tell them everything`s going to be okay when I`m not sure myself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Burch continues to look for a job where she can work from home, but she doesn`t know what the future holds.\par \par \uc2 TAMI BURCH: I think it`s going to take quite a bit of time to recover from this. It`s going to be building from ground zero for me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: After CBS News contacted the \uc2 Kansas Department of Labor\uc2 , it called Tami and told her it has now filed all of her claims, which now paves the way for her to get all the money. Joining us now with some financial advices for wo-- financial advice is what I was actually trying to say-- for women like Tami, is CBS News business analyst, that`s \uc2 Jill Schlesinger\uc2 . Good morning to you, Jill. So--\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2  (CBS News Business Analyst): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --what`s the best use of the money, do you think, for people who are in this situation that Tami is in?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : You know, I think about these women who are really struggling, and I say the most important thing you can do is save any money. And we know that big checks are going to start flowing in. Fourteen hundred dollars stimulus checks, childcare tax credits. That money`s going to come in, so do what most Americans have been doing with their excess cash. They`ve been saving it. Last month, Gayle, we saw the savings rate pop up over twenty percent. You wind the clock back maybe ten, fifteen years, nobody was saving a dime. So please, save, save, save, before you start paying those bills. Make it really safe.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sometimes it`s so hard to save, Jill, when you don`t have the money to save. So I`m looking at women who are thinking about leaving the job or considering it at some point, what do you think they should be thinking about financially?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : I think that the best plan of action is to really detail what your expenses are. So this can happen suddenly. We know that. So even if you have a job, you really should be looking at what are my essential things that I have to pay. And you know what`s essential, food, everything else is negotiable. But, you know, the categories include food, and shelter, and utilities. You heard Tami say she accumulated debt. A lot of people will get some money, maybe from unemployment, they`ll think I should pay down my debt, no. You need to keep that money safe, and you`re really looking at food first. Everything else is negotiable. And remember, you can never hide. In a situation where you`ve lost your job suddenly, you`ve got to notify every single person that`s out there. Whether you owe money to them, a utility provider, a landlord, get on the record and say COVID has impacted my life. And this is whether you leave voluntarily or involuntarily. Remember, Tami is actually really classified as an-- as a voluntary, but she doesn`t feel that way. She had no one to take care of her kids. So she has to really notify everyone out there.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Obviously, Jill, people are really worried about health care in this pandemic. If you`ve lost your job, what are your health care options?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, you know, with this new bill that was just passed, I want people to pay attention to the Affordable Care Act. For the next two years, premiums are capped at eight and a half percent of your modified adjusted gross income. That means way more people are going to qualify for the Affordable Care Act. Also remember, you`ll get more tax credits. There is, of course, Medicaid if you need it. And as we know, there is COBRA. COBRA is existing coverage which the government will pay for from April through September this year. So three important programs. Check them out.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All right. Jill, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, 48 HOURS investigates the case of a nurse accused of murdering her millionaire husband.\par \par \uc2 But first, a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210314eh3c000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Working Mothers Disproportionately Impacted By Pandemic\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Nikki Battiste \par \uc2 776 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, we have an exclusive new report from the National Women`s Law Center. Women have lost a generation of gains in the workforce, and more than two in five of the women`s jobs that were lost during the pandemic have yet to return. One of the reasons many women have made the tough decision to stop working is child care. CBS` Nikki Battiste reports in our series Women & the Pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE JOHNSON: So can you tell mommy what did you do?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: A lunchtime break in the park would have been unthinkable a year ago for forty-two-year-old Nicole Johnson and her daughter, Khloe.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE JOHNSON: You know you can`t scoot without me.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: That`s because Johnson worked as a full-time teaching assistant in Scarsdale, New York. But when the pandemic shuttered her school, she was out of the job, surviving on unemployment.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE JOHNSON: I was crying. I was literally crying all the time.\par \par \uc2 What number goes here?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Then in the fall of last year, just as Khloe was starting second grade remotely, Johnson was offered her job back.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE JOHNSON: I`m a single mom. I cannot leave a six-year-old home by herself. So, it wasn`t a choice.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Could you have afforded child care for Khloe?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE JOHNSON: No. That would take away what I need to take care of my necessity bills.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: A report released today exclusively to CBS News shows the devastating economic toll COVID is taking on women, who lost almost a million more jobs than men, with working mothers more likely to have jobs impacted by the pandemic, especially those in education, hospitality and retail.\par \par \uc2 FATIMA GOSS GRAVES: That`s been more true for black women and for Latina women who are more likely to be front-line workers.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Fatima Goss Graves is the president of the National Women`s Law Center.\par \par \uc2 FATIMA GOSS GRAVES: We have never been tasked with the really impossible thing of working and caregiving, and in some cases home schooling, all at the same time.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Do you think working moms will bounce back in the years to come?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE JOHNSON: I think it`s going to take time.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Johnson says once she can send her daughter back to school, she`ll be ready to send out her resume.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Battiste, CBS News, White Plains, New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And there is still much more news ahead on tonight`s CBS EVENING NEWS. A mudslide forces hundreds from their homes in Southern California. Look at this, a frightening scene on live TV as part of the set crashes on top of an anchor.\par \par \uc2 And a former college football player makes the save of the day, smashing a windshield with his bare hands.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A mudslide today forced hundreds of people from their homes in Southern California. Heavy rain caused the ground to give way in a neighborhood that was hit by wildfires late last year. Several vehicles were engulfed by the mud, but no serious injuries are reported.\par \par \uc2 And an \uc2 ESPN\uc2  anchor in Columbia might have to put in for hazard pay. In the middle of a live broadcast, a set wall crashed right on top of him. His co- anchors were startled-- startled and immediately went to commercial. We are happy to say the anchor later tweeted he was fine. No broken bones, just a few bruises. We checked our walls are safe here.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a former college football player is being called a hero for rescuing a woman who was trapped inside a truck that flipped on its side. Cell phone video shows Nicholas Lee smashing the windshield with his feet and bare hands to free the woman. The former defensive lineman says he`s now considering work in the medical field or as a firefighter. He`d be a great firefighter.\par \par \uc2 All right. Coming up next, a young student shares her experience battling a terrible illness. What she wrote is helping others facing a similar fight.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020210311eh3a000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Workers At An Amazon Warehouse Near Alabama Are Voting On Whether To Join A Retail Workers Union, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Anthony Mason, Mark Strassmann \par \uc2 5,057 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 March 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Hundreds of thousands of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees are watching one of the most significant unionization drives in a generation. Workers at an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse near Birmingham, Alabama, are voting on whether to join a retail workers union. Their colleagues around the country are wondering how that will affect them. Mark Strassmann is at that Fulfillment Center in Bessemer, Alabama, where he spoke to some of the workers. Mark, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (CBS News Senior National Correspondent): Good morning, Anthony. In two shifts a day here, fifty-eight hundred workers sort, package and ship the sort of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  boxes that many of us get deliver-- delivered at home. They make more than fifteen dollars an hour, which is more than double the minimum wage. But some of them tell us the company refuses to hear their complaints and frustrations.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Michael Foster`s a union organizer, and early this morning we found him outside the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse at shift change where he`s campaigned for votes since last October.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL FOSTER (Union Organizer): \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is treating the people disrespectful. They are basically telling the people, if you don`t want to abide by the way we work, you can go find another job somewhere else.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: According to the union, roughly eighty-five percent of the workers at this massive warehouse are black. Most are women. Many complain about grueling work, unsafe conditions, with inadequate bathroom and meal breaks.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER BATES (\uc2 Amazon\uc2  Employee): We`re being treated like we`re prisoners who`s there to get a job done.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Jennifer Bates and Darryl Richardson both work at the warehouse.\par \par \uc2 What`s your biggest frustration?\par \par \uc2 DARRYL RICHARDSON (\uc2 Amazon\uc2  Employee): Job security, respect and safe--just-- just-- just safety, wellbeing.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER BATES: What the community doesn`t realize is what`s going on behind the curtain. What are the people there going through just to make sure that we`re getting our packages?\par \par \uc2 DARRYL RICHARDSON: It`s time for us to make a stand. It`s time for some changes.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Frustrated workers contacted the Retail Wholesale and Department Store Union. Celebrities like Danny Glover support them.\par \par \uc2 DANNY GLOVER (Actor): \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  said the best anti-poverty program he knew of was the union. And that still remains so more than sixty years after he made that statement.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And this week, President Biden weighed in.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Every worker should have a free and fair choice to join a union.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But they are \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , and this is Alabama. The e-commerce behemoth is fighting the union drive in this right-to-work state. In a statement, the company told us, "If the union vote passes, it will impact everyone at the site and it`s important associates understand what that means for them and their day-to-day life working at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 ." \uc2 Amazon\uc2  worker Dawn Hoag will vote no.\par \par \uc2 DAWN HOAG (\uc2 Amazon\uc2  Employee): I have yet to personally encounter anyone that is pro-union. I do not see a point in paying somebody to do something I am fully capable to do for myself--and that being advocating.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But the union believes these workers need a voice, and they`ll deliver the votes despite long odds.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL FOSTER: If you put enough heart in anything that you do, you can do anything.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Even in Alabama?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL FOSTER: Even in Alabama. We have not been able to organize in Alabama as we wish we could, but things are meant to change.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: This is a vote by mail and will go on until March 29th. The union expects this to be a close vote. But whichever side wins, the loser could challenge the outcome. Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. Mark, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Very consequential vote. I understand--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --not understanding why you`re paying somebody and what are they going to go do for you. But you realized what they may do for you when you need them and they are not there.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. I don`t-- I don`t know the right answer here, to union or not to unionize but they are certainly raising some very troubling issues that I think need to be addressed.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. It`s-- and as we pointed out at the beginning, it`s very-- it`s-- we haven`t seen a unionization battle of this significant--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: --in quite a long time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know, union membership had been declining for years.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yep.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It may be back on the uptick. All right. Mark, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to the \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING podcast. You`ll get the day`s top stories in less than twenty minutes.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`ll look at what could be the future of tourism--vaccine passports. Why some fear it could lead to a new type of discrimination.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This morning, governments and businesses are considering a way for people to prove they`ve been vaccinated against COVID-19. They are called vaccine passports, and it could determine who can travel, stay at a hotel, or even visit a bar. Now Israel`s already using them, and the \uc2 European Union\uc2  just proposed them. The U.S., we`re still thinking about it. Roxana Saberi is in London with more on the story. Good morning to you, Roxana. Here is a question, what are the pros and the cons of this idea?\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI (CBS News Foreign Correspondent): Good morning, Gayle. Well, supporters say it could help lift lockdowns in cities like London. You get a shot, you get a digital pass on your phone, and then you can enter restaurants and pubs. But critics warn of the potential pitfalls over privacy and more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Man #1 speaking foreign language)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Israelis must now scan in to get in to gyms, hotels, even concerts like this.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: It is all set in the telephone. It`s very convenient.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: For more than three million Israelis and counting, this is the ticket back to some kind of normalcy.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Finally, all the way in the car, I sing (SINGING).\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The government-issued green pass shows personal details and proves they`ve had two doses of a COVID-19 vaccine. Now countries across Europe and beyond are considering similar so-called vaccine passports or immunity certificates, to inject life back into a flagging tourism industry and revive businesses and hospitality like pubs. The U.K. is studying whether to introduce vaccine certificates, raising the possibility that Brits could soon need a passport for a pint.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Yep, all good.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2  (U.K. Prime Minister): We haven`t had stuff like this before. We`ve never thought in terms of having something that you have to show to go to, you know, a pub or a theater. That and-- and so there are deep and complex issues that we need to explore.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Issues like potential discrimination against people who don`t want shot, along with those who can`t get one for medical reasons or because there`s not enough supply.\par \par \uc2 CLARE WENHAM: So these people won`t be able to participate in life, in public life, in social life, in economic life, in the same way as their counterparts who have been vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Clare Wenham from the \uc2 London School of Economics\uc2  says data privacy is another concern.\par \par \uc2 CLARE WENHAM: Is it going to be something that`s digital that tracks you? And that obviously opens a whole different range of conversations about, you know, security of your data, for example.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: So, governments shouldn`t rush it?\par \par \uc2 CLARE WENHAM: Governments shouldn`t rush it. I think it`s a Pandora`s box and think it`s a slippery slope into, you know, having life being governed by your health status.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: But as governments debate immunity certificates, businesses are pushing ahead. Some cruises and airlines, for instance, say they`ll require proof of inoculation to board. So while these passes are raising tricky ethical questions