ous.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we`re unanimous.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where-- and where did you get your PhD? Same place I did?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right. Exactly, right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Up next, Vlad Duthiers will be to-- bringing you the stories you`ll be talking about today.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is seven forty-two, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, I`m a little overshadowed by that Oprah, Meghan and Harry promo.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait until Sunday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, I`ll follow that up.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Me neither. Me neither.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mister Duthiers is here with What to Watch. And I have an old school toss from Jack Reynolds (ph), a viewer, who says Vlad Duthiers should win a Golden Globe for best handsome newscaster.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Is that even a category?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I didn`t know that was a category.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Maybe on Bizarro World, Jack.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like-- I like that it`s not most handsome.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s the best of the handsomest.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Have a very long list of people to thank starting with my ancestors.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s nice.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you very much for that, Jack.\par \par \uc2 Here are a few stories we think you`ll be talking about today. Books that have been pulled from the Dr. Seuss catalog are now selling for thousands of dollars online. "And to Think That I Saw It on Mulberry Street" and "If I Ran the Zoo" are on the list of six books by the legendary author that won`t be published anymore. Dr. Seuss Enterprises says it made the decision last year after reviewing the catalog of titles with a panel of experts. The books` illustrations contain racist stereotypes of Asian, Arab, and African people. Copies of the book, though, are selling for big money.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One hardback copy is going for, get this, ten grand on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . The Universal Studios resort in Orlando is now evaluating part of its Seuss Landing attraction that referenced some of the books being pulled.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. You know, when I first heard it initially, I went, come on, it`s "Green Eggs and Ham"--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --the-- the cat-- "The Cat in the Hat" because I love Dr. Seuss. But then, guys, when you look at the books, I have to say I did wince a few times when I was looking through it. I don`t know why we can`t just rewrite it or just put it in its proper context.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, so a couple of things. One, it`s the Dr. Seuss Enterprises themselves--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --who chose to pull these books--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Uh-Huh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --after they consulted with--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --a group of educators.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And these are some of the, sort of, lesser-known titles.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was going to say--\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Six titles--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --out of something like forty-plus books--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: --that he wrote over the course of his career.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. But when you hear Dr. Seuss, you go, no, no, no, no, no.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because we all 