r hit song, "Jolene." Dolly Parton donated one million dollars last year, folks, for research to develop the Moderna vaccine. She said this rhyming message is for anyone who is still on the fence about getting vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 DOLLY PARTON (DollyParton/\uc2 Instagram\uc2 ): Don`t be such a chicken squat. Get out there and get your shot.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Don`t be a chicken squat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hear you, Dolly.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Vlad, please, come on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Don`t be a chicken squat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. And her next line, Vlad, she goes because-- "vaccine, because once you`re dead, then it`s a bit too late." She`s right.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She said I`m old enough to get it. I`m smart to get-- enough to get it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, JPMorgan Chase CEO \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (8:00 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s Wednesday, March 3, 2021. Hump day. Wake up, everybody. Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. I am Gayle King with Tony Dokoupil and Anthony Mason.\par \par \uc2 President Biden says a vaccine rollout is accelerating. How we`re getting there with government help and a private sector team of rivals.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: COVID economic relief on a massive scale could soon be coming if it can get through Congress. JPMorgan Chase CEO \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  will talk about that and a new way to help minority-owned businesses.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Plus, Golden Globe winner Daniel Kaluuya and director Shaka King on their new film Judas and the Black Messiah. What the story of Black Panther`s Fred Hampton says about racism today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s in conversation.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s Eye Opener @ eight o`clock.\par \par \uc2 President Biden now says there will be enough doses for every adult in America by the end of May.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Texas is one of at least eleven states that are now easing restrictions despite those federal guidelines.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Here in Columbus, they reportedly got three hundred doses yesterday. And they said let`s do a trial run right away.\par \par \uc2 MAN (\uc2 The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center\uc2 ): Three, two, one, vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Lower courts had said that these two laws disproportionately affect voters of color. The hearing about them revealed one of the reasons why Democrats and Republicans keep fighting over these kinds of laws.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL: Dr. Seuss Enterprises announced that six of his books will no longer be published because they contain racist and insensitive imagery.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: "If you find that your book shelf just got a little bit duller, consider these kids` books from people of color. There`s a whole range of books that will make you feel merry, like there one called Hair Love by Matthew A. Cherry. There are so many stories, a whole great assorty, it`s fun to read books written after the `40s.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that. And we know Hair Love. Remember Matthew Cherry was here.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: We do and we love it. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I will say Teddy--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s a great idea.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --Teddy-- my Teddy rejects Dr. Seuss on a nightly basis.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He won`t read "The Cat in the Hat." And, you know, two-year- olds are not great literary critics, he just throws it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Really?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He says, get it out of the bed.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Very interesting.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah, it`s interesting. "Oh, the Places You`ll Go," though, he likes that.\par \par \uc2 All right. We begin-- hopeful news, President Biden says there should be enough COVID vaccine doses to inoculate every American adult by the end of May, about two months sooner than expected. And a big part of the reason is a new partnership between health care giants \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  and Merck. The federal government is investing a hundred and five million dollars to upgrade Merck facilities to produce the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  single-shot vaccine. The White House is saying this partnership will lead to a hundred million doses by the end of May.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : This is a wartime effort, and every action has been on the table including putting together breakthrough approaches. This is the type of collaboration between companies we saw in World War II.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Meanwhile, as early as today, the Senate could begin debating a Democratic stimulus plan that would send fourteen-hundred-dollar checks to millions of Americans. And Majority Leader Chuck Schumer says they do have the votes to pass the bill.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nearly ninety percent of small businesses say that sales are still below what they were before the pandemic, and that`s according to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . And more Asian, black, or Hispanic owners say their company`s finances are poor or fair when you compare them to white business owners. \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  recently pledged three hundred and fifty million dollars to help grow small businesses run by minority owners. It`s all part of the bank`s larger thirty-billion-dollar, that`s with a B, commitment to help address the racial wealth divide in this country. \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  is the chairman and CEO of JPMorgan Chase and joins us to-- to discuss. Good to see you, \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 . I love the numbers. They are very impressive, three hundred and fifty million. Good to see you. Thirty billion-- and now this new commitment includes forty million for over-- forty million for your Entrepreneurs of Color Fund. First, start us off by telling us why this initiative is important to you personally.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2  (JPMorgan Chase Chairman and CEO): Yeah. So, Gayle, welcome. Thrilled to be here. You know, look, we have-- we saw COVID. We saw the murder of George Floyd. We`ve had racial inequality in this country--and this highlighted it--for a hundred years. And I think it`s time for all of us to double down and try to help the rest of the community. Small business black entrepreneurs, Latinx entrepreneurs, Asian entrepreneurs, they need a little more money. They need a little more help, a little more consulting. So we`ve learned a lot as we`ve tried to help businesses in Detroit, which I remember talking to you about once. You know, we went into Detroit, and we helped a hundred small businesses grow. And so this effort you`re talking about today is simply taking that to Chicago, L.A., South Bronx, all around the country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Expanding it. So exactly how does it work, Jamie?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2 : Well, we go to a local community. We work with local people, like within Detroit with Kresge, and we make available capital to, you know, sometimes it`s a grant and sometimes it`s a loan, but to a small business. So like in Detroit, there were restaurants. There was a lady who started a gym. They wanted to expand. There was a contractor who wanted to expand who had-- had a government contract, but they needed the capital to finance it. If you want to do business with the government, you have to show that you have the wherewithal. And so we went in, and we got them the help they need. It might be a lawyer. It might be an accountant, but it certainly was a loan. It helped them expand. And it`s a wonderful thing, and a lot of those business members, they hire locally. So, it helps the community twice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2 : They expand as a business, and they hire locally.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Jamie, it`s Anthony. I-- I wanted to ask you about the-- the banking business, which-- which is not known for its diversity. What are you doing at \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  to ensure diversity in the company?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2 : Yeah. So, we`ve-- we`ve been working on diversity for a long time. In fact, we`ve doubled down there, too. So like black EDs and MDs in this company are up fifty percent in the last three years. Hiring, training, recruiting, half my direct reports are women. And we just had a woman who was a fabulous woman who was running our whole retail system as part of my management committee we just-- left to run another company. But we`re-- we`re doing everything we can with this company to help the black community.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: You were part of a group of business leaders that met with President Biden and Vice President Harris and Treasury Secretary Yellen recently. Did you talk about the-- the Democrats` 1.9-trillion-dollar stimulus bill, and how do you feel about it? Do you think it`s enough? Do you think it`s too much?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2 : Yeah, so we did. So, first of all, I was grateful the President and the vice president had us in. There were four CEOs in the room. We spent almost two hours, and we actually covered a lot of ground from the-- from that bill to immigration to taxation to-- and I-- and I love the fact he brought up American competitiveness. We need to make sure America is competitive for the next hundred years and not just to get through COVID. So, I`ll leave the bill itself. We need more help for those who really need it, unemployed, mothers who had to leave the workforce to go home to take care of family members or children, small businesses who, you know, really need the help to get through this. And I`ll leave the details to Democrats or Republicans to debate. But there`s a lot of stimulus. It`s going to get us through this, and thank God, we have this vaccine. So, I`m hoping when we all get to June and the flowers are coming out and people are getting their shots that we can get back to a normal life. And the stimulus will lead, in my view, to a very, very strong economy late this year and into next year and maybe even in to 2023.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jamie, you certainly have a reputation of playing nicely with others. Do you find a difference in working with this administration compared to the last administration?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2 : Yeah. Well, Gayle, my-- you know, my job is to the best I can for my company and my country. And I`m a patriot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2 : And I will work with any president to do the best I can for-- for America. So, you know, to me it`s always good when politicians speak to each other. You know, look, you know, my friend and your friend, Vernon Jordan--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2 : --boy, was one of the great human beings of all time. And what did we learn from him is talk to other people--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yep.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2 : --treat them with respect, you know, get into the details. You know, try to get everyone advancing in society.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, I like what you just said about being a patriot. There were rumors on the streets that you were thinking about running for President in 2020. Does President Dimon have a ring to you down the road?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2 : I-- I`m-- I-- I-- I`m definitely--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I-- I-- I-- I--\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2 : --not going to run for president.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s the answer, \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2 : I`m not going to run for President.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Well, that`s emphatic. \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us for this morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All right. Ahead, Pulitzer Prize-winning author Viet Thanh Nguyen talks about his family`s harrowing escape from Vietnam and how his own refugee story shaped his new novel.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s eight-oh-eight, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Actor Daniel Kaluuya just won a Golden Globe for playing Black Panther leader Fred Hampton in the movie Judas and the Black Messiah.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL KALUUYA (#goldenglobes): And I hope generations after this can see how brilliant he thought, how brilliantly he spoke and how brilliantly he loved.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very good point. Coming up, we`ll talk with him and the film`s director, Shaka King, about how they say they are trying to correct the narrative around the Black Panthers.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. We thank you for that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210304eh330008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Research From 2019 Found Black Americans Only Made Up 3.2 Percent Of Senior Leadership Roles At Large Companies, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 5,728 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 February 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As a part of our Black History Month coverage, \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING is taking a look at the challenges Black Americans face when trying to find a job or climb the corporate ladder. Research from 2019 found Black Americans only made up 3.2 percent of senior leadership roles at large companies. And according to a new \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  survey, eighty-one percent of Black professionals say seeing other Black people in positions of leadership would make their workplace feel more inclusive. We spoke with Zachary, a consultant, and Shinique, a marriage and family therapist, about what they say their experiences have been with racial inequity at work.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHINIQUE CHARLI WRIGHT (WrightNOW counseling): It seems like it`s a little bit difficult for people to either remember my name or pronounce it correctly or remember to pronounce it correctly. And so they would often ask for, well, what`s your middle name? What`s your-- you know, do you have a nickname? Which definitely makes me feel ashamed of my name at times because it seems like I`m not good enough or I`m stereotyped.\par \par \uc2 ZACHARY NUNN (Living Corporate Founder): As a young Black man in corporate America, folks have said all types of things about me. I`ve been told I`m too arrogant, too aggressive, sometimes not aggressive enough; too proactive, not proactive enough.\par \par \uc2 SHINIQUE CHARLI WRIGHT: Looking for a job in the corporate world, I had two resumes, both resumes were the same except one resume had Shinique, the other resume had Charli. If I put about ten resumes out, about seven came back for Charli. Seven phone calls to offer a job or offer an interview came back for Charli, and about two came back for Shinique.\par \par \uc2 ZACHARY NUNN: I was gunning for a promotion, and I was doing my thing, now look, I`m killing it, right. I`m actually doing all types of work, working in and outside of my business unit. I am doing above and beyond. I find out I got a White counterpart, he quits close to the top of the year, and then he comes back right before the promotion decisions are determined. And that guy got promoted with me. So equal outcomes, but effort was not equal. What I also realized is I`m not the only person out there experiencing this. I created Living Corporate, a digital media network that centers and amplifies Black and Brown folks at work. Gestures are cool, days off are cool, but the reality is if you`re not letting Black and Brown folks make actual decisions that can impact systemic change, you`re not doing anything.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hm.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  career expert Andrew McCaskill joins us now with more on race and inequity in the workplace. It`s all part of our partnership with \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 . Andrew, good morning to you. I want to get to the resume issue because it`s really fascinating and disturbing. But I want to start with your reporting. So you also spoke with Black professionals about what they face in the workplace. What did you learn, and what needs to change?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCASKILL (LinkedIn Career Expert): A lot needs to change. What we learned was that Black professionals overwhelmingly saw that-- responded that things that were barriers to their advancement were not seeing people who look like them at top and senior leadership, not having access to mentorship and sponsorship, not feeling a sense of belonging in the workplace. And finally just feeling like that there was no path for them for advancement. All of those things culminate to-- to Black professionals feeling like that there`s a more opportunity outside of the workforce and working for themselves than working for companies and corporations.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So let`s talk about this resume issue. We heard Shinique say that when she sends out her resume with the name Charli on top it, Charli Wright, she gets seven callbacks. If she sends out the same resume-- resume with Shinique right on it, she only gets two. And that actually matches up with your findings and with other people`s research. What the heck is going there?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCASKILL: What happens is-- is oftentimes unconscious bias is-- is in place. Sometimes just actual bias is in place. But people with equal qualifications and skills don`t always get equal outcomes. And the unfortunate fact is that a lot of professionals are starting to say-- feel like that they have to whiten their resume or remove racial descriptors from their resumes. I went to a historically Black college. I went to Morehouse College. I can`t imagine having to-- thinking I have to take that off my resume. But the onus is on the corporations to put things in place to make sure that racial discrimination for-- in their hiring process doesn`t creep in. And you have to be intentional about it. It doesn`t just happen organically.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. So let`s-- let`s talk about the intentions because I love Zachary and I love Shinique. I thought they raised really great points. So what should employers do to put that in place and be intentional? Give us two top things they could do today.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCASKILL: Today is they can do blind recruitment where they look in at the-- in the hiring process and start to just only look at qualifications and skills. The other thing that they can do is that they have to be-- hold themselves accountable. They need to start to measure and think about the number of people from underrepresented groups who are applying for roles and opportunities, and then juxtapose that to the number of folks who actually get callbacks and get hired in the end. This is not the responsibility of the job seeker. It requires extra effort for inclusion, and that has to be something that everybody at the corporation is willing to-- to go along with. It requires extra effort.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, they also talk about fear of retaliation, Black people in the workplace, when they speak up about it. So, you know, you`re looked as complaining, or causing troubling. What other obstacles do they face and how to navigate that?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCASKILL: There-- there are so many other obstacles that they face. But I think, Gayle, that one in four Black respondents said that they fear retaliation. And so that means that we have to have allies in this process, is that if you are a leader, you should be talking about inclusion because that sets the tone for culture. People are not leaving-- they`re just not leaving their jobs, they`re leaving toxic cultures, and they`re leaving the-- the-- they`re not wanting to have to deal with things like microaggressions at work or feeling like there`s hopelessness at work. So one thing that people can do is if you`re a leader, talk about this. Have the hard conversations. It shouldn`t just be Black people and people of color or people who are other talking about inequities at work. Because one in four Black folks said that they felt like that they would receive retaliation when they talk about inequities.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCASKILL: So we need our non-Black counterparts to jump in and talk about it, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. May be a hard conversation, but it`s a necessary conversation. Drew McCaskill, good to see you. Thank you so much for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, a new apology from country star Morgan Wallen after he was recorded using a racial slur. Why he`s telling his fans not-- not to defend him.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN WALLEN (Country Singer): I let so many people down and who mean a lot to me and who have given so much to me, and it`s just not fair. I`ve got many more things to learn. But I already know that I don`t want to add to any division. This week was a big lesson that sometimes we can do just that without even knowing it. Our actions matter. Our words matter. And I just want to encourage anyone watching to please learn from my mistake. There`s no reason to downplay what I did.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That was country singer Morgan Wallen apologizing and asking fans not to defend his use of a racial slur caught on camera last week. In an \uc2 Instagram\uc2  video posted last night, the country star says he used the language at the end of a seventy-two hour bender. He says he`s now been sober for nine days, and that he`s accepted invitations to be educated by Black leaders since the TMZ video surfaced of him using a racial slur. Wallen`s music has been pulled from radio stations, and he`s been suspended by his record label. But we should note his actual sales soared after this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: His record sales are going up. And I think that he wants people to know he doesn`t want to be rewarded for bad behavior.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s why he`s saying, okay, you know, you-- you may-- buying the music-- he doesn`t want people to think that`s an endorsement of his--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --of his bad behavior.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: There`s been obviously a lot of conversation about this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And there were some people saying you should forgive him and other people saying, wait, it`s too soon. And he-- he`s sort of been saying the same thing here, it`s too soon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s too soon.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I, you know, I need-- I`m in the learning process.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why are people rushing out to buy the records? That`s-- that`s sort of a disturbing--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because they`re trying to show that they support him. And, you know, not for nothing, his music is good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I-- I like Morgan Wallen`s music. And I think he wants to-- but he does want to send the message that this is not okay, what I did was not okay.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. And I think that`s important at this point.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. I`m glad he`s taking that tactic. And he-- he admits he`s got a lot of learning and growing up to do.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we will meet the woman who is set to become the youngest female CEO of a public company today. Wait until you hear her age. We`re going to talk with Bumble founder--this is Whitney Wolfe Herd--about the dating site and why she says the last year has been, well, the most challenging of her life for many reasons.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (8:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. It`s time to bring you some of the stories that we call Talk of the Table. And, Anthony, you`re up first.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. Remember that new \uc2 Jeep\uc2  commercial we saw in the Super Bowl featuring Bruce Springsteen?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I loved that ad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Loved it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Well, it is off the air after it was revealed he was recently arrested for alleged drunken driving. The \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  confirms Springsteen was arrested November 14th inside the Gateway National Recreation Area in New Jersey. He was issued citations for driving under the influence, reckless driving, and consuming alcohol in a closed area. A park spokes-- spokesperson said Springsteen was very cooperative. That \uc2 Jeep\uc2  ad with Springsteen aired first the first time, of course, during the Super Bowl. It got a very strong response from viewers. Springsteen is due to appear before a judge via-- via video conference later this month. But there`s been no comment from-- from the Springsteen camp. But the \uc2 Asbury Park Press\uc2  has reported that Springsteen`s blood alcohol content was .02, which is a quarter of the--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And this is not like--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: --that`s like a like a quarter of the legal limit in-- in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So there`s a lot of questions about why he was actually arrested here. He has no prior DWIs, traffic, or parking tickets on record. So there-- there are a lot of questions. If his blood alcohol level was low, that`s like one drink.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. And he`s also, as we know, not an irresponsible person.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But when you hear arrested for, you know, driving under the influence, your mind goes all the way to Z--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --and then you hear the details and you say--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --wait.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But then it says reckless driving, but he`s in this--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --national park.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah, yeah. So there are a lot of questions there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot of questions.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But the-- the upshot is for the moment, that \uc2 Jeep\uc2  ad is off the air.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. We are, what, almost a year now into the pandemic, into working from home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And apparently we have not figured out the Zoom things, whatever they call them. Because there`s another video conference glitch, and it has gone viral. So look what happened when Minnesota Congressman Tim Emmer spoke to a House Committee yesterday.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: We must make sure that our nation`s sole proprietors and the smallest of small businesses receive timely--\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXINE WATERS: Will the gentleman suspend? I`m sorry. Mister Emmer?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXINE WATERS: Are you okay?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: I am.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You`re upside down, Tom.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: I-- I don`t know how to fix that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "I don`t know how to fix that." So that`s Maxine Waters who is chair of the House Financial Services Committee. And that is not the Wizard of Oz. That is a representative from the fine state of Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s how I feel almost every day now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If the-- the tape played just a second longer, one of his colleagues yelled out, at least you`re not a cat.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great. Love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think we have to have young people in the room, because every-- all the people of a certain age, myself included, go, I don`t know how to fix that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the congressman said, I turned it on and I turned it off, but it`s still the same.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know how that works. So when you`re on it, do you not know you`re upside down?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I don`t know-- I-- I think you should be able to see that. I mean, I see my own (INDISTINCT) on Zoom.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was funny.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a wink--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s-- he`s okay. He`s okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah. He`s fine.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have a follow-up to the story we told you about yesterday, remember that Indiana woman who had a wonderful reaction--I love this--when dozens of runaway cows ran past her car on the highway? So here`s a clip from yesterday and our discussion.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA KUCZMANSKI (Yesterday; internet video): Oh, my gosh. No, this is fabulous.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING (Yesterday): This is one of those ones where you wish you could see their face.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because she goes, this is fabulous.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to know what she looks like. That was nice.\par \par \uc2 Be careful what you ask for because guess what we received yesterday, a video yesterday. And here`s a face behind that voice.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA KUCZMANSKI: Everybody`s calling me now because I`m the cow lady. And now after your rendition of my voice this morning, I`m Fran Drescher. So anyway, thank you for such a great clip. It was fabulous.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She got--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --she got a kick-- her name is Melissa Kuczmanski. I hope I`m saying that right, Melissa. Because it just shows you, because in my mind I pictured her being kind of grandma-like--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --or Aunt Clara-like or Aunt Esther-like.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Nope.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t-- but she`s young, she`s fun. She`s got a great sense of humor.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, does she work the-- by the hour for us? Because what I loved is she putting out into the universe--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --a desire to see wildlife.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Then the cows came.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So apparently her thoughts are very powerful.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like Melissa Kuczmanski. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (Cross-talking)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you for watching. I`m glad she saw it.\par \par \uc2 So our next guest leads the dating app that famously puts women in charge of making the first move. Now that company`s taking a bold new step in the business world. This morning, Bumble launches its initial public offering on the NASDAQ stock exchange. The platform has forty-two million active users. Today`s IPO could bring the company a valuation of up to eight billion, that`s with a B, dollars. It will make Bumble`s founder, Whitney Wolfe Herd is her name, the youngest female CEO of a public company right now. How old is Whitney Wolfe Herd? She`s thirty-one. She joins us now for an interview that you`re seeing first on \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. Whitney Wolfe Herd, congratulations to you. I think, okay, not only are you the youngest- - the youngest CEO, but you`re also by the end of the day, you could be a billionaire, Whitney. So did you sleep last night? I`m wondering what you`re feeling right now. Did you get a good night`s sleep?\par \par \uc2 WHITNEY WOLFE HERD (Bumble Founder and CEO): Well, I have a fourteen-month- old, Gayle. So I did not sleep that well. But we`re really excited this morning. And it`s so great to be with you. I wish I can`t be there in person.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I wish so too because, Whitney, I remember, 2014, let`s go back. You said it was a very dark place. You were going some-- through some things personally and professionally. And now you`ve clearly come out on the other side. What are you thinking about today, about your journey to get from there to here?\par \par \uc2 WHITNEY WOLFE HERD: Today is a remarkable day for me, in the sense that, you know, there was a time that I thought my career was over in 2014. And if I can sit in this seat today, it just goes to show that anything is possible, and anybody can rebuild themselves, just starting over, taking that first step, making the first move. And they can reinvent themselves when they go through hardship.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Without going into all the details, why did you think your career could possibly have been over in 2014? Just give us the cliff note version.\par \par \uc2 WHITNEY WOLFE HERD: Oh, I had left my previous company. And, you know, all of a sudden, I was being harassed on the internet by complete strangers. And I lost all of my confidence. But what I realized was you can lose confidence, but you can never kill ambition. And I was able to get back up and try to turn my-- my pain into something purposeful. And that led to the creation of Bumble.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said after today, Bumble, it will still be business as usual. How is that going to be possible for you, Whitney?\par \par \uc2 WHITNEY WOLFE HERD: We are so focused on our commitment to our community and to our mission and to driving a good and ethical business. And we are just going to stay committed to that. We are going to really focus on the long term.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, Whitney, I mean, a stock IPO is about investment in today`s company but also growth. One of the things I was interested to learn Bumble does is networking for business. And I got to say, if my wife caught me on Bumble and I said networking, that would not be an acceptable answer. So please explain.\par \par \uc2 WHITNEY WOLFE HERD: Well, so what`s interesting about Bumble is we have a bunch of accountability features to allow you to use the product for more than just dating. We actually allow you to disable the dating feature. And it`s time stamped for accountability so that your partner or anybody that you might want to show that you`re really using the product for something platonically or professionally will know that you`re doing just that. So we really engineer accountability into our product and allow you to meet new people, no matter who you`re looking for, even beyond dating.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But, Whitney, let`s talk about dating for a second because we had a Match.com adviser on yesterday, Helen Fisher, who told us that \uc2 Match.com\uc2  had seen a dating renaissance during the pandemic, which surprised a lot of us. Are you seeing the same thing?\par \par \uc2 WHITNEY WOLFE HERD: We have seen some really remarkable shifts in behavior through the pandemic. One of the most profound is slow dating, where people are really taking time to get to know each other digitally first before venturing into the-- to the real world. And, of course, this was really imposed due to the quarantines. But something quite beneficial came out of this, that people are really getting to know each other on a deeper level and understanding if they`re compatible before they go and meet a stranger in real life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So when you started Bumble, Whitney, those years ago, were you hoping to go public? Was that your goal when you first started this company?\par \par \uc2 WHITNEY WOLFE HERD: Our goal has always been to just build a really meaningful business brand that creates impact for our customers and changes the paradigm of how we connect. And the fact that we`ve made it this far is a remarkable feat for us. But we see this as day one of-- of the next chapter. And we`re so excited for what`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Another remarkable thing, Whitney, is-- is the tech industry is not known for having women out front. But-- but your board is seventy percent female. Your leadership team is fifty percent women. How have you achieved this in the tech industry?\par \par \uc2 WHITNEY WOLFE HERD: There are incredible women out there. And we have really, really tried to empower women, not only from our product standpoint but internally as well. And it is really important to walk the walk. And so we`ve really prioritized gender diversity in our business.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You also mentioned that you became a mom fourteen months ago, Bo`s (ph) mom. That`s your-- your-- your most important title. But you also said it`s been one of the cha-- one of the most challenging times in your life too. You talked about postpartum. Can you share how you`re feeling about that, how you navigated that?\par \par \uc2 WHITNEY WOLFE HERD: Well, mothers are remarkable. I have so much respect for mothers. And going through what everyone has gone through in this pandemic, mothers have really had a big burden imposed on them through this. And it`s been devastating to see what`s happened to women in the workforce. And, you know, I-- I`m very fortunate to have a healthy child. But postpartum is hard. And navigating postpartum while running a business and-- and going into a pandemic was a lot. But most importantly, what I`ve- - what I`ve learned coming out the other end of this is just how strong and remarkable women are. And I`m-- I`m honored to-- to be in this seat today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s your favorite color, Whitney Wolfe Herd?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Same as Gayle`s.\par \par \uc2 WHITNEY WOLFE HERD: Well, Gayle, take a guess. I think every time I`ve come on your show I`ve been in yellow. But you know what? I think I know your favorite color too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah, I know.\par \par \uc2 WHITNEY WOLFE HERD: It is also yellow, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. It`s sunshine and happiness and butter, all good things to me. So congrats to you today.\par \par \uc2 WHITNEY WOLFE HERD: And Bumble.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Bumble.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bumble.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re right.\par \par \uc2 WHITNEY WOLFE HERD: Thank you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. Whitney Wolfe Herd on the precipice of being the youngest woman to run a publicly traded company and by the end of day might be a billionaire. Let that sink in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We appreciate you being on with us this morning.\par \par \uc2 All right. Ahead, our A More Perfect Union series meets a former restaurant industry worker who reinvented himself as an online trivia host. How he`s bringing people together from all around the world. This is interesting.\par \par \uc2 But first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Our series, A More Perfect Union, is a reminder that what unites us as Americans is far greater than what divides us. Now the economic downturn during the pandemic has cost almost a quarter of all hospitality workers their jobs. That is far worse than any other industry. But one jobless bar and restaurant consultant has landed on his feet by remaking pub trivia for pandemic times. And as Adriana Diaz show us-- shows us, his success is keeping people both connected and doing good at the same time.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: American rap--\par \par \uc2 LOREN KEATING: Good morning, everyone.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ (CBS News Correspondent): Life online can put us all in a box- -\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Welcome to your date.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: --from first dates to court dates.\par \par \uc2 TODD J. HEPLER: Probable cause--\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But like a lot of us, Stephen Walsh hadn`t even heard of Zoom until last March--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON (March 24, 2020): The restaurant industry is reeling.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: --when COVID crashed his Baltimore event business.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: My inbox was full of cancelations. I said to my wife, I was like, we`re-- we`re in trouble here.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Wow. You must have panicked.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: When you think you have a business and it`s been going pretty well, and all of a sudden in a-- in a few days it`s gone.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But the Ireland native`s luck turned around, when else--St. Patrick`s Day, when he took an old hobby and brought it online hosting trivia on Zoom. A thousand games later, he`s reached players in eighty- seven countries.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: It`s definitely the most I`ve earned in my life. It`s turned into a career that I don`t plan on moving back from.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Right.\par \par \uc2 Regulars often play with friends in other places.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: She wrote her most famous book, a classic of its genre, at age twenty.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But his questions that make you think may be the biggest draw.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: That was Mary Shelley.\par \par \uc2 LAKEITHA SMITH: Walsh Trivia, it`s-- it`s hard.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: So hard, Walsh attracts Jeopardy! contestants and even other trivia hosts like Lakeitha Smith from North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 LAKEITHA SMITH: A trivia game is only as good as its host. Stephen does a really great job of keeping the audience engaged, which as a trivia host, I can say it`s difficult to do in person, but let alone over Zoom.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: After so much talk of trivia, I had to try it myself.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: What is the country that is home to the most people with a name starting and ending with same the same letter?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: So I`m assuming it`s a country that starts and ends with A.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Because a lot of countries end in A. Oh, Australia.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: So this is where I trick you. Everybody puts Australia.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: No.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: Argentina.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Ah.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: Argentina.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Walsh crafts questions he calls \uc2 Google\uc2 -proof.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: One team just high fived and kissed.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But instead of competing for cash, weekly champions pick a charity and pay it forward. Because of this, so far Walsh has raised more than a hundred thousand dollars, holding whole games to benefit autism and Alzheimer`s research, Baltimore`s Ready Initiative for low income college students and Philadelphia`s Youth Sentencing and Reentry Project, where after thirty-one years in prison, John Pace is now helping others with their second chance.\par \par \uc2 JOHN PACE (Youth Sentencing and Reentry Project Reentry Coordinator): I think they begin to trust me that I was able to go through that experience. I made poor decisions, I was able to transition out of that. And I`m making better decisions now.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: How do you feel having started this as a way to just get by for your family, and now you`re able to help others?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: It-- it`s been really, really fantastic on that end, like, people are so appreciative.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And people come back not just for the questions but for the co-hosts.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: Correct answer. Do you know?\par \par \uc2 JAMIESON (ph): Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Two-year-old Jamieson--\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: Number one.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: --and now his baby sister, Katarina (ph), born just three weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: My wife, she was like, will Stephen host the game from the hospital room? Check in (INDISTINCT) and find out.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Turns out the hospital Wi-Fi wasn`t strong enough.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN WALSH: She`s giving you the evil eye back.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But Walsh also had his hands full with his greatest win yet.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING, I`m Adriana Diaz.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody looks like they`re having a great time.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I mean, what a great turnaround from being in trouble in this pandemic and then starting this. And who knew you could make a business out of that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Maybe we need trivia on the back end of our-- our show. Why do people love trivia so much? I mean, I get it. I love it also.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I love that it`s Goo-- he`s made it \uc2 Google\uc2 -proof. That`s a-- that must mean it`s--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: --really, really good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought Adriana was very brave to do a question on camera because--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. Well, she could edit it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --please, let me know the answer. Please.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: It wasn`t live.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was not live.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You are watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. Thank you for that. We`ll be right back. Taking a break.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I`m still thinking about how you can use Bumble for non- dating purposes and what you might tell your girlfriend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can`t. And neither can you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Oh, honey, I turned off the dating part, does that--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: --would that work?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I turned it off. You can also turn it on then, right? The app is on your phone.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a temptation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The door is there. If you walk through it, you`re in trouble.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think Bumble`s a great idea. I`m so proud and so happy--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --for Whitney Wolfe Herd. She`s always such a lady, such a class act. Because I`ve asked her before, isn`t there a part of you that`s like, yeah, look at me now?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When she went through the bad time, she goes, no, I-- I never think about that. I just think about doing a good job-- doing a good job--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --and moving forward. She`s very cautious today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Her life--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She may think-- she may think that after the-- after the stock opens today and look at her bank account.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: She may go--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Her life is going to change so much.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. Sure is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Going to be--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Doing good things out there.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING at the table.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thanks for--\par \par \uc2 (Cross-talking)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t look for me on Bumble.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take it easy. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 cMMXXI CBS Broadcasting Inc. All Rights Reserved.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210213eh2b0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Inequality In The Workplace And Hear From Two People About The Challenges They Say They Have Faced Because Of Racial Bias\b0\par\par\uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL, GAYLE KING \par \uc2 3,008 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 February 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As a part of our Black History Month coverage, \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING is taking a look at the challenges Black Americans face when trying to find a job or climb the corporate ladder. Research from 2019 found Black Americans only made up 3.2 percent of senior leadership roles at large companies. And according to a new LinkedIn survey, eighty-one percent of Black professionals say seeing other Black people in positions of leadership would make their workplace feel more inclusive. We spoke with Zachary, a consultant, and Shinique, a marriage and family therapist, about what they say their experiences have been with racial inequity at work.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHINIQUE CHARLI WRIGHT (WrightNOW counseling): It seems like it`s a little bit difficult for people to either remember my name or pronounce it correctly or remember to pronounce it correctly. And so they would often ask for, well, what`s your middle name? What`s your-- you know, do you have a nickname? Which definitely makes me feel ashamed of my name at times because it seems like I`m not good enough or I`m stereotyped.\par \par \uc2 ZACHARY NUNN (Living Corporate Founder): As a young Black man in corporate America, folks have said all types of things about me. I`ve been told I`m too arrogant, too aggressive, sometimes not aggressive enough; too proactive, not proactive enough.\par \par \uc2 SHINIQUE CHARLI WRIGHT: Looking for a job in the corporate world, I had two resumes, both resumes were the same except one resume had Shinique, the other resume had Charli. If I put about ten resumes out, about seven came back for Charli. Seven phone calls to offer a job or offer an interview came back for Charli, and about two came back for Shinique.\par \par \uc2 ZACHARY NUNN: I was gunning for a promotion, and I was doing my thing, now look, I`m killing it, right. I`m actually doing all types of work, working in and outside of my business unit. I am doing above and beyond. I find out I got a White counterpart, he quits close to the top of the year, and then he comes back right before the promotion decisions are determined. And that guy got promoted with me. So equal outcomes, but effort was not equal. What I also realized is I`m not the only person out there experiencing this. I created Living Corporate, a digital media network that centers and amplifies Black and Brown folks at work. Gestures are cool, days off are cool, but the reality is if you`re not letting Black and Brown folks make actual decisions that can impact systemic change, you`re not doing anything.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hm.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: LinkedIn career expert Andrew McCaskill joins us now with more on race and inequity in the workplace. It`s all part of our partnership with LinkedIn. Andrew, good morning to you. I want to get to the resume issue because it`s really fascinating and disturbing. But I want to start with your reporting. So you also spoke with Black professionals about what they face in the workplace. What did you learn, and what needs to change?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCASKILL (LinkedIn Career Expert): A lot needs to change. What we learned was that Black professionals overwhelmingly saw that-- responded that things that were barriers to their advancement were not seeing people who look like them at top and senior leadership, not having access to mentorship and sponsorship, not feeling a sense of belonging in the workplace. And finally just feeling like that there was no path for them for advancement. All of those things culminate to-- to Black professionals feeling like that there`s a more opportunity outside of the workforce and working for themselves than working for companies and corporations.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So let`s talk about this resume issue. We heard Shinique say that when she sends out her resume with the name Charli on top it, Charli Wright, she gets seven callbacks. If she sends out the same resume-- resume with Shinique right on it, she only gets two. And that actually matches up with your findings and with other people`s research. What the heck is going there?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCASKILL: What happens is-- is oftentimes unconscious bias is-- is in place. Sometimes just actual bias is in place. But people with equal qualifications and skills don`t always get equal outcomes. And the unfortunate fact is that a lot of professionals are starting to say-- feel like that they have to whiten their resume or remove racial descriptors from their resumes. I went to a historically Black college. I went to Morehouse College. I can`t imagine having to-- thinking I have to take that off my resume. But the onus is on the corporations to put things in place to make sure that racial discrimination for-- in their hiring process doesn`t creep in. And you have to be intentional about it. It doesn`t just happen organically.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. So let`s-- let`s talk about the intentions because I love Zachary and I love Shinique. I thought they raised really great points. So what should employers do to put that in place and be intentional? Give us two top things they could do today.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCASKILL: Today is they can do blind recruitment where they look in at the-- in the hiring process and start to just only look at qualifications and skills. The other thing that they can do is that they have to be-- hold themselves accountable. They need to start to measure and think about the number of people from underrepresented groups who are applying for roles and opportunities, and then juxtapose that to the number of folks who actually get callbacks and get hired in the end. This is not the responsibility of the job seeker. It requires extra effort for inclusion, and that has to be something that everybody at the corporation is willing to-- to go along with. It requires extra effort.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, they also talk about fear of retaliation, Black people in the workplace, when they speak up about it. So, you know, you`re looked as complaining, or causing troubling. What other obstacles do they face and how to navigate that?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCASKILL: There-- there are so many other obstacles that they face. But I think, Gayle, that one in four Black respondents said that they fear retaliation. And so that means that we have to have allies in this process, is that if you are a leader, you should be talking about inclusion because that sets the tone for culture. People are not leaving-- they`re just not leaving their jobs, they`re leaving toxic cultures, and they`re leaving the-- the-- they`re not wanting to have to deal with things like microaggressions at work or feeling like there`s hopelessness at work. So one thing that people can do is if you`re a leader, talk about this. Have the hard conversations. It shouldn`t just be Black people and people of color or people who are other talking about inequities at work. Because one in four Black folks said that they felt like that they would receive retaliation when they talk about inequities.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCASKILL: So we need our non-Black counterparts to jump in and talk about it, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. May be a hard conversation, but it`s a necessary conversation. Drew McCaskill, good to see you. Thank you so much for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, a new apology from country star Morgan Wallen after he was recorded using a racial slur. Why he`s telling his fans not-- not to defend him.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN WALLEN (Country Singer): I let so many people down and who mean a lot to me and who have given so much to me, and it`s just not fair. I`ve got many more things to learn. But I already know that I don`t want to add to any division. This week was a big lesson that sometimes we can do just that without even knowing it. Our actions matter. Our words matter. And I just want to encourage anyone watching to please learn from my mistake. There`s no reason to downplay what I did.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That was country singer Morgan Wallen apologizing and asking fans not to defend his use of a racial slur caught on camera last week. In an \uc2 Instagram\uc2  video posted last night, the country star says he used the language at the end of a seventy-two hour bender. He says he`s now been sober for nine days, and that he`s accepted invitations to be educated by Black leaders since the TMZ video surfaced of him using a racial slur. Wallen`s music has been pulled from radio stations, and he`s been suspended by his record label. But we should note his actual sales soared after this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: His record sales are going up. And I think that he wants people to know he doesn`t want to be rewarded for bad behavior.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s why he`s saying, okay, you know, you-- you may-- buying the music-- he doesn`t want people to think that`s an endorsement of his--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --of his bad behavior.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: There`s been obviously a lot of conversation about this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: And there were some people saying you should forgive him and other people saying, wait, it`s too soon. And he-- he`s sort of been saying the same thing here, it`s too soon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s too soon.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I, you know, I need-- I`m in the learning process.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why are people rushing out to buy the records? That`s-- that`s sort of a disturbing--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because they`re trying to show that they support him. And, you know, not for nothing, his music is good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I-- I like Morgan Wallen`s music. And I think he wants to-- but he does want to send the message that this is not okay, what I did was not okay.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. And I think that`s important at this point.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. I`m glad he`s taking that tactic. And he-- he admits he`s got a lot of learning and growing up to do.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we will meet the woman who is set to become the youngest female CEO of a public company today. Wait until you hear her age. We`re going to talk with Bumble founder--this is Whitney Wolfe Herd--about the dating site and why she says the last year has been, well, the most challenging of her life for many reasons.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (8:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING. It`s time to bring you some of the stories that we call Talk of the Table. And, Anthony, you`re up first.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah. Remember that new \uc2 Jeep\uc2  commercial we saw in the Super Bowl featuring Bruce Springsteen?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I loved that ad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Loved it.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Well, it is off the air after it was revealed he was recently arrested for alleged drunken driving. The \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  confirms Springsteen was arrested November 14th inside the Gateway National Recreation Area in New Jersey. He was issued citations for driving under the influence, reckless driving, and consuming alcohol in a closed area. A park spokes-- spokesperson said Springsteen was very cooperative. That \uc2 Jeep\uc2  ad with Springsteen aired first the first time, of course, during the Super Bowl. It got a very strong response from viewers. Springsteen is due to appear before a judge via-- via video conference later this month. But there`s been no comment from-- from the Springsteen camp. But the \uc2 Asbury Park Press\uc2  has reported that Springsteen`s blood alcohol content was .02, which is a quarter of the--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And this is not like--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: --that`s like a like a quarter of the legal limit in-- in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: So there`s a lot of questions about why he was actually arrested here. He has no prior DWIs, traffic, or parking tickets on record. So there-- there are a lot of questions. If his blood alcohol level was low, that`s like one drink.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah. And he`s also, as we know, not an irresponsible person.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But when you hear arrested for, you know, driving under the influence, your mind goes all the way to Z--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --and then you hear the details and you say--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yep.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --wait.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But then it says reckless driving, but he`s in this--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --national park.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah, yeah. So there are a lot of questions there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot of questions.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: But the-- the upshot is for the moment, that \uc2 Jeep\uc2  ad is off the air.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. We are, what, almost a year now into the pandemic, into working from home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And apparently we have not figured out the Zoom things, whatever they call them. Because there`s another video conference glitch, and it has gone viral. So look what happened when Minnesota Congressman Tim Emmer spoke to a House Committee yesterday.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: We must make sure that our nation`s sole proprietors and the smallest of small businesses receive timely--\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXINE WATERS: Will the gentleman suspend? I`m sorry. Mister Emmer?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXINE WATERS: Are you okay?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: I am.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You`re upside down, Tom.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: I-- I don`t know how to fix that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "I don`t know how to fix that." So that`s Maxine Waters who is chair of the House Financial Services Committee. And that is not the Wizard of Oz. That is a representative from the fine state of Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: That`s how I feel almost every day now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If the-- the tape played just a second longer, one of his colleagues yelled out, at least you`re not a cat.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great. Love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think we have to have young people in the room, because every-- all the people of a certain age, myself included, go, I don`t know how to fix that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the congressman said, I turned it on and I turned it off, but it`s still the same.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know how that works. So when you`re on it, do you not know you`re upside down?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I don`t know-- I-- I think you should be able to see that. I mean, I see my own (INDISTINCT) on Zoom.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was funny.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a wink--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He`s-- he`s okay. He`s okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah. He`s fine.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have a follow-up to the story we told you about yesterday, remember that Indiana woman who had a wonderful reaction--I love this--when dozens of runaway cows ran past her car on the highway? So here`s a clip from yesterday and our discussion.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA KUCZMANSKI (Yesterday; internet video): Oh, my gosh. No, this is fabulous.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING (Yesterday): This is one of those ones where you wish you could see their face.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because she goes, this is fabulous.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to know what she looks like. That was nice.\par \par \uc2 Be careful what you ask for because guess what we received yesterday, a video yesterday. And here`s a face behind that voice.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA KUCZMANSKI: Everybody`s calling me now because I`m the cow lady. And now after your rendition of my voice this morning, I`m Fran Drescher. So anyway, thank you for such a great clip. It was fabulous.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She got--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --she got a kick-- her name is Melissa Kuczmanski. I hope I`m saying that right, Melissa. Because it just shows you, because in my mind I pictured her being kind of grandma-like--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --or Aunt Clara-like or Aunt Esther-like.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Nope.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t-- but she`s young, she`s fun. She`s got a great sense of humor.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, does she work the-- by the hour for us? Because what I loved is she putting out into the universe--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: --a desire to see wildlife.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Then the cows came.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So apparently her thoughts are very powerful.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like Melissa Kuczmanski. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (Cross-talking)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you for watching. I`m glad she saw it.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210213eh2b000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Chicago Is Temporarily Delaying Part Of Its Return To In-Person Learning After The Teachers Union Threatened To Strike, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King \par \uc2 1,198 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 February 2021\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2021 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Chicago is temporarily delaying part of its return to in-person learning after the teachers union there threatened to strike over safety concerns during this pandemic. It`s all part of a national debate about reopening schools in which the teachers are often caught between their love for the class and fears about their own health and the health of their own students. Meg Oliver has a story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PEGGY WAGNER (Chicago Teacher): We have to count how many eggs.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Peggy Wagner is in her seventeenth year teaching special ed students in a low-income neighborhood of Chicago. It`s her passion.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY WAGNER: My students are my breath.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The veteran teachers worried about her student`s well-being and plans to return to school in-person as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 Why do you want to go back to in-person teaching?\par \par \uc2 PEGGY WAGNER: It`s the classroom. You know, you-- the remote teaching is-- was interesting, challenging and necessary. So, I mastered it, right? But I`m in my dining room. That`s not my home turf.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Chicago Mayor \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  says the city has spent roughly one hundred million dollars on ventilation, masks and safety protocols. But last week, more than seventy percent of union members voted against returning to the classroom, including preschool teacher Vanessa Saucedo.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA SAUCEDO (Chicago Teacher): We had a significant number of families that were affected by death from COVID as well as just entire households that got sick.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Corey DeAngelis with \uc2 the Reason Foundation\uc2 , a libertarian think tank, has been studying the link between unions and reopening schools. He looked at more than eight hundred school districts and found the stronger the teachers union, the less likely a school will reopen in person. And COVID-19 risks were not always the only part of the equation.\par \par \uc2 COREY DEANGELIS (\uc2 The Reason Foundation\uc2  School Choice Director): It`s not because they are bad people. There`s a lot of good people in the teachers union and in the public school system. It`s because of the incentives. They get to stay home and retain the same amount of benefits and pay and job security.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Demands from teachers unions have become big sticking points in negotiations. In Chicago, the union has raised concerns about remote work requests being denied. And teachers in Los Angeles say being vaccinated isn`t safe enough. They want to see COVID rates drop before they return to the classroom. The president of the country`s largest teachers union, Becky Pringle, says safety is their top priority.\par \par \uc2 But do you believe teachers unions are becoming scapegoats in this debate to reopen schools?\par \par \uc2 BECKY PRINGLE (National Education Association President): People are frightened. They are scared. When that happens, when people come from a place of fear, they started to blame other people.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: With this national debate causing a clear divide in the education community, teachers like Wagner are saying it`s the students who are suffering.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY WAGNER: Both organizations need to come to some kind of settlement that involves safety for children.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: For \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING, Meg Oliver.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: I-- I mean, well, I`ve said this before--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: --my wife is a teacher--\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: --and I-- I hear about this all day, every day. The teachers are really worried. It`s that simple.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mm-Hm.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: They want to be safe. They want the kids to be safe. You know, the unions are strong and they are-- they are trying to work for the teachers but I promise you every teacher wants to be back in school.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I-- I was going to say that. All the teachers want to get--\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: They all want to be back at school.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They don`t want to be in their dining room--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: --their kitchen teaching--\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY MASON: No. And they are in back in this littlest room in the house, where my wife is every day. But-- but they want to be safe because very often they have people at home who are all vulnerable, and they don`t want to bring it back to them.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well said.\par \par \uc2 All right. Moving on, in Moscow this morning, opposition leader \uc2 Alexei Navalny\uc2  is back in court. His arrest two weeks ago led to two weekends of massive protests all over Russia, and now the court is deciding whether to keep him in prison for an alleged parole violation. Holly-- Holly Williams reports now on the case and the next step for opponents of President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The Russian government`s fiercest critic could soon be silenced. \uc2 Alexei Navalny\uc2  supporters were rounded up this morning by police outside the courthouse. Vladimir Ashurkov is one of Navalny`s allies and told us the Russian political opposition is growing, showing the cracks in the government`s rule.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR ASHURKOV (\uc2 Anti-Corruption Foundation\uc2 ): They feel that they are weak. I-- I think they feel that they are vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Navalny was poison with a deadly nerve agent Novichok in August last year, an apparent assassination attempt. He survived after being evacuated to Germany. A recent investigation linked the poisoning to Russian intelligence agents. Navalny was detained by police when he returned to Russia last month, sparking mass protests in freezing weather in scores of cities across the country. Thousands of protesters were taken into custody by the police. But none of that stopped Navalny from releasing this video accusing Russia`s President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  of corruption receiving this opulence seaside mansion as a bribe. It`s been watched more than a hundred million times. Putin denies the palatial home is his.\par \par \uc2 Do you think the protests will continue?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR ASHURKOV: If Navalny is locked up, the anger will only multiply.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Even behind bars, \uc2 Alexei Navalny\uc2  could be a powerful symbol of dissent in Vladimir Putin`s Russia.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING, Holly Williams, London.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. Ahead, Tom Brady and Rob Gronkowski hope to add another Super Bowl ring to their collection. This time with the Buccaneers. We`ll take a look at their decade-long friendship on and off the field.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to the \uc2 CBS\uc2  THIS MORNING podcast. You`ll get the day`s top stories in less than twenty minutes.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXI CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020210203eh220005l\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\cl